window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["mphil-cp-2023"] = {"id": "mphil-cp-2023", "title": "M.Phil CP Entrance Test 2023 (college not printed)", "fullTitle": "Entrance Test 2023, M.Phil Clinical Psychology (real paper; filed as KU 2023, the university is not printed on it)", "sub": "70 real questions · marking scheme not printed · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology and Genetics", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "When conducting research on attribution theories, which type of attribution refers to the tendency to explain one's successes as a result of internal, stable factors, and failures as a result of external, situational factors?", "options": {"a": "Dispositional attribution", "b": "Self-serving attribution", "c": "Fundamental attribution error", "d": "Covariation model"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the self-serving attributional bias is the tendency to credit our successes to internal, stable causes and to blame our failures on external, situational ones. Attribution theory asks how ordinary people explain behaviour. Fritz Heider (1958) first split causes into internal (dispositional: ability, effort, character) and external (situational: luck, task difficulty, other people). Later research showed that our explanations are tilted, and this particular tilt depends on whether the outcome was good or bad. Dale Miller and Michael Ross (1975) reviewed the early studies and found the success half (taking credit) better supported than the failure half; a later meta-analysis by Amy Mezulis and colleagues (2004) confirmed that the bias is large and widespread, though smaller in many Asian cultures. The usual explanation is protection of self-esteem, helped by a wish to look good in front of others (self-presentation). For example, when Arjun tops his statistics test he says, 'I am good with numbers'; when he fails the next one he says, 'The paper was unfair and the invigilator kept disturbing us.' Option b is right because only the self-serving bias switches the direction of the explanation with the outcome: internal and stable for success, external and situational for failure. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error also contrasts internal and external causes, but it concerns how we explain other people's behaviour (overrating their dispositions), not how we explain our own successes and failures.", "topic": "Self-serving attributional bias", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which social psychological theory suggests that individuals perceive others in terms of their positive or negative traits, leading to a \"halo\" effect?", "options": {"a": "Implicit personality theory", "b": "Self-perception theory", "c": "Trait-ascription bias", "d": "Primacy effect"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: implicit personality theory is the set of unspoken beliefs each of us holds about which traits go together, and it is what produces the halo effect. Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri (1954) gave the idea its name. When we meet someone we know only a few of their traits, so we fill in the rest from our private 'theory' of personality: warm people must also be generous, clever people must also be hard-working, attractive people must also be kind. The halo effect is the best-known product of this filling-in. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after finding that army officers who rated a soldier high on physique also rated him high on intelligence, loyalty and leadership; one good impression spread to unrelated traits. It also works in reverse, when one bad trait darkens everything (the 'horns' effect). For example, Kavya's new colleague Rohan is cheerful and well dressed, so she assumes he is also competent and honest, though she has no evidence on either. Halo effects bias interviews, teachers' grading and performance appraisals, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. Option a is right because only implicit personality theory explains why a single positive or negative trait colours our judgements of all the others. Exam trap: the primacy effect (Solomon Asch, 1946) also shapes first impressions, but it is about the order of information (early traits weigh most), not about one trait spreading to the rest.", "topic": "Implicit personality theory and halo", "fig": "Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri"}, {"q": "The Asch conformity experiment demonstrated that individuals are more likely to conform to group pressure when:", "options": {"a": "The group is unanimous in their judgments", "b": "The task is easy and straightforward", "c": "The participants have high self-esteem", "d": "The group is smaller in size"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: in Solomon Asch's (1951, 1956) experiments people conformed most when the majority was unanimous. Asch seated one real participant among about seven confederates and asked each person, aloud, which of three comparison lines matched a standard line. The answer was obvious: tested alone, people erred on under 1 percent of judgements. But on 12 of the 18 trials (the critical ones) every confederate gave the same wrong answer. Participants went along on about 37 percent of critical trials, and about three quarters conformed at least once. In interviews, most yielders said they did not want to stand out (normative influence) or came to doubt their own judgement (informational influence). Asch then varied the set-up. When just one confederate broke ranks and gave the correct answer (a dissenting ally), conformity fell to roughly a quarter of its usual level; even a dissenter who gave a different wrong answer helped. Unanimity, not sheer numbers, was what mattered. For example, if Neha's whole study group insists that a wrong option is correct, she may go along; if one friend, Kabir, quietly agrees with her, she will usually stick to her own answer. Option a is right because breaking the majority's unanimity was the single strongest way to reduce conformity in Asch's work. Exam trap: group size does matter, but only up to about three or four confederates; beyond that conformity levels off, so a smaller group gives less conformity, not more.", "topic": "Asch conformity: unanimity", "fig": "Solomon Asch"}, {"q": "According to the Realistic Conflict Theory, intergroup conflicts are more likely to occur when:", "options": {"a": "There is cooperation and interdependence between groups", "b": "Groups have shared goals and superordinate identities", "c": "There is competition for limited resources", "d": "Individuals are exposed to positive outgroup exemplars"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: realistic conflict theory holds that intergroup hostility arises when groups compete for limited resources that only one side can have. The logic is simple: if my group's gain is your group's loss (land, jobs, prizes, water), the groups come to see each other as enemies, and prejudice, negative stereotypes and aggression follow. Donald Campbell (1965) gave it the name realistic group conflict theory, but the classic evidence is the Robbers Cave experiment of Muzafer Sherif (1954; book 1961). Sherif took 22 boys aged about 11 to 12 to a summer camp in Oklahoma in two separate groups, who named themselves the Eagles and the Rattlers. A tournament with prizes only for the winners soon produced name-calling, a burned flag and raids on each other's cabins. Mere contact, such as shared meals and films, did not help; meals became food fights. What worked were superordinate goals, tasks neither group could achieve alone, such as repairing the camp's water supply and pulling a stalled supply truck. For example, if Arjun's and Imran's housing colonies must share one water tanker, quarrels and stereotypes grow; when both must work together to get a new pipeline approved, the hostility falls. Option c is right because competition for scarce resources is exactly the cause the theory names. Exam trap: shared and superordinate goals appear in the same study, but as the cure for the conflict, not its cause.", "topic": "Realistic conflict theory", "fig": "Muzafer Sherif"}, {"q": "According to the Elaboration Likelihood Model (ELM), which route of persuasion occurs when individuals are motivated and able to process a persuasive message carefully?", "options": {"a": "Peripheral route", "b": "Central route", "c": "Heuristic route", "d": "Dual-process route"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: in the elaboration likelihood model the central route is taken when people are both motivated and able to think carefully about a message. Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) argued that persuasion depends on elaboration, the amount of careful thought a person gives to the arguments. When motivation is high (the issue matters personally, or the person enjoys thinking, called high need for cognition) and ability is high (enough time and knowledge, no distraction), people take the central route: they weigh the quality of the arguments, so strong arguments persuade and weak ones backfire. Attitudes formed this way last longer, resist counter-arguments and predict behaviour. When either motivation or ability is low, people take the peripheral route: they respond to cues such as an attractive or famous source, the sheer number of arguments or a catchy jingle, and the change is weaker and short-lived. For example, Meera, who is about to buy a laptop for her PhD work, studies battery and processor comparisons (central route), while her cousin Rohan, who is only browsing, is swayed by the cricketer in the advertisement (peripheral route). Option b is right because careful processing by a motivated and able person is the definition of the central route. Exam trap: heuristic and systematic are the two routes of Shelly Chaiken's (1980) heuristic-systematic model, a sister theory; the ELM's own terms are central and peripheral.", "topic": "ELM central route", "fig": "Richard Petty and John Cacioppo"}, {"q": "The Zimbardo Stanford Prison Experiment demonstrated the powerful influence of:", "options": {"a": "Obedience to authority figures", "b": "Groupthink in decision-making", "c": "The bystander effect in emergencies", "d": "Social roles and situational factors on behavior"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Stanford Prison Experiment showed how strongly social roles and the situation can shape behaviour. In August 1971 Philip Zimbardo, with Craig Haney and Curtis Banks, turned a basement of Stanford University's psychology building into a mock prison. Twenty-four psychologically healthy male students were randomly assigned to be guards or prisoners. Prisoners were 'arrested' at home, stripped of their names and known only by numbers; guards wore uniforms and mirrored sunglasses. Within days many guards became harsh and humiliating, and the prisoners, after a brief rebellion, grew passive, distressed and helpless. The study, planned for two weeks, was stopped after six days. Because the roles were assigned at random, Zimbardo argued that the cruelty came from the role and the setting, not from the personalities of the people in them. For example, Kabir, a gentle student made a 'guard', might start shouting at 'prisoners' simply because the uniform and the rules seem to demand it. The study has been heavily criticised: there was no control group, Zimbardo himself acted as prison superintendent, and Thibault Le Texier (2019) used archive material to argue that the guards were coached to be tough. Option d is right because the lesson the study is known for is the power of roles and situational factors. Exam trap: Stanley Milgram's (1963) studies also show the power of the situation, but they concern obedience to an authority's direct commands, which is a different study.", "topic": "Stanford Prison Experiment", "fig": "Philip Zimbardo"}, {"q": "In the context of Cognitive Dissonance Theory, what term refers to the tendency of individuals to justify their efforts and investments in a decision, even if the decision turns out to be unfavorable?", "options": {"a": "Self-justification", "b": "Effort justification", "c": "Rationalization", "d": "Confirmation bias"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: effort justification is the tendency to value something more because we suffered or worked hard to get it, even when the result turns out poor. It comes from Leon Festinger's (1957) theory of cognitive dissonance: holding two clashing cognitions ('I worked very hard for this' and 'this is worthless') is uncomfortable, and because the effort cannot be undone, people change the other belief and decide the outcome was worth it. Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills (1959) tested this. Female students who wanted to join a discussion group went through no initiation, a mild one or a severe, embarrassing one (reading obscene words aloud to a male experimenter). They then listened to a deliberately dull, halting discussion. The severe-initiation group rated the discussion and its members as far more interesting. The effect helps explain loyalty after harsh ragging or hazing, and why costly courses or therapies feel more valuable. For example, Arjun spends two years and his savings on coaching for a job exam, finds the job dull, yet insists to everyone that it was the best decision of his life. Option b is right because the stem describes justifying effort and investment already spent. Exam trap: rationalisation sounds similar, but it is a psychoanalytic defence mechanism, not a dissonance term. Key note: self-justification is Elliot Aronson's broader umbrella for all dissonance-driven justifying, so a is close; but the stem's 'justify their efforts and investments' names the specific form, effort justification, so b stands.", "topic": "Effort justification", "fig": "Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills"}, {"q": "Who among the following philosophers is associated with Romanticism, which emphasizes intuition and emotion over reason and logic?", "options": {"a": "John Locke", "b": "Immanuel Kant", "c": "René Descartes", "d": "Friedrich Nietzsche"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: of the four thinkers listed, Friedrich Nietzsche (1844 to 1900) is the one linked to the Romantic stress on instinct, passion and feeling over cold reason. Romanticism was a movement in art, literature and philosophy from the late 18th to the mid 19th century. Reacting against the Enlightenment's faith in reason, science and order, it prized emotion, intuition, imagination, individuality and closeness to nature. Its usual philosophical names are Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712 to 1778), Johann Gottfried Herder (1744 to 1803) and Friedrich Schelling (1775 to 1854). Nietzsche came later but grew out of the same tradition, shaped by Arthur Schopenhauer (1788 to 1860) and his idea of a blind, striving will. In The Birth of Tragedy (1872) Nietzsche contrasted the Apollonian (order, reason, form) with the Dionysian (passion, intoxication, instinct) and argued that life and art need the Dionysian force that rationalism had pushed aside. His later ideas, the will to power and his attack on the claims of pure reason and conventional morality, carry the same spirit, and they fed the later idea that hidden drives rather than reason steer behaviour. Option d is right because Nietzsche alone among the options championed instinct and emotion against rationality. Exam trap: Immanuel Kant (1724 to 1804) influenced the Romantics, but he himself was the great defender of reason. Key note: Nietzsche is often classed as a post-Romantic who later criticised Romanticism, and the standard Romantic names (Rousseau, Herder, Schelling) are not offered; since Locke, Descartes and Kant all champion reason or experience, Nietzsche is the best of the four and d stands.", "topic": "Romanticism and Nietzsche", "fig": "Friedrich Nietzsche"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of negative reinforcement?", "options": {"a": "Giving a child a time-out for misbehaving", "b": "Taking away a teenager's phone for breaking curfew", "c": "Giving an employee extra vacation days for working overtime", "d": "Giving someone a speeding ticket for driving too fast"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: giving an employee extra vacation days for working overtime is the only option in which a consequence makes a behaviour more likely, so it is the only reinforcement offered. B. F. Skinner (1938, 1953) sorted operant consequences into four cells using two questions. Does the behaviour increase (reinforcement) or decrease (punishment)? Is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not pleasant and unpleasant. Negative reinforcement is the removal of an unpleasant (aversive) stimulus after a behaviour, which makes that behaviour more likely: you take a painkiller and the headache goes, so you reach for painkillers again; you buckle the seatbelt and the beeping stops. In option c, days off remove the demands of work, an aversive state, so overtime is strengthened by taking something unpleasant away. The other three options all aim to reduce a behaviour, so they are punishments, not reinforcements. Option c is right because it is the only option in which a behaviour (working overtime) is strengthened, and read as relief from work it is negative reinforcement. Exam trap: students often equate negative reinforcement with punishment because both sound bad; negative reinforcement always increases behaviour, while punishment decreases it. Key note: none of the four options is a clean textbook case, since extra vacation days can equally be read as a reward added, which would be positive reinforcement; but every other option is a form of punishment, so c stands as the only reinforcement option.", "topic": "Negative reinforcement versus punishment", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Who among the following psychologists is associated with object relations theory and is known for his work on early childhood development?", "options": {"a": "Alfred Adler", "b": "Carl Jung", "c": "Erik Erikson", "d": "D.W. Winnicott"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: Donald Winnicott (1896 to 1971), the English paediatrician and psychoanalyst, is a leading object relations theorist known for his work on early childhood. Object relations theory shifted psychoanalysis away from Sigmund Freud's (1905) drives towards relationships: the 'objects' are the people, above all the mother, whom the infant takes into its inner world, and these early bonds shape later personality. Its other major names are Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960), Ronald Fairbairn (1889 to 1964) and Margaret Mahler (1897 to 1985). Winnicott, who saw thousands of mothers and babies in his clinic, added several famous ideas. The good-enough mother meets the baby's needs almost completely at first, then fails in small, bearable ways, so the child learns to tolerate frustration. She provides a holding environment of physical and emotional safety. As the child learns that the mother is separate, it adopts a transitional object (1953), a blanket or soft toy that is neither fully 'me' nor 'not me' and soothes it when she is away. Good-enough care lets a spontaneous true self grow; without it the child builds a compliant false self. For example, two-year-old Anaya will not sleep without her old cotton dupatta while her mother is at work: the dupatta is her transitional object. Option d is right because Winnicott alone among the options belongs to the object relations school. Exam trap: Erik Erikson (1950) also wrote famously about childhood, but he was an ego psychologist with psychosocial stages, not an object relations theorist.", "topic": "Winnicott and object relations", "fig": "Donald Winnicott"}, {"q": "The \"Levels of Processing\" theory suggests that the depth at which information is processed during encoding influences its later retrieval. Which level of processing is associated with a shallow, superficial analysis of information?", "options": {"a": "Top-down processing", "b": "Bottom-up processing", "c": "Structural processing", "d": "Semantic processing"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: in the levels of processing theory, structural processing is the shallowest level, where only the physical appearance of a stimulus is analysed. Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) argued against thinking of memory as a set of separate stores. Instead, memory is a by-product of how deeply we process information when we first meet it. There are three broad levels. Structural (visual) processing attends to how a word looks: 'Is the word in capital letters?' Phonemic processing attends to how it sounds: 'Does it rhyme with WEIGHT?' Semantic processing attends to its meaning: 'Does it fit the sentence: He met a ___ in the street?' Craik and Endel Tulving (1975) asked such questions and then gave a surprise memory test. Words processed for meaning were remembered far better than words judged for rhyme, and those better than words judged for type case. So deeper processing leaves more durable traces, and elaborative rehearsal (linking an item to meaning) works better than maintenance rehearsal (mere repetition). For example, if Riya only notices that a new term in her notes is printed in bold, she will soon forget it; if she asks what it means and links it to a patient she has seen, she will remember it. Option c is right because structural processing is the named shallow, superficial level of the model. Exam trap: bottom-up processing sounds shallow, but top-down and bottom-up are directions of processing in perception, not depth levels in Craik and Lockhart's model.", "topic": "Levels of processing", "fig": "Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart"}, {"q": "In the context of attention, which term describes the phenomenon where the detection of a target stimulus is impaired when it appears in close temporal proximity to another stimulus?", "options": {"a": "Inattentional blindness", "b": "Attentional blink", "c": "Change blindness", "d": "Cocktail party effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the attentional blink is the failure to detect a second target that appears shortly after a first target in a rapid stream of stimuli. Jane Raymond, Kimron Shapiro and Karen Arnell (1992) named it using rapid serial visual presentation (RSVP): letters flash one after another in the same spot, about ten every second. Participants must identify a first target (T1, a white letter among black ones) and then report whether a second target (T2, a black X) appeared. If T2 comes about 200 to 500 milliseconds after T1, it is often missed, as if attention had blinked. If T2 comes immediately after T1 it is often still seen (lag-1 sparing), and after about half a second detection recovers. The usual explanation is that attention is still busy consolidating T1 into working memory, so T2 cannot be processed in time and fades. The blink shows that attention is limited in time, not only in space. For example, while watching a fast news ticker, Kabir spots his team's score and then misses the breaking headline that flashes a third of a second later. Option b is right because the stem describes impaired detection of a target that follows closely in time after another stimulus, which is the definition of the attentional blink. Exam trap: inattentional blindness and change blindness are also failures of awareness, but they concern unexpected objects and changes in a scene, not a second target close in time.", "topic": "Attentional blink", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Visual illusions are powerful tools for studying perceptual processes. The \"Müller-Lyer illusion\" and the \"Ponzo illusion\" are examples of:", "options": {"a": "Binocular depth cues", "b": "Monocular depth cues", "c": "Size constancy illusions", "d": "Geometric optical illusions"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Muller-Lyer and Ponzo illusions are classic geometric optical illusions. A geometric (geometrical-optical) illusion is a simple line drawing in which we misjudge length, size, direction or straightness, even when we know the true answer. In the Muller-Lyer illusion (Franz Muller-Lyer, 1889), two equal lines look unequal: the one ending in outward-pointing fins looks longer than the one ending in arrowheads. In the Ponzo illusion (Mario Ponzo, 1911), two equal horizontal bars are drawn between converging lines like railway tracks, and the upper bar, nearer the point where the lines meet, looks longer. Other members of the family include the Ebbinghaus circles and the Zollner, Poggendorff and Hering figures. Most viewers see these illusions, and they persist even after we measure the lines, which is why researchers use them to study how the brain interprets visual cues. Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits (1966) found that people raised among rectangular buildings are more prone to the Muller-Lyer illusion (the carpentered-world hypothesis). For example, Riya measures both Muller-Lyer lines with a ruler, finds them equal, and still sees one as longer. Option d is right because geometric optical illusions is the standard name for this class of figures. Exam trap: the Ponzo figure uses linear perspective, a monocular depth cue, so b tempts, but the figure is an illusion, not a cue. Key note: Richard Gregory (1963) explained both illusions as misapplied size constancy (inappropriate constancy scaling), so c is tempting; but that is an explanation of why they occur, not the name of their class, so d stands.", "topic": "Geometric optical illusions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Working memory is a system responsible for temporarily holding and manipulating information. Which brain area is critically involved in working memory processes?", "options": {"a": "Hippocampus", "b": "Prefrontal cortex", "c": "Amygdala", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the prefrontal cortex, especially its dorsolateral part, is critically involved in working memory. Working memory is the small, active workspace in which we hold information for a few seconds and work on it, as in mental arithmetic or following directions. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) split it into a central executive (an attentional controller), a phonological loop for sounds and words and a visuospatial sketchpad for images and locations; Baddeley added an episodic buffer in 2000. The brain evidence points to the frontal lobes. Joaquin Fuster and Garrett Alexander (1971) found neurons in the monkey prefrontal cortex that keep firing through the delay while the animal remembers where food was hidden, and stop once it responds. Monkeys with prefrontal lesions fail such delayed-response tasks, and brain imaging in humans shows dorsolateral prefrontal activity rising with working memory load, for example in n-back tasks. Damage here produces a dysexecutive syndrome: poor planning, distractibility and difficulty switching between tasks. For example, when Arjun keeps a new phone number in mind while dialling, and adjusts it when told one digit is wrong, his dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is doing the work. Option b is right because the prefrontal cortex holds and manipulates information online, which is what working memory means. Exam trap: the hippocampus is famous for memory, but it consolidates new long-term memories; after surgery removed about the front half of each hippocampus, patient H.M. could not form new long-term memories yet kept a normal digit span.", "topic": "Working memory and prefrontal cortex", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Flashbulb memories are vivid and highly detailed memories of emotionally significant events. According to Brown and Kulik's theory, what contributes to the formation of flashbulb memories?", "options": {"a": "Elaborate rehearsal and deep encoding", "b": "Emotional arousal and distinctiveness", "c": "Serial position effect and primacy effect", "d": "Semantic clustering and spreading activation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Roger Brown and James Kulik (1977) held that flashbulb memories form when an event is highly surprising and highly consequential, that is, emotionally arousing and distinctive. A flashbulb memory is a vivid, detailed and confident memory of the circumstances in which one heard shocking news: where you were, who told you, what you were doing. Brown and Kulik coined the term after asking Americans how they learned of President John Kennedy's assassination in 1963; most could describe the scene years later as if it had been photographed. They proposed a special 'Now Print!' mechanism: surprise triggers a check of how much the event matters, and if it matters, the whole scene is printed into memory. Emotional arousal is the engine; later retelling then shapes the story. Later research challenged the photograph idea. Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch (1992) had students write how they heard of the Challenger shuttle disaster the morning after; two and a half years later many accounts had changed, yet confidence stayed high. Jennifer Talarico and David Rubin (2003) found the same after the 9/11 attacks: flashbulb memories are no more consistent than everyday memories, but they feel more vivid. For example, Meera can 'see' exactly where she was when she heard about the 2008 Mumbai attacks, yet her story today may differ from what she wrote in her diary that week. Option b is right because arousal and distinctiveness (surprise plus consequence) are what Brown and Kulik named. Exam trap: rehearsal does matter in their account, but as a later shaping process, not as the trigger that forms the memory.", "topic": "Flashbulb memories", "fig": "Roger Brown and James Kulik"}, {"q": "The \"framing effect\" in decision making refers to the phenomenon where individuals' choices are influenced by the way a decision is presented. Which prospect theory concept explains why people tend to be risk-averse when options are framed in terms of gains and risk-seeking when options are framed in terms of losses?", "options": {"a": "Anchoring bias", "b": "Endowment effect", "c": "Loss aversion", "d": "Overconfidence bias"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: loss aversion is the prospect theory concept offered here that explains why framing the same choice as gains or as losses changes people's appetite for risk. Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky (1979) proposed prospect theory to describe how people actually decide under risk. Outcomes are judged as gains or losses from a reference point, not as final states of wealth, and losses loom larger than equal gains: losing ₹1,000 hurts roughly twice as much as winning ₹1,000 pleases. Tversky and Kahneman (1981) showed the framing effect with the Asian disease problem, in which 600 people face a deadly outbreak. Framed as gains ('200 people will be saved' versus a one-third chance that all 600 are saved), most people chose the sure option: risk aversion. Framed as losses ('400 people will die' versus a one-third chance that nobody dies), most chose the gamble: risk seeking. The options were identical; only the wording changed. For example, Kabir agrees to a surgery when his doctor says it has a 90 percent survival rate, while his friend refuses the same surgery when told it has a 10 percent death rate. Option c is right because loss aversion, the heavier weight of losses, is the prospect theory principle that pushes people to gamble rather than accept a sure loss. Exam trap: the endowment effect also grows out of loss aversion, but it is about overvaluing what we own, not about risk under framing. Key note: strictly, the switch in risk attitude comes from the S-shaped value function (concave for gains, convex for losses: diminishing sensitivity), and loss aversion is the separate fact that the loss limb is steeper; but loss aversion is the only prospect theory concept among the options, so c stands.", "topic": "Framing and loss aversion", "fig": "Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky"}, {"q": "Formal reasoning involves deductive reasoning based on logical rules. Which famous logical problem illustrates the challenges of formal reasoning and the tendency for people to focus on irrelevant information?", "options": {"a": "The Monty Hall problem", "b": "The Wason selection task", "c": "The syllogism task", "d": "The Tower of Hanoi problem"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Wason selection task is the famous logical problem that shows how poorly people handle formal reasoning and how they are drawn to irrelevant information. Peter Wason (1966) showed people four cards: E, K, 4 and 7. Each card has a letter on one side and a number on the other. The rule is: 'If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other.' Which cards must be turned over to test the rule? The correct answer is E and 7. E must be checked because an odd number behind it would break the rule; 7 must be checked because a vowel behind it would break the rule. K is irrelevant, and so is 4, because the rule says nothing about what must lie behind an even number. Fewer than one person in ten gets it right; most choose E and 4, or E alone. They pick the cards named in the rule (matching bias) and look for confirming cases instead of the one card that could falsify it. Performance improves sharply with familiar social rules: Richard Griggs and James Cox (1982) used 'If a person is drinking beer, they must be over 19', and Leda Cosmides (1989) argued that humans have evolved a skill for detecting cheaters. For example, Riya easily checks the beer drinker and the 16-year-old at a party, yet fails the identical logic with letters and numbers. Option b is right because the selection task is the classic demonstration of these errors. Exam trap: syllogisms also test deduction and show belief bias, but the famous problem for confirmation-seeking and irrelevant cards is Wason's.", "topic": "Wason selection task", "fig": "Peter Wason"}, {"q": "According to the \"Whorfian hypothesis\" (linguistic relativity), the language we speak influences our thoughts and perceptions of the world. Which version of the hypothesis suggests that language has a strong determinative influence on thought and perception?", "options": {"a": "Linguistic determinism", "b": "Linguistic relativism", "c": "Linguistic universals", "d": "Linguistic determinacy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: linguistic determinism is the strong version of the Whorfian hypothesis, the claim that language determines thought and perception. Edward Sapir (1884 to 1939) and his student Benjamin Lee Whorf (1897 to 1941) argued that the language we speak shapes how we see the world; Whorf's essays were collected in Language, Thought and Reality (1956). The idea comes in two strengths. The strong version, linguistic determinism, says language fixes thought: if your language has no word or grammatical form for something, you cannot think it. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says only that language influences thought, making some distinctions easier to notice, remember or talk about. The strong version has largely failed. Eleanor Rosch (then Heider, 1972) found that the Dani of New Guinea, who have only two basic colour words, still remembered focal colours much as English speakers do. The weak version has some support, such as small speed advantages in telling apart colours that one's language names separately. For example, Hindi separates chacha, mama, mausa and phupha where English has only 'uncle'. On the strong version, Kabir's English-speaking friend could not grasp these relationships; in fact he can, though he needs more words to express them, which fits relativity at most. Option a is right because determinism is the strong, determinative claim the stem describes. Exam trap: linguistic relativity, the label printed in the stem itself, is the weak version, so it cannot be the strong one.", "topic": "Sapir-Whorf linguistic determinism", "fig": "Benjamin Lee Whorf"}, {"q": "In language, pragmatics refers to the study of how context influences the interpretation of meaning. Which of the following is an example of a pragmatic inference?", "options": {"a": "Recognizing a homophone based on the surrounding words", "b": "Using verb tense to indicate the timing of an event", "c": "Identifying a metaphor based on its figurative meaning", "d": "Inferring a speaker's intended meaning from their tone of voice"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: inferring what a speaker really means from their tone of voice is a pragmatic inference. Language is studied at several levels: phonology (sounds), morphology and syntax (word forms and grammar), semantics (the literal meaning of words and sentences) and pragmatics (how people use language in context to mean more than the words say). Pragmatics covers indirect requests ('Can you pass the salt?' is a request, not a question about ability), politeness, turn-taking and sarcasm. Paul Grice (1975) explained much of this with his cooperative principle: listeners assume speakers are being informative, truthful, relevant and clear (the maxims of quantity, quality, relation and manner). When a speaker seems to break a maxim, the listener infers a hidden meaning, called a conversational implicature. Tone of voice (prosody) is one of the strongest cues, since the same words can praise or mock. For example, when Kabir drops his phone in a puddle and his friend says 'Great job' in a flat, drawn-out voice, Kabir knows he means the opposite. The literal meaning is praise; the intended meaning is teasing. Reading it needs context and knowledge of the speaker, not just a dictionary, which is why people with autism spectrum disorder or right-hemisphere damage often struggle with such inferences. Option d is right because it goes beyond the literal words to the speaker's intention, using context and tone. Exam trap: metaphor also goes beyond literal meaning, so c tempts, but c is about knowing the figurative sense of an expression, while d is about reading one speaker's intention in one situation. Key note: many linguists, following Paul Grice, treat metaphor as a pragmatic implicature too, so c is not wholly outside pragmatics; but inferring intended meaning from tone is the clearer, textbook pragmatic inference, so d stands.", "topic": "Pragmatics and implicature", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Kurt Lewin's field theory emphasizes the dynamic interplay between an individual and their environment. What term refers to the psychological space around a person, including their thoughts, feelings, and experiences?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive domain", "b": "Social domain", "c": "Emotional field", "d": "Life space"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: in Kurt Lewin's (1936) field theory the life space is the whole psychological world of a person at a given moment, the person plus the environment as he or she perceives it. Lewin (1890 to 1947), a Gestalt-trained psychologist who fled Nazi Germany, borrowed the idea of a field from physics: behaviour is caused by all the forces acting on a person at that moment. His formula B = f(P, E) says behaviour is a function of the person and the environment, and the two together make up the life space. The environment here is psychological, not physical: what matters is how things appear to the person, including his or her needs, goals, fears and memories. In Principles of Topological Psychology (1936) Lewin mapped the life space as regions (activities and goals) separated by boundaries; regions carry positive or negative valences that attract or repel, and vectors (forces) push the person along paths. The framework let him describe conflicts such as approach-approach and approach-avoidance, and it led to his later work on group dynamics. For example, Arjun's life space on the evening before an exam includes his textbook (a goal region), a cricket match on TV (an attractive region with positive valence), his fear of failing (a negative valence) and a power cut (a barrier). Option d is right because life space is Lewin's own term for the psychological space around a person. Exam trap: the word field appears in field theory, so emotional field sounds Lewinian, but it is not one of his terms.", "topic": "Lewin's life space", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "H.J. Eysenck's personality theory suggests that personality can be described along three major dimensions: extraversion-introversion, neuroticism-stability, and psychoticism. What is the term used to describe the underlying biological basis for these personality traits?", "options": {"a": "Archetypes", "b": "Temperaments", "c": "Traits", "d": "Biogenic factors"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Hans Eysenck (1916 to 1997) treated his three dimensions as temperaments, broad, biologically based styles of behaviour that are largely inherited. Eysenck used factor analysis to find a few superfactors, or types, at the top of a hierarchy that runs from specific responses to habitual responses to traits to types. He then asked why these dimensions exist, and in The Biological Basis of Personality (1967) he answered with biology. Extraversion depends on the ascending reticular activating system, which sets the level of cortical arousal: introverts have a higher baseline arousal and avoid extra stimulation, while extraverts are under-aroused and seek it. Neuroticism depends on the reactivity of the limbic system, the visceral brain, which governs emotional responses. Psychoticism, developed with Sybil Eysenck (1976), was tentatively linked to male sex hormones. Twin studies supported a sizeable genetic share for all three. Eysenck tied his scheme to the four temperaments of Galen (2nd century CE): melancholic (unstable introvert), choleric (unstable extravert), sanguine (stable extravert) and phlegmatic (stable introvert). For example, Riya, a calm, quiet student who prefers the library to a noisy cricket stadium, falls in the phlegmatic quadrant, because on Eysenck's account her cortex is already well aroused. Option b is right because temperament is the term for the inherited, biological core that Eysenck's dimensions describe. Exam trap: traits sounds right because Eysenck is a trait theorist, but traits are a lower, descriptive level of his hierarchy, not the biological basis.", "topic": "Eysenck's biological temperaments", "fig": "Hans Eysenck"}, {"q": "George Kelly's personal construct theory proposes that individuals create mental frameworks, known as personal constructs, to interpret and understand the world around them. What term describes the process by which individuals adjust their constructs when faced with new experiences that do not fit existing categories?", "options": {"a": "Social comparison", "b": "Cognitive dissonance", "c": "Accommodation", "d": "Assimilation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: accommodation is the term offered for adjusting one's constructs when new experiences do not fit the old categories. George Kelly (1955) proposed in The Psychology of Personal Constructs that every person acts like a scientist: we build personal constructs, bipolar dimensions such as friendly versus hostile or honest versus dishonest, use them to predict events, and revise them when predictions fail. His experience corollary states that a person's construction system changes as he or she construes new events, and his modulation corollary adds that how far it can change depends on how permeable the constructs are (open to new elements) rather than impermeable (rigid whatever the evidence, as in prejudice). Kelly called his stance constructive alternativism: we can always re-construe our lives. The word accommodation comes from Jean Piaget (1936), who used it for changing a schema to fit new experience, in contrast to assimilation, fitting new experience into an existing schema. For example, Arjun construes all professors as strict versus lenient; when he meets a professor who is strict about deadlines yet warm and flexible about ideas, he splits the construct into two separate ones. That revision is accommodation. Option c is right because it is the only option that names changing the framework itself to fit new experience. Exam trap: assimilation is the opposite process, keeping the construct unchanged and forcing the new experience to fit it. Key note: Kelly's own words for this are construct revision (the experience corollary) and permeability, while accommodation is Piaget's term; it is used here because it names the same adjustment, so c stands.", "topic": "Kelly: construct revision", "fig": "George Kelly"}, {"q": "Personal construct theory posits that individuals have unique systems of personal constructs that shape their perceptions of reality. What term describes the tendency to interpret ambiguous information in a way that confirms existing beliefs or expectations?", "options": {"a": "Self-fulfilling prophecy", "b": "Confirmation bias", "c": "Social desirability bias", "d": "False consensus effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, interpret and remember information in ways that confirm what we already believe or expect. Peter Wason (1960) showed it with his 2-4-6 task. Participants were told that the triple 2-4-6 fitted a rule and had to discover the rule by proposing new triples. Most guessed 'numbers rising by two' and tested only triples that fitted their guess (8-10-12, 20-22-24), never ones that could prove it wrong. The real rule was simply 'any increasing numbers'. Raymond Nickerson (1998) reviewed the bias and found it in science, medicine, courts and daily life. It works at every step: we choose sources that agree with us, read ambiguous evidence as support, and remember hits better than misses. In George Kelly's (1955) personal construct theory, constructs channel what we notice, so a person tends to see what his or her constructs predict. For example, Meera believes her new flatmate Kavya is rude; when Kavya does not reply to a message, Meera reads it as rudeness rather than a busy day, and she forgets the times Kavya helped her. Clinicians face the same trap: once a diagnosis is suspected, they may look only for symptoms that fit it. Option b is right because interpreting ambiguous information so that it supports existing beliefs is the definition of confirmation bias. Exam trap: the self-fulfilling prophecy also starts from an expectation, but there the expectation changes behaviour and makes itself come true, while confirmation bias only distorts how we read the evidence.", "topic": "Confirmation bias", "fig": "Peter Wason"}, {"q": "Raymond B. Cattell's 16 Personality Factors (16PF) questionnaire assesses a wide range of personality traits. Which of the following is NOT one of the primary personality factors measured by the 16PF questionnaire?", "options": {"a": "Extraversion", "b": "Emotional stability", "c": "Dominance", "d": "Agreeableness"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: agreeableness is not one of the 16PF's primary factors; it is a Big Five dimension. Raymond B. Cattell (1949) built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire by factor analysis. He began from the lexical hypothesis, that important traits are encoded in everyday words; Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had listed about 18,000 English trait words. Cattell reduced these to surface trait clusters and then, by factor analysis of ratings, questionnaires and objective tests, to 16 source traits. The 16 primary factors, labelled by letter, are Warmth (A), Reasoning (B), Emotional Stability (C), Dominance (E), Liveliness (F), Rule-Consciousness (G), Social Boldness (H), Sensitivity (I), Vigilance (L), Abstractedness (M), Privateness (N), Apprehension (O), Openness to Change (Q1), Self-Reliance (Q2), Perfectionism (Q3) and Tension (Q4). Factoring the primaries gives five global, second-order factors: Extraversion, Anxiety, Tough-Mindedness, Independence and Self-Control. Scores are reported in stens (1 to 10). Agreeableness is one of the Big Five dimensions, measured for example by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's NEO Personality Inventory (1985). For example, Kabir's 16PF profile might show high Warmth and high Dominance, but it contains no agreeableness score at all. Option d is right because agreeableness belongs to a different model and is not measured by the 16PF. Exam trap: Emotional Stability sounds like a Big Five label (the reverse of neuroticism), but it and Dominance are genuine 16PF primaries (C and E). Key note: extraversion is not one of the 16 primaries either, since it is a global, second-order factor the 16PF also scores; but agreeableness is not measured by the 16PF at all, so d is the best answer and stands.", "topic": "16PF primary factors", "fig": "Raymond B. Cattell"}, {"q": "Hans Eysenck proposed that personality traits are influenced by biological factors, particularly variations in brain functioning. What term refers to the stable and enduring characteristics that define an individual's personality?", "options": {"a": "Temperament", "b": "Idiosyncrasy", "c": "Heritability", "d": "Disposition"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: a disposition is a stable, enduring tendency to think, feel and act in a characteristic way, and personality is usually described as a set of such dispositions. Trait theorists use the word for the lasting building blocks of personality. Gordon Allport (1937) treated traits as real structures inside the person and sorted them into cardinal (one ruling passion), central (the five to ten words a friend would use) and secondary (narrow preferences); he later called these individual traits personal dispositions (1961). Hans Eysenck (1967), named in the stem, argued that such dispositions rest on biology: extraversion on the arousal level of the ascending reticular activating system and neuroticism on the reactivity of the limbic system. Example: Kabir is generous with classmates, with strangers on the train and with his younger cousins, year after year; that consistency across situations and time is a disposition. Option d is right because the stem asks for the general term for the stable and enduring characteristics that define a personality, and that is exactly what disposition means. Exam trap: the stem opens with biology, which pulls you towards temperament. But temperament is the narrower, inherited, early-appearing core of emotional style (emotionality, activity and sociability in Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin's 1984 model; the easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up babies of Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, 1977), on which traits are later built. Key note: because Eysenck's dimensions are biological, some would argue that temperament (a) fits. The definition asked for, however, is the broad one covering all stable characteristics of personality, not only the inherited core, so the answer used here, d, stands.", "topic": "Disposition versus temperament", "fig": "Gordon Allport"}, {"q": "The distribution of sample means approaches a normal distribution as the sample size increases, as per which theory?", "options": {"a": "Central limit theorem", "b": "Central tendency theorem", "c": "Theory of dispersion", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the central limit theorem states that as sample size grows, the distribution of sample means approaches a normal curve, whatever the shape of the population. Imagine drawing sample after sample from a population, working out each sample's mean, and plotting those means. That plot is the sampling distribution of the mean. The theorem says three things about it: its mean equals the population mean (μ); its spread, the standard error, equals the population SD divided by the square root of N; and its shape becomes normal once N is large, conventionally 30 or more. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first showed this for coin tosses, and Pierre-Simon Laplace (1810) generalised it. Example: Riya studies monthly pocket money among Pune students, which is badly skewed because a few students get very large sums. If she draws many samples of 40 students, the means of those samples still pile up in a roughly bell-shaped curve around the true mean. This is why z tests, t tests and confidence intervals can rely on the normal curve even when raw psychological scores are skewed. Option a is right because the stem restates the theorem itself: sample means become normally distributed as N increases. Exam trap: do not confuse the central limit theorem with the law of large numbers of Jacob Bernoulli (1713). The law of large numbers says a sample mean gets closer to the population mean as N grows (accuracy); the central limit theorem says the means become normally distributed (shape). Examiners also invent look-alike names from familiar words, such as 'central tendency theorem'.", "topic": "Central limit theorem", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The probability of event A occurring given that event B has occurred is better explained by:", "options": {"a": "Bayes theorem", "b": "Binomial distribution", "c": "Central limit theorem", "d": "NPC"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the probability of A given that B has occurred, written P(A|B), is a conditional probability, and Bayes' theorem is the rule for working it out. Thomas Bayes (1763), an English Presbyterian minister, set out the idea in an essay that his friend Richard Price published after his death. The theorem reads P(A|B) = P(B|A) × P(A) / P(B). In words: start with the prior probability of A (how common it is before any evidence), multiply by how likely the evidence B is if A is true, and divide by how likely B is overall. The result is the posterior probability, the updated belief once B is known. Example: depression affects 1 percent of students at Arjun's college. A screening questionnaire catches 90 percent of true cases but also flags 10 percent of healthy students. Arjun screens positive. Bayes gives P(depressed | positive) = (0.90 × 0.01) / (0.90 × 0.01 + 0.10 × 0.99), about 8 percent. A positive result is far less telling than it feels, because the base rate is low; Paul Meehl and Albert Rosen (1955) made this base-rate point for psychological tests. Option a is right because Bayes' theorem is the standard tool for finding the probability of one event given another. Exam trap: students often treat P(A|B) as the same as P(B|A). They are not: the chance of a positive test if depressed (90 percent) is very different from the chance of being depressed if the test is positive (about 8 percent). Ignoring the base rate in this way is the base-rate fallacy.", "topic": "Bayes' theorem", "fig": "Thomas Bayes"}, {"q": "What is the non-parametric alternative to the independent t-test?", "options": {"a": "ANOVA", "b": "Mann-Whitney U test", "c": "MANOVA", "d": "Runs test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t test. The t test compares the means of two independent groups and assumes interval data, roughly normal distributions and similar variances. When those assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small samples or badly skewed scores, you switch to a rank test. Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947) built the U test: pool the scores of both groups, rank them from lowest to highest, then ask whether one group's ranks run consistently higher than the other's. U counts how often a score in one group beats a score in the other. It is equivalent to the rank-sum test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). Example: Meera rates the homesickness of 12 hostel students and 10 day scholars on a 1 to 5 scale. The ratings are ordinal and skewed, so she ranks all 22 together and runs Mann-Whitney instead of t. Option b is right because each parametric test has a rank-based partner, and for two independent groups that partner is the Mann-Whitney U test. Exam trap: match the design before the test. Two related samples (paired t) take the Wilcoxon signed-rank test; three or more independent groups (one-way ANOVA) take the Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952); three or more related samples (repeated-measures ANOVA) take the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937). Students who remember only 'Wilcoxon' often pick the signed-rank test for independent groups, which is wrong.", "topic": "Non-parametric alternative to t", "fig": "Henry Mann and Donald Whitney"}, {"q": "As per Spearman, the path of science is paved with achievements of the:", "options": {"a": "allegedly unachievable", "b": "achievable", "c": "allegedly achievable", "d": "unachievable"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the line credited to Charles Spearman (1863 to 1945) says that the path of science is paved with achievements of the 'allegedly unachievable'. The point is that scientific progress usually comes from doing what experts had declared impossible. The word 'allegedly' carries the meaning: the task was only said to be unachievable, and then someone achieved it. Spearman, a British psychologist who took his doctorate in the Leipzig laboratory founded by Wilhelm Wundt (1879), is himself a good example of that spirit. In 1904, in 'General Intelligence, Objectively Determined and Measured', he showed that schoolchildren's marks in different subjects all correlate positively and explained this by a general factor, g, common to every test plus a specific factor, s, peculiar to each: the two-factor theory. In doing so he founded factor analysis and claimed to measure intelligence objectively at a time when many held that the mind could not be measured. He also gave psychology the rank-order correlation coefficient, rho (1904), and the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (1910) for estimating reliability when a test is lengthened. Option a is right because it is the only completion that makes the sentence say something: progress is built from feats that were once called impossible. Exam trap: d ('unachievable') looks close but makes the line self-contradictory, since an achievement of the truly unachievable cannot exist; the whole trick lies in the single word 'allegedly'. Key note: the exact source of this line could not be traced, so no book or year is given for the quote itself. Of the four options only 'allegedly unachievable' gives a meaningful sentence, so a stands.", "topic": "Spearman on the path of science", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "MANOVA compares means of multiple dependent variables across groups. _____ does the same while taking into consideration covariates as well.", "options": {"a": "MANCOVA", "b": "ANOVA", "c": "ANCOVA", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: MANCOVA, multivariate analysis of covariance, compares groups on two or more dependent variables at once while statistically adjusting for one or more covariates. The names are built from parts. ANOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, compares group means on one dependent variable (DV). Add 'M' for multivariate and you test several DVs together (MANOVA), which avoids the inflated Type I error of running many separate ANOVAs; its most reported statistic is Wilks' lambda. Add 'C' for covariance and you first remove the influence of a covariate, a variable related to the DV that you could not control by design, such as baseline severity, age or IQ. So: ANOVA = one DV; ANCOVA = one DV plus covariates; MANOVA = several DVs; MANCOVA = several DVs plus covariates. Example: Kabir compares CBT, mindfulness training and a waiting list on two outcomes, depression and anxiety scores after eight weeks. Because the groups started at different severity levels, he enters baseline depression as a covariate. Two DVs plus a covariate means MANCOVA. Option a is right because the stem asks for the MANOVA design that also takes covariates into account, which is exactly what the C adds. Exam trap: in all these names, M means more than one DV and C means a covariate. ANCOVA has the covariate but only one DV, so it is the near-miss. The assumption the covariance designs add is homogeneity of regression slopes: the covariate must relate to the DV in the same way in every group.", "topic": "MANCOVA and its relatives", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a qualitative data analysis software?", "options": {"a": "ATLAS.ti", "b": "NVivo", "c": "MAXQDA", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: ATLAS.ti, NVivo and MAXQDA are all qualitative data analysis programs, members of the family called CAQDAS (computer-assisted qualitative data analysis software). Qualitative research produces words and images rather than numbers: interview transcripts, field notes, open-ended answers, videos. These programs let the researcher import that material, attach codes to passages, retrieve every passage with the same code, write memos, link codes into categories and draw maps of how themes relate. ATLAS.ti grew out of a project at the Technical University of Berlin; NVivo comes from QSR International in Australia and succeeded its older program NUD*IST; MAXQDA is made by VERBI Software in Berlin. Example: Riya has 25 interviews with caregivers of people with dementia. In NVivo she tags every mention of guilt, then pulls up all the guilt passages side by side to build a theme. Option d is right because each of the three named programs is a standard qualitative analysis package. Exam trap: the software only organises the data; the coding decisions and the interpretation remain the researcher's work. Do not confuse these programs with statistical packages such as SPSS, R, SAS or Stata, which analyse numbers; in a 'Which is not qualitative software?' question, SPSS is the usual answer.", "topic": "Qualitative analysis software", "fig": ""}, {"q": "As per Miles and Huberman, data analysis involves:", "options": {"a": "Coding", "b": "Abstracting", "c": "Categorising", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: for Matthew Miles and A. Michael Huberman (1984; 2nd edition 1994), qualitative analysis involves coding, categorising and abstracting, because all three are part of what they call data reduction. In Qualitative Data Analysis they describe analysis as three concurrent flows of activity. Data reduction is 'the process of selecting, focusing, simplifying, abstracting, and transforming' the raw field notes and transcripts: you code passages, write summaries, cluster codes into categories and pull out abstract themes. Data display organises the reduced data into matrices, charts and networks so that patterns can be seen at a glance. Conclusion drawing and verification notes regularities and explanations and then tests them against the data. The three run back and forth throughout a study rather than in a fixed sequence. Example: Arjun interviews 15 first-generation college students. He codes 'fear of letting parents down' in many transcripts (coding), groups it with related codes under 'family obligation' (categorising), and lifts that into the broader idea of 'carrying the family's hopes' (abstracting). He then lays the themes out in a matrix by gender (display) and checks his hunch against cases that do not fit (verification). Option d is right because coding, categorising and abstracting are all parts of data reduction in their model. Exam trap: students remember the three headings (reduction, display, conclusion drawing) and then fail to see that coding, categorising and abstracting are activities inside the first one. A third edition (2014) added Johnny Saldana as co-author.", "topic": "Miles and Huberman's analysis", "fig": "Matthew Miles and A. Michael Huberman"}, {"q": "The most common criticism faced by qualitative research is its:", "options": {"a": "Low external validity", "b": "Less generalizability", "c": "Subjectivity", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: low external validity, limited generalisability and subjectivity are all standard criticisms of qualitative research. Qualitative studies explore meaning and experience in depth, usually through interviews, observation or documents, with small purposive samples chosen because they are information-rich, not because they represent a population. That design brings two linked charges. First, findings from 8 or 10 participants in one setting may not hold elsewhere: this is low external validity, which is the same idea as limited generalisability under another name. Second, the researcher is the instrument who collects, codes and interprets the data, so personal values and expectations can shape the findings, another researcher might read the same transcripts differently, and replication is hard. Qualitative researchers reply that their aim is depth, not statistical generalisation. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) replaced the experimental criteria with trustworthiness: credibility, transferability (the reader judges the fit to another setting from thick description), dependability and confirmability, supported by reflexivity, member checking and triangulation. Example: Meera interviews nine first-time mothers with postnatal depression in one Chennai hospital. Her account is rich, but critics ask whether it applies to mothers elsewhere and whether her own views coloured her coding. Option d is right because each listed criticism is a standard one, and a and b are two names for the same problem. Exam trap: when two options say the same thing in different words, as a and b do here, neither can be the single correct answer, which points to 'All of these'.", "topic": "Criticisms of qualitative research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "If a researcher collects data along a straight line within a particular habitat, the sampling strategy involved is:", "options": {"a": "Line sampling", "b": "Area sampling", "c": "Line transect sampling", "d": "Expert sampling"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: collecting data along a straight line laid across a habitat is line transect sampling. A transect is a line or path across an area. The researcher marks the line, walks it, and records the organisms seen on or near it, usually noting each one's distance from the line. In a belt (strip) transect only individuals within a fixed width on each side are counted; in distance sampling the drop-off in sightings with distance is used to estimate population density for the whole area. The method comes from ecology and wildlife surveys (birds, deer, plants along a hillside) because it covers a large area cheaply and shows how species change from one zone to the next, such as from a riverbank to dry scrub. Social researchers borrow the idea in the 'transect walk' of participatory rural appraisal, where a team walks across a village with residents and notes land use, water points and housing along the way. Example: Kabir lays three 1 km lines across a grassland reserve in Gujarat, counts every blackbuck he sees and records its distance from the line, to estimate how many live in the reserve. Option c is right because the stem's two clues, a straight line and a habitat, define a transect. Exam trap: 'line sampling' is a vague near-miss that sounds right but drops the defining word; the standard name of the method is line transect sampling. Area sampling, the other look-alike, divides a region into geographic clusters rather than following a line.", "topic": "Line transect sampling", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a style of report writing?", "options": {"a": "APA", "b": "ASA", "c": "CMS", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: APA, ASA and CMS are all style guides used in writing research reports. A style guide sets the rules for how a report is laid out and, above all, how sources are cited in the text and listed at the end. APA style comes from the American Psychological Association's Publication Manual, now in its 7th edition (2020). It uses author-date citation, for example (Milgram, 1963), and is the standard in psychology, so it governs most dissertations and journal articles in the field. ASA style is the American Sociological Association's guide, also author-date, used in sociology. CMS is The Chicago Manual of Style from the University of Chicago Press, first published in 1906, which offers two systems: notes and bibliography (footnotes, common in history) and author-date. Other common styles are MLA (Modern Language Association, humanities), Harvard (a generic author-date style) and Vancouver (numbered references, medicine). Example: Riya writes her M.Phil dissertation in APA style, but when she sends a chapter to a history journal she must reformat the references in Chicago notes and bibliography. Option d is right because each abbreviation names a recognised style of report writing and citation. Exam trap: ASA is easy to misread as the American Statistical Association, and CMS as something technical; here ASA means the American Sociological Association and CMS the Chicago Manual of Style, both genuine style guides.", "topic": "Report writing and citation styles", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The concept of keeping participants, researchers or both unaware about what has been assigned to the treatment group is called:", "options": {"a": "Blinding", "b": "Confounding", "c": "Placebo", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: keeping participants, researchers or both unaware of who has been assigned to the treatment group is called blinding (or masking). Its purpose is to stop expectations from biasing the results. Participants who know they got the real treatment may improve simply because they expect to (the placebo effect), and researchers who know may treat or score the groups differently without meaning to (experimenter expectancy effects). Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode (1963) showed the second: students told their rats were 'maze-bright' recorded better maze learning than students told theirs were 'maze-dull', though the rats were alike. Levels of blinding: in a single-blind study the participants do not know their condition; in a double-blind study neither the participants nor the researchers who deal with them know; in a triple-blind study the data analysts are kept unaware as well. Example: in Kabir's trial of a new anxiety app against a sham app, the students do not know which app they have, and the psychologist who rates their anxiety at eight weeks does not know either. That is double-blind. In psychotherapy trials participants usually know which therapy they receive, so often only the outcome assessor can be blind. Option a is right because the stem gives the exact definition of blinding. Exam trap: do not confuse blinding with allocation concealment. Allocation concealment hides the random sequence before and during enrolment, so the recruiter cannot steer patients into a group; blinding hides the assigned condition after allocation.", "topic": "Blinding in experiments", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In which of the following studies is a group of subjects followed over time?", "options": {"a": "Case control study", "b": "Cohort study", "c": "Cross-sectional study", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: in a cohort study a group of people (a cohort) is identified and then followed over time to see what happens to them. Typically the cohort is free of the outcome at the start; members are classified as exposed or unexposed to some factor and tracked forward to see who develops the outcome. This gives incidence (new cases) and the relative risk, and it places the cause before the effect in time. A prospective cohort runs forward from now; a retrospective (historical) cohort uses old records to follow people from a past starting point. Classic examples are the Framingham Heart Study, which has followed residents of a Massachusetts town since 1948; the British Doctors Study of Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill, begun in 1951 to track smoking and death; and the Dunedin study in New Zealand, which has followed about 1,000 children born in 1972 to 1973. Example: Riya records the screen time of 800 Class 9 students in 2024 and follows them for five years to see who develops depression. Option b is right because following a group of subjects over time is the defining feature of a cohort study. Exam trap: the case-control study looks the other way. It starts with people who already have the outcome (cases) and similar people who do not (controls), then looks back at their past exposures, as in Doll and Hill's 1950 study of lung cancer patients. Case-control is backward-looking and gives odds ratios; cohort is forward-looking and gives relative risk.", "topic": "Cohort study design", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The overall score that is obtained from various subtests of Wechsler's test is called:", "options": {"a": "Large scale I.Q.", "b": "Full scale I.Q.", "c": "Composite I.Q.", "d": "Deviation I.Q."}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: on the Wechsler scales the overall score combined from the subtests is the Full Scale IQ (FSIQ). David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale, and later the WAIS (1955), WISC and WPPSI, because he wanted a profile of abilities as well as a single total. Each subtest gives a scaled score (mean 10, SD 3). On the WAIS-IV (2008), 10 core subtests form four index scores: Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed. The four indices together yield the Full Scale IQ, the best single estimate of general intellectual ability. Older editions combined a Verbal IQ and a Performance IQ into the FSIQ; the WISC-V (2014) uses five primary indices. Index scores and the FSIQ are reported as deviation IQs, with mean 100 and SD 15. Example: Arjun's index scores range from 85 on Working Memory to 112 on Verbal Comprehension. His FSIQ falls in the average range, but the gap between the two indices tells the clinician more than the total does. Option b is right because Full Scale IQ is Wechsler's own name for the overall composite. Exam trap: deviation IQ is tempting because the FSIQ is expressed as one. But deviation IQ is the scoring method, a standard score comparing a person with others of the same age, not the name of the total; every Wechsler index is a deviation score too.", "topic": "Wechsler Full Scale IQ", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "Emotional quotient is measured as:", "options": {"a": "M.A/A x 100", "b": "A/M.A x 100", "c": "EA/A x 100", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: on the textbook ratio formula, emotional quotient (EQ) = emotional age (EA) divided by chronological age (A) × 100. The formula copies the old ratio IQ. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age to get an intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied by 100 in the Stanford-Binet to remove decimals: IQ = MA/CA × 100. Other age-based quotients follow the same pattern: developmental quotient = developmental age/CA × 100, and the social quotient of Edgar Doll's Vineland Social Maturity Scale (1935) = social age/CA × 100. For EQ, emotional age is the age level of emotional maturity a person shows. Example: Meera is 10 years old, and her emotional maturity matches that of a typical 12-year-old. EQ = 12/10 × 100 = 120, above average. Emotional intelligence itself was defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) as the ability to perceive, use, understand and manage emotions; Daniel Goleman's Emotional Intelligence (1995) popularised it, and Reuven Bar-On's Emotional Quotient Inventory (EQ-i, 1997) made the label EQ common. Option c is right because it places emotional age over chronological age, which is how every ratio quotient is built. Exam trap: option a is the ratio IQ (mental age, not emotional age), and option b is the same ratio turned upside down. Key note: modern EI tests such as the EQ-i and the MSCEIT report deviation-style standard scores (mean 100, SD 15) rather than an age ratio, so no current test computes EQ this way. The ratio formula is the textbook definition the question asks for, so c stands.", "topic": "Emotional quotient formula", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The book \"Hereditary Genius\" is authored by:", "options": {"a": "Francis Galton", "b": "Darwin", "c": "Mendel", "d": "Weber"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: Hereditary Genius (1869) was written by Francis Galton (1822 to 1911), the English polymath who founded the study of individual differences. Galton traced eminence through the families of judges, statesmen, commanders, scientists, poets and others, and found that eminent men had far more eminent relatives than chance would predict. He concluded that ability is inherited, largely discounting the advantages of wealth, schooling and connections that such families also shared. The book launched his lifelong work on heredity: he popularised the phrase 'nature and nurture' (1874), coined the word eugenics (1883), ran an anthropometric laboratory in London (1884), developed correlation and regression towards the mean, pioneered twin studies and the questionnaire, and tried word association on himself (1879). Option a is right because Galton is the author of Hereditary Genius. Exam trap: Charles Darwin is the tempting wrong answer, because the book applies his ideas on variation and heredity to human ability and because Galton was his half-cousin. Darwin's own books were On the Origin of Species (1859) and The Descent of Man (1871); Galton wrote Hereditary Genius after reading the Origin.", "topic": "Galton's Hereditary Genius", "fig": "Francis Galton"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a correct statement?", "options": {"a": "Luria had nothing to do with the Luria-Nebraska Battery", "b": "Luria was the main proponent of the Luria-Nebraska Battery", "c": "The Luria-Nebraska Battery comprises three dimensions", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: of the four statements, the one meant as correct is that Alexander Luria had nothing to do with the Luria-Nebraska Battery. Alexander Romanovich Luria (1902 to 1977) was a Soviet neuropsychologist. In Higher Cortical Functions in Man (1962; English 1966) he described a flexible, qualitative examination: instead of comparing scores with norms, he watched exactly how and where a patient's performance broke down. He also grouped brain activity into three functional units (arousal; receiving and storing information; planning and checking action). Anne-Lise Christensen (1975) gathered his test procedures into a kit, Luria's Neuropsychological Investigation. Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch in Nebraska then turned those items into a standardised, scored battery (1978; manual 1980): Form I has 269 items in 11 clinical scales, from Motor and Rhythm to Memory and Intellectual Processes, and Form II adds a 12th, Intermediate Memory. Luria died in 1977, before the battery appeared; he neither built nor endorsed it, and his own method was the opposite of a fixed, normed battery. Option a is right because Luria lent the battery only his name and his test ideas. Exam trap: the name makes b look obvious, but a battery named after a person need not be built by that person. Option c fails because the battery has 11 or 12 clinical scales, not three dimensions. Key note: 'nothing to do with' overstates the case, because the items come from Luria's examination. But he took no part in developing or standardising the battery, which is the point the statement makes, so a stands.", "topic": "Luria and the Luria-Nebraska Battery", "fig": "Alexander Luria"}, {"q": "The original intent of Halstead's test was to describe the function of which part of the brain?", "options": {"a": "Occipital lobe", "b": "Temporal lobe", "c": "Hippocampus", "d": "Frontal lobe"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: Ward Halstead (1947) designed his tests to describe the functions of the frontal lobes. Working at the University of Chicago from the 1930s, Halstead set out to measure what he called 'biological intelligence', a brain-based capacity distinct from the school-type intelligence that IQ tests sample. He believed it depended mainly on the frontal lobes, and his book Brain and Intelligence: A Quantitative Study of the Frontal Lobes (1947) reported tests given to patients with frontal damage, patients with other brain damage and healthy controls. His tests included the Category Test (abstract reasoning and concept formation), the Tactual Performance Test (fitting blocks into a form board while blindfolded) and Finger Tapping. His student Ralph Reitan (1922 to 2014) found that the tests were sensitive to brain damage wherever it lay, not only in the frontal lobes, and turned them into the Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery for detecting and localising brain dysfunction, scored with a Halstead Impairment Index (0.5 or more suggests impairment). Example: Kabir, recovering from a frontal head injury, may still do well on vocabulary yet struggle to find the rule on the Category Test, the kind of loss Halstead wanted to capture. Option d is right because the original purpose was a quantitative study of frontal lobe function, as his book's title says. Exam trap: the finished battery is a general screen for brain damage, so students assume Halstead aimed at the whole brain or at memory structures; his original target was the frontal lobes.", "topic": "Halstead's biological intelligence", "fig": "Ward Halstead"}, {"q": "The Halstead-Reitan Battery encompasses:", "options": {"a": "Speech Perception Test", "b": "Aphasia Screening Test", "c": "Seashore Rhythm Test", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes the Speech-Sounds Perception Test, the Aphasia Screening Test and the Seashore Rhythm Test. The battery is named after Ward Halstead and Ralph Reitan (1922 to 2014): it began with Halstead's tests of 1947 and was expanded and validated by Reitan, his student, as a fixed battery to detect brain damage, judge its severity and suggest where it lies. Its core tests are the Category Test (abstract reasoning, learning from feedback); the Tactual Performance Test (blindfolded form board, giving time, memory and location scores); the Seashore Rhythm Test (are two rhythmic patterns the same or different?), adapted from the musical talent tests of Carl Seashore (1919) and tapping sustained auditory attention; the Speech-Sounds Perception Test (picking the spoken nonsense word from written choices); and the Finger Tapping Test (motor speed of each hand). Allied tests are the Trail Making Test, Parts A and B; the Reitan-Indiana Aphasia Screening Test, adapted from the screening test of Ward Halstead and Joseph Wepman (1949), which checks naming, reading, writing, spelling, arithmetic and copying shapes; a sensory-perceptual examination; and grip strength. Example: if Riya, after a left-hemisphere stroke, fails items on the Aphasia Screening Test and taps more slowly with her right hand, the pattern points to left-sided damage. Option d is right because each of the three named tests belongs to the battery. Exam trap: the Speech-Sounds Perception Test is often printed as 'Speech Perception Test', and the Aphasia Screening Test is an allied test, so students wrongly rule them out; the Rorschach, by contrast, is never part of the battery.", "topic": "Halstead-Reitan Battery tests", "fig": "Ward Halstead and Ralph Reitan"}, {"q": "How many cards in Rorschach's test are black and white?", "options": {"a": "5", "b": "3", "c": "6", "d": "4"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: five of the ten Rorschach cards are black and white (achromatic): Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test, a set of ten symmetrical inkblots, in Psychodiagnostik; he died the next year, aged 37. The cards are always shown in the same order, I to X. Of the other five, two (II and III) are black and grey with patches of bright red, and three (VIII, IX and X) are fully coloured in pastels. Hence the textbook phrase 'five black-and-white and five coloured'. Colour matters in scoring, because responses that use it (such as 'blood, because it is red') are read as signs of emotional responsiveness. Card V is the one most often seen as a bat or butterfly. Today records are usually scored with John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) or its successor, R-PAS (2011). Option a is right because Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII are the achromatic ones. Exam trap: 6 and 4 come from miscounting Cards II and III, which are mostly black but carry red, so they count as chromatic; 3 is the number of fully coloured cards, not the black-and-white ones.", "topic": "Rorschach achromatic cards", "fig": "Hermann Rorschach"}, {"q": "The Word Association Test is:", "options": {"a": "Objective", "b": "Subjective", "c": "Projective", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: the word association test is a projective technique. The examiner reads a list of stimulus words, one at a time, and the person replies with the first word that comes to mind. Because the stimulus is open and the reply unplanned, the answer is assumed to reveal inner associations the person would not disclose on a questionnaire. Francis Galton (1879) first tried the method on himself. Carl Gustav Jung (1904 to 1910) made it a clinical tool with 100 stimulus words, timing every reply and treating 'complex indicators' (a long delay, no reply, repeating the stimulus word, an odd or very personal answer) as signs of an emotionally charged complex. Grace Kent and Aaron Rosanoff (1910) published a 100-word list with frequency norms from 1,000 normal adults, so a reply could be scored as common or unusual. Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted projective tests into five families by the response they demand (association, construction, completion, choice or ordering, expression), and the word association test, with the Rorschach, is the classic association technique. Example: when the examiner says 'mother', Meera pauses for eight seconds and then says 'gone'; the delay and the reply point to a painful complex worth exploring. Option c is right because the test uses an ambiguous stimulus, an open response and a disguised purpose, the marks of a projective method. Exam trap: because its stimuli are plain words rather than inkblots or pictures, some papers treat the word association test as 'not projective'. By Lindzey's standard classification it is projective, and it is usually called the oldest projective technique.", "topic": "Word association as projective", "fig": "Carl Gustav Jung"}, {"q": "In the case of projective techniques, a therapist is more interested in exploring the _____ mind.", "options": {"a": "Conscious", "b": "Subconscious", "c": "Unconscious", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: projective techniques aim to explore the unconscious mind. Their logic is the projective hypothesis: when a person faces an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus (an inkblot, a vague picture, half a sentence), the stimulus supplies so little structure that whatever structure appears in the response must come from the person. Needs, conflicts, wishes and fears that the person cannot or will not report are 'projected' into the answer, usually without awareness. The idea draws on the topographic model of Sigmund Freud (1900), which divides the mind into the conscious (what we are aware of now), the preconscious (what can be recalled with effort) and the unconscious (wishes and memories kept out of awareness that still drive behaviour). For Freud, projection was a defence that attributes one's own unacceptable impulse to someone else; testing borrowed a broader sense. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term 'projective methods' and set out the hypothesis behind them. Example: shown the TAT card of a boy looking at a violin, Arjun tells a story about a father who smashes it in anger, and the clinician asks whether the punishing parent comes from Arjun's own life. Option c is right because projectives were designed to bypass conscious control and reach unconscious material. Exam trap: 'subconscious' looks like a synonym, but it is a lay word that Freud rejected because it blurs the preconscious and the unconscious; the technical term is unconscious.", "topic": "Projective hypothesis and the unconscious", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which edition of ICD is currently in use by therapists?", "options": {"a": "7th", "b": "10th", "c": "6th", "d": "11th"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: the edition of the International Classification of Diseases currently in use is the 11th, ICD-11. The ICD is the World Health Organization's (WHO) classification of all diseases and health conditions, used for diagnosis, health statistics and insurance worldwide. ICD-6 (1948) was the first to include a section on mental disorders. ICD-10 was endorsed by the World Health Assembly in 1990 and came into use from 1994, with mental disorders in Chapter V (F codes, such as F20 for schizophrenia) and its 'blue book' of Clinical Descriptions and Diagnostic Guidelines (1992). ICD-11 was adopted in May 2019 and took effect on 1 January 2022. Mental disorders now form Chapter 6, 'Mental, behavioural or neurodevelopmental disorders', with codes such as 6A20 for schizophrenia. Changes include new diagnoses such as complex PTSD, prolonged grief disorder and gaming disorder, a dimensional, severity-based model of personality disorder, and the removal of the schizophrenia subtypes. Example: Kabir, a clinical psychologist in Delhi, still sees F32 written in some hospital files, but the WHO's current code for a single depressive episode lies in the 6A70 block. Option d is right because ICD-11 has been the WHO's official edition since 2022. Exam trap: the 10th edition tempts because it was in use for nearly three decades and still fills Indian case records and textbooks. Key note: many hospitals, clinics and insurers, including in India, still code with ICD-10 during the change-over, which is why 10th is tempting. But 'currently in use' means the WHO's current edition, so d stands.", "topic": "ICD editions and ICD-11", "fig": ""}, {"q": "How many personality disorders are mentioned in DSM-5?", "options": {"a": "10", "b": "8", "c": "9", "d": "6"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: DSM-5 (2013) lists ten specific personality disorders. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, stays stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 sorts the ten into three clusters by descriptive similarity. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A memory aid is 'weird, wild and worried', with counts of 3, 4 and 3. DSM-5 also has personality change due to another medical condition and other specified and unspecified personality disorder, but these are not specific types. DSM-5-TR (2022) keeps the same ten, and DSM-IV also had ten. Example: Kabir is so preoccupied with orderliness, perfectionism and control that he misses every deadline; he fits obsessive-compulsive personality disorder in Cluster C. Option a is right because 3 + 4 + 3 gives ten specific disorders in the main (Section II) DSM-5 classification. Exam trap: the number 6 comes from the Alternative DSM-5 Model for Personality Disorders in Section III, which keeps only six specific types (antisocial, avoidant, borderline, narcissistic, obsessive-compulsive and schizotypal) and rates everyone else by level of personality functioning and pathological traits. ICD-11 goes further and drops the types altogether, using a single personality disorder graded mild, moderate or severe, with trait domain qualifiers.", "topic": "DSM-5 personality disorders count", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of Mendel's laws is generally considered universally acceptable and without any exception?", "options": {"a": "Law of Segregation", "b": "Law of Dominance", "c": "Law of Independent Assortment", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the law of segregation is the one Mendelian law treated as universal, with no standard exception. Gregor Mendel (1866), a monk who crossed pea plants at the abbey in Brno, published 'Experiments on Plant Hybridization'; it was ignored until Hugo de Vries, Carl Correns and Erich von Tschermak rediscovered it in 1900. Three rules come from his crosses. Law of dominance: in a heterozygote one allele (dominant) masks the other (recessive). Law of segregation: the two alleles of a gene separate when gametes form, so each gamete carries only one; the alleles never blend or contaminate each other, which is why it is also called the law of purity of gametes. Law of independent assortment: alleles of different genes sort into gametes independently of each other. Segregation follows directly from meiosis, where the two members of each chromosome pair are pulled apart, so it applies to every sexually reproducing diploid organism. Example: Riya's parents are both Tt for a trait; each egg or sperm carries either T or t, never both, which is how a tt child can appear from two Tt parents. Option a is right because segregation is the only law with no textbook exception, while the other two break in well-known cases. Exam trap: dominance is the law students meet first, so it feels the most basic, but incomplete dominance (red x white snapdragons give pink) and codominance (blood group AB) break it. Key note: strictly, rare events can upset it (nondisjunction can put both alleles into one gamete, and meiotic drive lets one allele reach more than half of the gametes), but textbooks treat segregation as the universal law, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Mendel's law of segregation", "fig": "Gregor Mendel"}, {"q": "Watson and Crick proposed:", "options": {"a": "How many complementary bases may pair", "b": "Mutation through tautomerism", "c": "Semi-conservative replication", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: Watson and Crick proposed all three: specific complementary base pairing, mutation through tautomeric shifts and semi-conservative replication. James Watson and Francis Crick (1953) published the double-helix model of DNA in Nature on 25 April 1953. They built on X-ray data from Rosalind Franklin (her Photo 51, 1952) and Maurice Wilkins (1953), and on Erwin Chargaff's (1950) finding that the amount of adenine equals thymine and guanine equals cytosine. Their model has two antiparallel sugar-phosphate strands with the bases inside, held by hydrogen bonds in fixed pairs: adenine with thymine, guanine with cytosine. That is the point of option a, loosely worded: only complementary bases may pair. The paper ended: 'It has not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have postulated immediately suggests a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material.' Their second Nature paper (30 May 1953) spelled this out: the strands unwind and each serves as a template for a new partner, so every daughter molecule keeps one old strand and one new strand (option c). The same paper suggested that spontaneous mutations arise when a base briefly takes a rare tautomeric form (a shifted hydrogen atom) and mispairs, for example adenine pairing with cytosine (option b). Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl (1958) later proved semi-conservative replication by growing bacteria on heavy nitrogen (15N) and then light nitrogen. Example: when Arjun draws a replication fork with each old strand paired to a new one, he is drawing the Watson and Crick proposal. Option d is right because each of the three statements comes from their two 1953 papers. Exam trap: students credit semi-conservative replication to Meselson and Stahl; they proved it, but Watson and Crick proposed it.", "topic": "Watson and Crick's DNA model", "fig": "James Watson and Francis Crick"}, {"q": "In the human genome, chromosome _____ has the most genes and _____ the least.", "options": {"a": "1, Y", "b": "2, X", "c": "Y, 1", "d": "2, Y"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: chromosome 1 has the most genes and the Y chromosome the fewest. A human body cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes, numbered roughly from largest (1) to smallest, plus the sex chromosomes, XX in females and XY in males. The whole genome holds about 20,000 protein-coding genes, a figure firmed up after the Human Genome Project was completed in 2003. Chromosome 1 is the largest, about 8 percent of all human DNA, and carries roughly 2,000 protein-coding genes, more than any other chromosome. The Y chromosome is small, much of it repetitive DNA, and carries only a few dozen protein-coding genes. The best known is SRY (the sex-determining region of the Y), which switches on testis development in the embryo. Over evolution the Y lost most of the genes it once shared with the X, because along most of its length it does not swap segments with a partner chromosome, so damaged genes are not repaired by recombination. Option a is right because it names the largest, gene-richest chromosome (1) first and the gene-poorest (Y) second, matching the order of the blanks. Exam trap: X and Y form a pair, so students assume they are alike, but the X is a large chromosome with about 800 protein-coding genes, while the Y has only a few dozen.", "topic": "Gene counts on human chromosomes", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The actual structure of t-RNA looks like:", "options": {"a": "Inverted U", "b": "S shaped", "c": "Inverted L", "d": "T shaped"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: in its real three-dimensional form, transfer RNA (tRNA) is folded into an inverted L shape. tRNA is the adaptor molecule of protein synthesis: one end carries a specific amino acid, the other end reads a codon on messenger RNA at the ribosome. Each tRNA is a single strand, usually about 76 to 90 nucleotides long, that base-pairs with itself. Drawn flat, this pairing gives the cloverleaf model, worked out by Robert Holley (1965) for yeast alanine tRNA (Nobel Prize 1968): an acceptor stem ending in CCA, where the amino acid attaches, a D arm, an anticodon arm and a T arm. The cloverleaf, however, is only the secondary structure. X-ray crystallography of yeast phenylalanine tRNA in 1973 to 1974, by the groups of Alexander Rich and Sung-Hou Kim and of Aaron Klug, revealed the tertiary structure: the arms stack on one another to form two limbs at right angles, an L. The acceptor end sits at the tip of one limb and the anticodon loop at the tip of the other, so the amino acid and the codon-reading part are held far apart, which suits their separate jobs inside the ribosome. Example: when Meera revises translation, she should draw the cloverleaf to show which bases pair, but remember that the molecule in the cell is the compact inverted L. Option c is right because inverted L is the standard name for tRNA's actual, folded shape. Exam trap: the cloverleaf is the most familiar diagram, and the word 'actual' in the stem is there to push you past it to the three-dimensional form.", "topic": "tRNA tertiary structure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In a typical monohybrid cross, the phenotypic ratio is _____ and the genotypic ratio is _____.", "options": {"a": "3:1, 1:2:1", "b": "1:2:1, 3:1", "c": "1:3, 1:1:2", "d": "3:1, 1:3"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: in a typical monohybrid cross the F2 phenotypic ratio is 3:1 and the genotypic ratio is 1:2:1. A monohybrid cross follows one gene with two alleles. Gregor Mendel (1866) crossed pure-breeding tall pea plants (TT) with pure-breeding dwarf plants (tt). Every F1 plant was Tt and tall, because T is dominant. When the F1 plants self-pollinated (Tt x Tt), each parent made T and t gametes in equal numbers (segregation), and a Punnett square gives four equally likely combinations: TT, Tt, tT and tt. Genotypes: 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt. Phenotypes: TT and Tt plants are both tall, so 3 tall : 1 dwarf. Mendel's own counts came very close, for example 787 tall to 277 dwarf plants, about 2.84 to 1. Option a is right because it gives the phenotype ratio (3:1) in the first blank and the genotype ratio (1:2:1) in the second, matching the order in the stem. Exam trap: the two ratios are often printed in swapped order, so check which blank asks for appearance (phenotype) and which for genetic make-up (genotype). Related ratios worth knowing: a test cross (Tt x tt) gives 1:1, a dihybrid F2 gives 9:3:3:1, and with incomplete dominance the phenotype ratio equals the genotype ratio, 1:2:1.", "topic": "Monohybrid cross ratios", "fig": "Gregor Mendel"}, {"q": "The concept of the synapse was given by:", "options": {"a": "Donald Hebb", "b": "Milner", "c": "William Sherrington", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the concept, and the name, of the synapse came from the British physiologist Charles Scott Sherrington (1897). A synapse is the junction where one neuron passes its signal to another cell across a tiny gap. Santiago Ramon y Cajal's neuron doctrine (from 1888) had argued that neurons are separate cells rather than a continuous net, which raised the question of how they communicate. Sherrington, writing the nervous system volume of the seventh edition of Michael Foster's Textbook of Physiology (1897), introduced the word synapse (from the Greek for 'clasping together'); he had first proposed 'syndesm', and the Cambridge classicist Arthur Verrall suggested the better word. Sherrington never saw a synapse. He inferred its properties from spinal reflexes in cats and dogs: a delay at the junction, temporal and spatial summation, one-way conduction and central inhibition, including reciprocal inhibition of opposing muscles. The Integrative Action of the Nervous System (1906) set this out, and he shared the 1932 Nobel Prize with Edgar Adrian. Example: when Kabir's knee jerks to a tap, the tiny delay inside his spinal cord is the kind of evidence Sherrington used. Option c is right because Sherrington is the scientist credited with the synapse concept. Exam trap: Donald Hebb (1949) is famous for the 'Hebbian synapse', so his name sounds right, but he described how synapses strengthen with use, not the synapse itself. Key note: the paper prints 'William Sherrington'; the man is Charles Scott Sherrington, and the option clearly means him, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Origin of the synapse concept", "fig": "Charles Scott Sherrington"}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about cardiac pacemaker cells?", "options": {"a": "They exhibit graded depolarization", "b": "They deviate from the norms of the usual action potential", "c": "They have an unstable resting membrane potential", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three statements are true of cardiac pacemaker cells. These are specialised heart muscle cells, mainly in the sinoatrial (SA) node in the wall of the right atrium, that set the heartbeat by firing on their own (automaticity). A neuron or a ventricular muscle cell sits at a stable resting potential (about -70 mV in a neuron, nearer -90 mV in ventricular muscle) until something stimulates it. Pacemaker cells have no stable rest. After each beat the membrane starts at about -60 mV and drifts slowly upward, the pacemaker potential (phase 4): first through the 'funny current' (If), a mainly sodium inward current through HCN channels that open when the cell is most negative, then through T-type calcium channels. When the drift reaches threshold, about -40 mV, L-type calcium channels open and carry the upstroke. This upstroke is slower than the fast sodium spike of neurons, and there is no plateau as in ventricular muscle; potassium channels then repolarise the cell and the cycle repeats. The autonomic nervous system tunes the slope of the drift: sympathetic noradrenaline steepens it and speeds the heart, vagal acetylcholine flattens it and slows the heart. Example: when Riya runs up the stairs, sympathetic input steepens her pacemaker drift and her heart rate rises. Option d is right because the slow drift is a graded depolarisation (a), the calcium-driven, plateau-free spike departs from the usual action potential (b), and the lack of a fixed resting level is an unstable resting membrane potential (c). Exam trap: students learn one textbook action potential (a fast sodium upstroke from a fixed -70 mV) and assume every excitable cell follows it; pacemaker cells are the standard exception.", "topic": "Cardiac pacemaker potentials", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The neurons that hyperpolarize initially and then rapidly depolarize are called:", "options": {"a": "Rebound neurons", "b": "Glial cells", "c": "Bipolar neurons", "d": "Unipolar neurons"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: neurons that are first hyperpolarised and then fire a rapid burst of depolarisation are called rebound neurons, after the behaviour known as post-inhibitory rebound. Normally an inhibitory input (an IPSP) pushes the membrane below rest and makes firing less likely. In some neurons, notably thalamic relay neurons, the hyperpolarisation itself primes a later burst. Two kinds of channel explain it. T-type (low-threshold) calcium channels are inactivated at the normal resting level; a spell of hyperpolarisation removes that inactivation, so when the inhibition ends they open and produce a low-threshold calcium spike crowned by a burst of sodium action potentials. The h-current, carried by HCN channels, is switched on by hyperpolarisation and also pulls the membrane back up. Henrik Jahnsen and Rodolfo Llinas (1984) described these properties in guinea-pig thalamic neurons. Post-inhibitory rebound lets inhibition create rhythm: it contributes to thalamic oscillations such as sleep spindles and to the alternating bursts of the central pattern generators for walking and breathing. Example: think of Arjun pressing a spring down and then letting go; the spring shoots back past its starting point, as the membrane does when the inhibition is lifted. Option a is right because it is the only option that names the firing behaviour in the stem; the other three are kinds of cell defined by shape or by not being neurons. Exam trap: bipolar and unipolar can sound like a two-phase or one-phase response, but they count the processes leaving the cell body and say nothing about firing pattern. Key note: 'rebound neurons' is not a formal cell class like bipolar or unipolar neurons; the behaviour is post-inhibitory rebound, and the other three options are structural classes, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Post-inhibitory rebound", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is affected by opiates?", "options": {"a": "Serotonin", "b": "Endorphins", "c": "Dopamine", "d": "GABA"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: opiates act on the endorphin system, the body's own opioid chemicals. Opiates are drugs from the opium poppy and their relatives: morphine, codeine and heroin (wholly synthetic drugs such as fentanyl are usually called opioids). They relieve pain, produce euphoria and slow breathing. In 1973 Candace Pert and Solomon Snyder showed that the brain has specific opiate receptors, which raised the question of why a brain would carry receptors for a plant drug. In 1975 John Hughes and Hans Kosterlitz isolated the first natural ligands, the enkephalins; the endorphins (the name means 'endogenous morphine') and the dynorphins followed. These endogenous opioid peptides act at mu, delta and kappa receptors, and morphine and heroin act mainly at mu receptors by mimicking them. The system damps pain, especially in the periaqueductal grey and the spinal cord, and is released in stress, injury and intense exercise. Example: Kabir, a long-distance runner, feels less pain late in a race partly because his endorphins are acting on the same receptors that morphine would occupy. Option b is right because opiates bind directly to the receptors of the endorphin (opioid) system. Exam trap: dopamine is the famous 'reward' transmitter and opiates do raise dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens, so c tempts, but that effect is indirect. Key note: endorphins are neuropeptides that act as neuromodulators rather than classic small-molecule transmitters, and opiates reach dopamine and GABA only indirectly (they inhibit GABA interneurons in the ventral tegmental area, which frees the dopamine neurons); the system opiates act on directly is the endorphin system, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Opiates and endogenous opioids", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following is an example of a generalized reinforcer?", "options": {"a": "Grades", "b": "Social smile", "c": "Money", "d": "Water"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: money is the classic generalised reinforcer. B. F. Skinner (1953), in Science and Human Behavior, separated primary reinforcers, which work without learning because they meet biological needs (food, water, warmth), from conditioned (secondary) reinforcers, which were once neutral but became reinforcing by being paired with primary ones. A conditioned reinforcer paired with many different primary reinforcers becomes a generalised reinforcer: it keeps its power whatever the person happens to lack at the moment, because it can be exchanged for almost anything. Money buys food when you are hungry, a drink when you are thirsty, shelter, entertainment and status, so it rarely loses its effect through satiation. Tokens in a token economy work the same way; Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) described long-stay psychiatric patients who earned tokens and exchanged them for privileges and goods. Example: Meera's parents give her a small sum for each revision chapter she finishes; because the money can buy books, snacks or a film ticket, it keeps working whether or not she wants any one of them right now. Option c is right because money is the textbook case: one conditioned reinforcer linked to many primary ones. Exam trap: water is the opposite type, a primary reinforcer, and it loses its power once thirst is satisfied. Key note: Skinner also listed attention, approval and affection as generalised reinforcers, so a social smile (approval) and grades (which lead to praise, admission and jobs) can work this way too; money is the textbook example because it exchanges for almost any primary reinforcer, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Generalised reinforcers", "fig": "B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "The idea that personality can be created by the interaction between a person, his environment and his behaviour is known as:", "options": {"a": "Circular creation", "b": "Interactionism", "c": "Reciprocal determinism", "d": "Combination theory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the idea that personality grows out of the mutual influence of the person, the environment and behaviour is Albert Bandura's reciprocal determinism. Albert Bandura (1978), in 'The self system in reciprocal determinism', argued against two one-way views: the radical behaviourism of B. F. Skinner (1953), in which the environment shapes behaviour, and trait views, in which inner dispositions drive behaviour. In his model, later called triadic reciprocal causation in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986), three sets of factors are joined by two-way arrows: person factors (thoughts, expectations, self-efficacy, feelings), behaviour, and the environment (other people, rewards, settings). Each is both cause and effect. People are not simply pushed by their surroundings; their behaviour also selects and changes the environment they then meet. Example: Meera expects to do badly in statistics (person), so she skips practice (behaviour); her teacher, seeing little effort, gives her less attention (environment), which lowers her expectation further. If she joins a study group, the same loop can run the other way. Option c is right because reciprocal determinism is the exact name for this three-way, mutual influence. Exam trap: interactionism also says that person and situation work together, but it is a two-factor model in which behaviour is only the outcome; Bandura's addition is that behaviour is itself a third, active cause.", "topic": "Bandura's reciprocal determinism", "fig": "Albert Bandura"}, {"q": "Which of the following traits is considered to be highly heritable?", "options": {"a": "Honesty", "b": "Cleanliness", "c": "Introversion", "d": "Steadfastness"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: introversion (the low end of extraversion) is the trait with strong, well-replicated heritability. Heritability is the proportion of variation in a trait, within a given population, that is due to genetic differences between people; it says nothing about how genetic one person's trait is. Behaviour geneticists estimate it by comparing identical (monozygotic) twins, who share all their genes, with fraternal (dizygotic) twins, who share about half, and by adoption studies. In the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart, run by Thomas Bouchard from 1979, identical twins raised in different homes were about as alike in personality as those raised together; Auke Tellegen and colleagues (1988) found that roughly half of the variation in most personality traits was genetic. Across twin studies, extraversion-introversion is one of the most consistently heritable dimensions. Hans Eysenck (1967) tied introversion to a chronically higher level of cortical arousal, and Jerome Kagan's studies of behaviourally inhibited infants in the 1980s showed that shy, cautious temperament appears very early. Example: Riya and her identical twin, adopted into different families in Pune and Chennai, both turn out quiet, prefer small gatherings and need time alone, while their adoptive siblings differ. Option c is right because introversion-extraversion is a broad temperament dimension with a strong biological basis. Exam trap: honesty sounds like a fixed part of character, but Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928) found that children's honesty changed from one situation to the next.", "topic": "Heritability of introversion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "All schizophrenics exhibit:", "options": {"a": "Sad mood", "b": "Anhedonia", "c": "Distorted thinking", "d": "Delusions"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: distorted (disordered) thinking is the feature treated as present in all people with schizophrenia. Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) called the illness dementia praecox, stressing early onset and decline. Eugen Bleuler (1911) renamed it schizophrenia ('split mind', meaning a splitting of mental functions, not multiple personalities) and divided its symptoms into fundamental symptoms, present in every case, and accessory symptoms, such as delusions and hallucinations, which come and go. His fundamental symptoms are the four As: loosened associations, flat or incongruous affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into an inner world). For Bleuler the loosening of associations, a disturbance of thinking, was the central one. Modern criteria differ: DSM-5 needs two or more of five symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms) for a significant part of one month, at least one being delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech, with signs lasting six months. Example: Arjun hears voices and has grown withdrawn and flat but holds no delusions; he still meets DSM-5 criteria, and a careful interview often shows loose, tangential thinking, Bleuler's core sign. Option c is right because, in the classical view the stem reflects, disturbed thinking is the core of the illness and the only listed option close to universal. Exam trap: delusions are the best-known symptom, but they are accessory in Bleuler's scheme and not required by DSM-5. Key note: modern criteria make no symptom universal, but in Bleuler's sense distorted thinking is the core feature of the illness, and it is the only option that comes close to 'all', so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Core symptom of schizophrenia", "fig": "Eugen Bleuler"}, {"q": "The relationship between Parkinsonism and schizophrenia is:", "options": {"a": "Both are caused by too much dopamine", "b": "Both are caused by too little dopamine", "c": "Both are caused by excess use of amphetamines", "d": "Schizophrenia is associated with too much dopamine and Parkinsonism with too little"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: schizophrenia is linked with too much dopamine activity and Parkinsonism with too little, so the two sit at opposite ends of the same transmitter. Dopamine neurons form several pathways. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra to the striatum and helps control movement; the mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and serves reward and salience. In Parkinson's disease the dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra die; Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) found the striatum of patients almost empty of dopamine, which led to treatment with L-dopa, the precursor that crosses the blood-brain barrier. The result of the loss is resting tremor, rigidity, slowness of movement (bradykinesia) and a shuffling gait. The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia grew from drug evidence. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that chlorpromazine and haloperidol work by blocking monoamine receptors, dopamine among them; Philip Seeman and colleagues (1976) showed that clinical antipsychotic doses track how strongly the drugs bind dopamine receptors (the type now called D2); and amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can produce a paranoid psychosis. Excess mesolimbic activity is thought to drive the positive symptoms. The clinical see-saw shows the link: Kabir, given a D2-blocking antipsychotic for his voices, develops a tremor and a stiff, slow walk (drug-induced parkinsonism), while a Parkinson's patient on too much L-dopa may begin to hallucinate. Option d is right because it states this opposite-direction relationship. Exam trap: c sounds plausible because amphetamine does cause psychosis, but it does not cause Parkinsonism, which comes from the death of nigral dopamine neurons.", "topic": "Dopamine in schizophrenia and Parkinsonism", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Tardive dyskinesia is:", "options": {"a": "Memory loss", "b": "Loss of sensation", "c": "Psychomotor agitation", "d": "Repetitive movement of the face and the body"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: tardive dyskinesia (TD) is a disorder of involuntary, repetitive movements, mainly of the face and mouth and sometimes of the limbs and trunk. 'Tardive' means late and 'dyskinesia' abnormal movement: it appears after months or years of treatment with dopamine-blocking drugs, especially first-generation antipsychotics such as haloperidol and chlorpromazine, and sometimes the anti-sickness drug metoclopramide. Typical signs are lip smacking, chewing, puckering, tongue thrusting, grimacing and rapid blinking, along with writhing of the fingers or rocking of the trunk. The leading explanation is that long blockade of D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway makes them supersensitive, so the movement circuits overreact. TD may be irreversible, so prevention matters: the lowest effective dose and regular screening with the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS). In 2017 two VMAT2 inhibitors, valbenazine and deutetrabenazine, were approved in the United States to treat it. TD is the last of the extrapyramidal side effects to appear: acute dystonia within hours to days, akathisia and drug-induced parkinsonism within days to weeks, tardive dyskinesia after months to years. Example: Meera, on haloperidol for ten years, keeps making chewing movements and pushing her tongue into her cheek even when she is not eating. Option d is right because TD is defined by repetitive, involuntary movements of the face and body. Exam trap: psychomotor agitation sounds like 'too much movement', but restless pacing soon after starting an antipsychotic is akathisia, an early side effect, not TD.", "topic": "Tardive dyskinesia", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which one of the following is called the hunger centre?", "options": {"a": "LH", "b": "VMH", "c": "VSM", "d": "MH"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: the lateral hypothalamus (LH) was called the hunger, or feeding, centre. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, regulates eating, drinking, temperature and hormone release. Classic lesion studies in rats produced a dual-centre model of hunger. In 1940 Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson found that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) made rats overeat (hyperphagia) and grow obese, so the VMH looked like a satiety, or stop, centre. In 1951 the Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck found that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus made rats stop eating (aphagia) and drinking (adipsia), even to the point of starving, while electrical stimulation of the LH could make a well-fed animal eat. Eliot Stellar (1954) combined these findings into the dual-centre theory: the LH switches eating on, the VMH switches it off. Example: in the dual-centre picture, when Kabir has skipped lunch the LH 'start' signal dominates and he feels hungry; after a large thali the VMH 'stop' signal takes over and he feels full. Option a is right because the LH is the site the dual-centre model names as the hunger centre. Exam trap: VMH is the best-known hypothalamic eating abbreviation, so students pick it, but it is the satiety centre, the opposite role. The modern view is more complex: the arcuate and paraventricular nuclei, hormones such as leptin and ghrelin, and peptides such as neuropeptide Y and orexin all take part, and some VMH lesion effects came from damage to nearby fibre tracts and raised insulin.", "topic": "Lateral hypothalamus feeding centre", "fig": "Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck"}, {"q": "In DID, the host identity refers to:", "options": {"a": "The one that is first to emerge as a new identity", "b": "The most dominant one", "c": "The one that exists before the onset of the disorder", "d": "The one that is most erratic"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: in dissociative identity disorder (DID) the host is the dominant identity, the one in charge of the body most of the time. DID, called multiple personality disorder until DSM-IV (1994), requires two or more distinct personality states, or an experience of possession, that disrupt the sense of self, with recurrent gaps in memory for everyday events, personal information or trauma. The identities are of two kinds. The host is the identity that holds executive control most often, handles daily life, usually carries the person's legal name and is the one who comes for treatment; it is often unaware of the others. DSM-IV-TR described this primary identity as often passive, dependent, guilty and depressed. The alters are the other identities: child alters, protectors, persecutors and helpers, which may differ in age, gender, voice and handwriting. Changing from one to another is a switch. Most patients report severe childhood abuse or neglect. Example: Meera comes to the clinic as Meera, a quiet schoolteacher puzzled by clothes she does not remember buying; later an angry alter calling herself Rani appears in a session. Meera, who runs her life most days, is the host. Option b is right because 'most dominant' captures the host's defining feature, executive control most of the time. Exam trap: the host is often assumed to be the original person, but it is frequently not the identity that existed before the disorder began. Key note: c describes the original (birth) identity, which may be dormant and is not necessarily the host; the host is the one in charge most of the time, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "DID host identity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Chronic Fatigue Syndrome includes:", "options": {"a": "Extreme fatigue", "b": "Muscle pain", "c": "Chest pain", "d": "All of the above"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: chronic fatigue syndrome (CFS) can include extreme fatigue, muscle pain and chest pain. CFS, also called myalgic encephalomyelitis (ME/CFS), is a long-lasting illness of profound, unexplained exhaustion. The research definition most often taught is that of Keiji Fukuda and colleagues for the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (1994): at least six months of persistent or relapsing fatigue that is new, not the result of ongoing exertion, not substantially relieved by rest and that greatly reduces activity, plus four or more of eight symptoms: impaired memory or concentration, sore throat, tender lymph nodes, muscle pain, pain in several joints without swelling or redness, new headaches, unrefreshing sleep and post-exertional malaise lasting more than 24 hours. Other medical causes, such as an underactive thyroid or anaemia, must be ruled out. In 2015 the Institute of Medicine proposed the name systemic exertion intolerance disease, stressing that effort makes the symptoms worse. Its historical relative is neurasthenia, named by George Miller Beard (1869) for unexplained nervous exhaustion. Example: Riya, a software engineer, has been exhausted for eight months since a viral fever; a short walk leaves her in bed for two days, her muscles ache and her sleep never refreshes her. Option d is right because fatigue is the defining symptom, muscle pain is one of the listed criteria, and chest pain is among the other symptoms patients report. Exam trap: the name points only to fatigue, so a looks sufficient, but the syndrome is a cluster of symptoms. Key note: chest pain is not one of the core criteria; it is among the other symptoms some patients report, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Chronic fatigue syndrome symptoms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies utilizes body language the most?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Rogerian therapy", "c": "Group therapy", "d": "Gestalt therapy"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: Gestalt therapy makes the most use of body language. Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970), with his wife Laura Perls, built a therapy around awareness in the here and now; its founding book, Gestalt Therapy: Excitement and Growth in the Human Personality (1951), was written by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman. Its idea is that people avoid feelings and carry 'unfinished business', and that what they avoid shows in the body before it is put into words. So the therapist watches posture, gestures, breathing, tone of voice and facial expression, and draws the client's attention to them: 'What is your hand doing right now?' 'Be your hand; give it a voice.' Exaggeration exercises ask the client to repeat and enlarge a small movement, such as tapping a foot, until its meaning becomes clear. Other techniques include the empty chair (speaking to an absent person or a part of oneself), the top dog versus underdog dialogue, and staying with a feeling instead of explaining it. 'Why' questions are discouraged; 'what' and 'how' questions keep the client in present experience. Example: Kabir says calmly that he is not angry with his father, but his jaw is tight and his fist clenched; the Gestalt therapist asks him to notice the fist and speak as it. Option d is right because attending to and working with nonverbal, bodily expression is a central Gestalt method. Exam trap: Rogerian therapy is also present-centred and warm, but it works mainly through reflecting the client's words and feelings, not through the body.", "topic": "Gestalt therapy and body language", "fig": "Fritz Perls"}, {"q": "Hilde Bruch proposed a theory regarding:", "options": {"a": "Convulsive disorder", "b": "OCD", "c": "Eating disorder", "d": "Hysteria"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: Hilde Bruch proposed a theory of eating disorders, above all anorexia nervosa. Hilde Bruch (1962), a German-born American psychiatrist (1904 to 1984) who worked for decades with obese and anorexic young people in New York and Houston, described three core disturbances in anorexia nervosa: a distorted body image (the emaciated girl does not see herself as thin), a failure to read inner bodily signals such as hunger, fullness and emotion (interoceptive confusion), and an all-pervasive, paralysing sense of ineffectiveness. Her books Eating Disorders: Obesity, Anorexia Nervosa, and the Person Within (1973) and The Golden Cage (1978) traced these to early family life: a compliant, high-achieving child whose needs were met according to what her parents decided she needed rather than her own cues, so she never learned to trust her own feelings. In adolescence, rigid control of food and weight becomes the one area where she feels truly in charge. Bruch's treatment helped patients discover their own feelings and abilities rather than interpreting hidden conflicts for them. Example: Meera, a top student who has always done what her parents wanted, starts counting every calorie at fifteen; for Bruch the dieting is her bid for autonomy and control. Option c is right because Bruch's theory concerns anorexia nervosa and related eating disorders. Exam trap: anorexia was once called anorexie hystérique, so hysteria tempts, but Bruch's account is a developmental theory of control and selfhood, not a theory of hysteria.", "topic": "Hilde Bruch on anorexia nervosa", "fig": "Hilde Bruch"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Symptoms are caused and maintained by interpersonal problems. Reason (R): Interpersonal therapy is a psychodynamic therapy that focuses on current relationships.", "options": {"a": "Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A", "b": "Both A and R are correct and R is not the correct explanation of A", "c": "A is true but R is false", "d": "A is false but R is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: both A and R are true, but R does not explain A. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) was set out by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984) as a brief treatment, usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions. Assertion A is true as IPT's working premise: symptoms arise and are kept going in an interpersonal context, so the therapist links the depression to one of four problem areas (grief, role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal deficits) and works on that problem. Reason R is also true as a description: IPT grew from the interpersonal school of Adolf Meyer and Harry Stack Sullivan (1953) and from John Bowlby's attachment theory (1969), so it is often grouped with the brief psychodynamic therapies, and it focuses on current relationships rather than childhood conflict. But R does not explain A. R says what kind of therapy IPT is and where it looks; it gives no reason why symptoms come from interpersonal problems. If anything, A is the rationale for the therapy's focus, not the other way round. Example: Arjun became depressed after moving from Lucknow to Bengaluru for a new job and losing his friends; an IPT therapist treats this as a role transition and helps him build new ties. Option b is right because both statements hold but R is not the reason for A. Exam trap: when R repeats the topic of A, students assume it explains A; ask whether R answers 'why?' for A. Key note: IPT itself is agnostic about the ultimate cause of depression, and some would mark R false because IPT does not interpret the transference or dig into the past; as the statements are commonly read, both stand and R does not explain A, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT)", "fig": "Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman"}, {"q": "Multimodal therapy assesses a person's:", "options": {"a": "Cognitive functioning", "b": "BASIC ID", "c": "Biological profile", "d": "Overall intelligence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: multimodal therapy assesses a person's BASIC I.D. Arnold Lazarus (1973), a South African-born clinical psychologist trained in behaviour therapy, argued that lasting change needs work across all the modalities of human functioning, and developed multimodal therapy, later set out in Multimodal Behavior Therapy (1976) and The Practice of Multimodal Therapy (1981). BASIC I.D. is an acronym for seven interacting modalities: Behaviour, Affect, Sensation, Imagery, Cognition, Interpersonal relationships, and Drugs/biology (medication, health, diet, exercise). The therapist builds a modality profile, listing problems and treatments under each heading, and may use 'bridging' (starting in the client's preferred modality before moving to an avoided one) and 'tracking' (noting the firing order of modalities, for example sensation, then cognition, then affect). Lazarus called his stance technical eclecticism: borrow effective techniques from any school while explaining them with one theory, social learning. Example: for Riya's panic attacks the profile might list avoiding the metro (behaviour), terror (affect), palpitations (sensation), images of fainting (imagery), the thought 'I am dying' (cognition), dependence on her brother (interpersonal) and too much coffee (drugs/biology), each with its own intervention. Option b is right because BASIC I.D. is the multimodal assessment framework itself. Exam trap: Arnold Lazarus (multimodal therapy) is often confused with Richard Lazarus, the stress and coping theorist (Stress, Appraisal, and Coping, 1984, with Susan Folkman).", "topic": "Lazarus's multimodal therapy", "fig": "Arnold Lazarus"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"mphil-cp-2023": [{"q": "When conducting research on attribution theories, which type of attribution refers to the tendency to explain one's successes as a result of internal, stable factors, and failures as a result of external, situational factors?", "concept": "The answer is b: the self-serving attributional bias is the tendency to credit our successes to internal, stable causes and to blame our failures on external, situational ones. Attribution theory asks how ordinary people explain behaviour. Fritz Heider (1958) first split causes into internal (dispositional: ability, effort, character) and external (situational: luck, task difficulty, other people). Later research showed that our explanations are tilted, and this particular tilt depends on whether the outcome was good or bad. Dale Miller and Michael Ross (1975) reviewed the early studies and found the success half (taking credit) better supported than the failure half; a later meta-analysis by Amy Mezulis and colleagues (2004) confirmed that the bias is large and widespread, though smaller in many Asian cultures. The usual explanation is protection of self-esteem, helped by a wish to look good in front of others (self-presentation). For example, when Arjun tops his statistics test he says, 'I am good with numbers'; when he fails the next one he says, 'The paper was unfair and the invigilator kept disturbing us.' Option b is right because only the self-serving bias switches the direction of the explanation with the outcome: internal and stable for success, external and situational for failure. Exam trap: the fundamental attribution error also contrasts internal and external causes, but it concerns how we explain other people's behaviour (overrating their dispositions), not how we explain our own successes and failures.", "traps": {"a": "Dispositional attribution is one side of Fritz Heider's (1958) internal versus external distinction: explaining any behaviour by a person's traits or abilities. It names a type of cause, not a bias that switches between internal and external depending on success or failure.", "c": "The fundamental attribution error, named by Lee Ross (1977), is overrating dispositions and underrating the situation when we explain other people's behaviour. The stem is about explaining one's own outcomes, and the pattern changes with success or failure, which the error does not.", "d": "The covariation model is Harold Kelley's (1967) account of how we choose between causes using consensus, consistency and distinctiveness information. It is a rational model of how attribution should work, not a bias."}}, {"q": "Which social psychological theory suggests that individuals perceive others in terms of their positive or negative traits, leading to a \"halo\" effect?", "concept": "The answer is a: implicit personality theory is the set of unspoken beliefs each of us holds about which traits go together, and it is what produces the halo effect. Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri (1954) gave the idea its name. When we meet someone we know only a few of their traits, so we fill in the rest from our private 'theory' of personality: warm people must also be generous, clever people must also be hard-working, attractive people must also be kind. The halo effect is the best-known product of this filling-in. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after finding that army officers who rated a soldier high on physique also rated him high on intelligence, loyalty and leadership; one good impression spread to unrelated traits. It also works in reverse, when one bad trait darkens everything (the 'horns' effect). For example, Kavya's new colleague Rohan is cheerful and well dressed, so she assumes he is also competent and honest, though she has no evidence on either. Halo effects bias interviews, teachers' grading and performance appraisals, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. Option a is right because only implicit personality theory explains why a single positive or negative trait colours our judgements of all the others. Exam trap: the primacy effect (Solomon Asch, 1946) also shapes first impressions, but it is about the order of information (early traits weigh most), not about one trait spreading to the rest.", "traps": {"b": "Self-perception theory is Daryl Bem's (1967, 1972) idea that we infer our own attitudes by watching our own behaviour, as an outside observer would. It is about knowing ourselves, not about one trait of another person colouring the rest.", "c": "Trait-ascription bias is the tendency to see our own behaviour as flexible and driven by the situation while seeing other people's behaviour as fixed, predictable traits. It contrasts self and others; it does not explain the spread of one trait to unrelated ones.", "d": "The primacy effect, shown by Solomon Asch (1946) with the list 'intelligent, industrious, impulsive, critical, stubborn, envious', is the greater weight given to information that comes first. It explains order effects in impressions, not the halo."}}, {"q": "The Asch conformity experiment demonstrated that individuals are more likely to conform to group pressure when:", "concept": "The answer is a: in Solomon Asch's (1951, 1956) experiments people conformed most when the majority was unanimous. Asch seated one real participant among about seven confederates and asked each person, aloud, which of three comparison lines matched a standard line. The answer was obvious: tested alone, people erred on under 1 percent of judgements. But on 12 of the 18 trials (the critical ones) every confederate gave the same wrong answer. Participants went along on about 37 percent of critical trials, and about three quarters conformed at least once. In interviews, most yielders said they did not want to stand out (normative influence) or came to doubt their own judgement (informational influence). Asch then varied the set-up. When just one confederate broke ranks and gave the correct answer (a dissenting ally), conformity fell to roughly a quarter of its usual level; even a dissenter who gave a different wrong answer helped. Unanimity, not sheer numbers, was what mattered. For example, if Neha's whole study group insists that a wrong option is correct, she may go along; if one friend, Kabir, quietly agrees with her, she will usually stick to her own answer. Option a is right because breaking the majority's unanimity was the single strongest way to reduce conformity in Asch's work. Exam trap: group size does matter, but only up to about three or four confederates; beyond that conformity levels off, so a smaller group gives less conformity, not more.", "traps": {"b": "An easy, clear-cut task gives less conformity, not more. Conformity rises when the task is difficult or ambiguous, because people then use others as a source of information, as in Muzafer Sherif's (1935) autokinetic studies.", "c": "High self-esteem and self-confidence make people more willing to stand alone, so they lower conformity. People who feel insecure or low in status are the ones more likely to yield.", "d": "Asch found conformity rose as the unanimous majority grew from one to about three or four and then levelled off. A smaller group of one or two confederates produces less conformity, not more."}}, {"q": "According to the Realistic Conflict Theory, intergroup conflicts are more likely to occur when:", "concept": "The answer is c: realistic conflict theory holds that intergroup hostility arises when groups compete for limited resources that only one side can have. The logic is simple: if my group's gain is your group's loss (land, jobs, prizes, water), the groups come to see each other as enemies, and prejudice, negative stereotypes and aggression follow. Donald Campbell (1965) gave it the name realistic group conflict theory, but the classic evidence is the Robbers Cave experiment of Muzafer Sherif (1954; book 1961). Sherif took 22 boys aged about 11 to 12 to a summer camp in Oklahoma in two separate groups, who named themselves the Eagles and the Rattlers. A tournament with prizes only for the winners soon produced name-calling, a burned flag and raids on each other's cabins. Mere contact, such as shared meals and films, did not help; meals became food fights. What worked were superordinate goals, tasks neither group could achieve alone, such as repairing the camp's water supply and pulling a stalled supply truck. For example, if Arjun's and Imran's housing colonies must share one water tanker, quarrels and stereotypes grow; when both must work together to get a new pipeline approved, the hostility falls. Option c is right because competition for scarce resources is exactly the cause the theory names. Exam trap: shared and superordinate goals appear in the same study, but as the cure for the conflict, not its cause.", "traps": {"a": "Cooperation and positive interdependence, where each group needs the other to succeed, reduce intergroup conflict. They are part of the remedy Sherif used, the opposite of the condition the stem asks about.", "b": "Shared goals and a common superordinate identity (as in Samuel Gaertner and John Dovidio's common ingroup identity model, 2000) lower hostility. In Robbers Cave the superordinate goals ended the conflict; they did not cause it.", "d": "Exposure to positive outgroup exemplars, admired members of the other group, is a prejudice-reduction technique from contact and implicit-attitude research. It weakens prejudice rather than creating conflict."}}, {"q": "According to the Elaboration Likelihood Model (ELM), which route of persuasion occurs when individuals are motivated and able to process a persuasive message carefully?", "concept": "The answer is b: in the elaboration likelihood model the central route is taken when people are both motivated and able to think carefully about a message. Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) argued that persuasion depends on elaboration, the amount of careful thought a person gives to the arguments. When motivation is high (the issue matters personally, or the person enjoys thinking, called high need for cognition) and ability is high (enough time and knowledge, no distraction), people take the central route: they weigh the quality of the arguments, so strong arguments persuade and weak ones backfire. Attitudes formed this way last longer, resist counter-arguments and predict behaviour. When either motivation or ability is low, people take the peripheral route: they respond to cues such as an attractive or famous source, the sheer number of arguments or a catchy jingle, and the change is weaker and short-lived. For example, Meera, who is about to buy a laptop for her PhD work, studies battery and processor comparisons (central route), while her cousin Rohan, who is only browsing, is swayed by the cricketer in the advertisement (peripheral route). Option b is right because careful processing by a motivated and able person is the definition of the central route. Exam trap: heuristic and systematic are the two routes of Shelly Chaiken's (1980) heuristic-systematic model, a sister theory; the ELM's own terms are central and peripheral.", "traps": {"a": "The peripheral route is taken when motivation or ability is low, so people rely on surface cues (source attractiveness, number of arguments) rather than argument quality. It is the opposite of the condition in the stem.", "c": "The heuristic route belongs to Shelly Chaiken's (1980) heuristic-systematic model, where simple rules such as 'experts are usually right' guide judgement. It is not an ELM route, and it matches low-effort processing, not careful thought.", "d": "'Dual-process' is the umbrella name for the family of theories (the ELM, the heuristic-systematic model) that propose two modes of processing. It names the type of model, not one of its routes."}}, {"q": "The Zimbardo Stanford Prison Experiment demonstrated the powerful influence of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Stanford Prison Experiment showed how strongly social roles and the situation can shape behaviour. In August 1971 Philip Zimbardo, with Craig Haney and Curtis Banks, turned a basement of Stanford University's psychology building into a mock prison. Twenty-four psychologically healthy male students were randomly assigned to be guards or prisoners. Prisoners were 'arrested' at home, stripped of their names and known only by numbers; guards wore uniforms and mirrored sunglasses. Within days many guards became harsh and humiliating, and the prisoners, after a brief rebellion, grew passive, distressed and helpless. The study, planned for two weeks, was stopped after six days. Because the roles were assigned at random, Zimbardo argued that the cruelty came from the role and the setting, not from the personalities of the people in them. For example, Kabir, a gentle student made a 'guard', might start shouting at 'prisoners' simply because the uniform and the rules seem to demand it. The study has been heavily criticised: there was no control group, Zimbardo himself acted as prison superintendent, and Thibault Le Texier (2019) used archive material to argue that the guards were coached to be tough. Option d is right because the lesson the study is known for is the power of roles and situational factors. Exam trap: Stanley Milgram's (1963) studies also show the power of the situation, but they concern obedience to an authority's direct commands, which is a different study.", "traps": {"a": "Obedience to authority is the subject of Stanley Milgram's (1963) shock experiments, where 65 percent of participants obeyed to 450 volts. The prison study is about people slipping into assigned roles, not about following an experimenter's direct orders.", "b": "Groupthink is Irving Janis's (1972) account of how cohesive, insulated groups make poor decisions by suppressing dissent (the Bay of Pigs invasion). The prison study did not examine group decision making.", "c": "The bystander effect, shown by John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968), is that the more people witness an emergency, the less likely any one of them is to help. It concerns helping, not role behaviour in a prison."}}, {"q": "In the context of Cognitive Dissonance Theory, what term refers to the tendency of individuals to justify their efforts and investments in a decision, even if the decision turns out to be unfavorable?", "concept": "The answer is b: effort justification is the tendency to value something more because we suffered or worked hard to get it, even when the result turns out poor. It comes from Leon Festinger's (1957) theory of cognitive dissonance: holding two clashing cognitions ('I worked very hard for this' and 'this is worthless') is uncomfortable, and because the effort cannot be undone, people change the other belief and decide the outcome was worth it. Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills (1959) tested this. Female students who wanted to join a discussion group went through no initiation, a mild one or a severe, embarrassing one (reading obscene words aloud to a male experimenter). They then listened to a deliberately dull, halting discussion. The severe-initiation group rated the discussion and its members as far more interesting. The effect helps explain loyalty after harsh ragging or hazing, and why costly courses or therapies feel more valuable. For example, Arjun spends two years and his savings on coaching for a job exam, finds the job dull, yet insists to everyone that it was the best decision of his life. Option b is right because the stem describes justifying effort and investment already spent. Exam trap: rationalisation sounds similar, but it is a psychoanalytic defence mechanism, not a dissonance term. Key note: self-justification is Elliot Aronson's broader umbrella for all dissonance-driven justifying, so a is close; but the stem's 'justify their efforts and investments' names the specific form, effort justification, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Self-justification is Elliot Aronson's general term for every way people defend past choices to reduce dissonance. It is the broad family; the stem asks for the specific form tied to effort and investment.", "c": "Rationalisation is a defence mechanism (the term is Ernest Jones's, 1908) in which a person gives a plausible but false reason for behaviour to hide the real one. It belongs to psychoanalysis, not to dissonance theory's account of valuing effort.", "d": "Confirmation bias, studied by Peter Wason (1960), is seeking and favouring evidence that supports what we already believe. It concerns testing beliefs, not inflating the value of a costly choice."}}, {"q": "Who among the following philosophers is associated with Romanticism, which emphasizes intuition and emotion over reason and logic?", "concept": "The answer is d: of the four thinkers listed, Friedrich Nietzsche (1844 to 1900) is the one linked to the Romantic stress on instinct, passion and feeling over cold reason. Romanticism was a movement in art, literature and philosophy from the late 18th to the mid 19th century. Reacting against the Enlightenment's faith in reason, science and order, it prized emotion, intuition, imagination, individuality and closeness to nature. Its usual philosophical names are Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712 to 1778), Johann Gottfried Herder (1744 to 1803) and Friedrich Schelling (1775 to 1854). Nietzsche came later but grew out of the same tradition, shaped by Arthur Schopenhauer (1788 to 1860) and his idea of a blind, striving will. In The Birth of Tragedy (1872) Nietzsche contrasted the Apollonian (order, reason, form) with the Dionysian (passion, intoxication, instinct) and argued that life and art need the Dionysian force that rationalism had pushed aside. His later ideas, the will to power and his attack on the claims of pure reason and conventional morality, carry the same spirit, and they fed the later idea that hidden drives rather than reason steer behaviour. Option d is right because Nietzsche alone among the options championed instinct and emotion against rationality. Exam trap: Immanuel Kant (1724 to 1804) influenced the Romantics, but he himself was the great defender of reason. Key note: Nietzsche is often classed as a post-Romantic who later criticised Romanticism, and the standard Romantic names (Rousseau, Herder, Schelling) are not offered; since Locke, Descartes and Kant all champion reason or experience, Nietzsche is the best of the four and d stands.", "traps": {"a": "John Locke (1632 to 1704) was a British empiricist; in An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1690) he called the newborn mind a tabula rasa written on by experience. He stands for sense experience and reason, not intuition and emotion.", "b": "Immanuel Kant (1724 to 1804) was the leading Enlightenment philosopher of reason; his Critique of Pure Reason (1781) reconciled rationalism and empiricism through innate categories of the mind. His idealism influenced the Romantics, but he defended reason.", "c": "Rene Descartes (1596 to 1650) was a rationalist who held that certain knowledge comes from reason ('cogito ergo sum', I think, therefore I am) and proposed mind-body dualism. He is the opposite of a Romantic."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an example of negative reinforcement?", "concept": "The answer is c: giving an employee extra vacation days for working overtime is the only option in which a consequence makes a behaviour more likely, so it is the only reinforcement offered. B. F. Skinner (1938, 1953) sorted operant consequences into four cells using two questions. Does the behaviour increase (reinforcement) or decrease (punishment)? Is a stimulus added (positive) or removed (negative)? Positive and negative mean plus and minus, not pleasant and unpleasant. Negative reinforcement is the removal of an unpleasant (aversive) stimulus after a behaviour, which makes that behaviour more likely: you take a painkiller and the headache goes, so you reach for painkillers again; you buckle the seatbelt and the beeping stops. In option c, days off remove the demands of work, an aversive state, so overtime is strengthened by taking something unpleasant away. The other three options all aim to reduce a behaviour, so they are punishments, not reinforcements. Option c is right because it is the only option in which a behaviour (working overtime) is strengthened, and read as relief from work it is negative reinforcement. Exam trap: students often equate negative reinforcement with punishment because both sound bad; negative reinforcement always increases behaviour, while punishment decreases it. Key note: none of the four options is a clean textbook case, since extra vacation days can equally be read as a reward added, which would be positive reinforcement; but every other option is a form of punishment, so c stands as the only reinforcement option.", "traps": {"a": "A time-out removes the child from attention and enjoyable activity (time-out from positive reinforcement) in order to reduce misbehaviour. Something pleasant is taken away to decrease behaviour, which is negative punishment.", "b": "Taking away the phone removes a valued privilege so that breaking curfew becomes less likely. This is negative punishment, specifically response cost, not reinforcement.", "d": "A speeding ticket is meant to reduce speeding, so it is punishment: positive punishment if the ticket is counted as an added aversive, negative punishment if the fine is counted as money taken away. Either way it decreases behaviour and is not reinforcement."}}, {"q": "Who among the following psychologists is associated with object relations theory and is known for his work on early childhood development?", "concept": "The answer is d: Donald Winnicott (1896 to 1971), the English paediatrician and psychoanalyst, is a leading object relations theorist known for his work on early childhood. Object relations theory shifted psychoanalysis away from Sigmund Freud's (1905) drives towards relationships: the 'objects' are the people, above all the mother, whom the infant takes into its inner world, and these early bonds shape later personality. Its other major names are Melanie Klein (1882 to 1960), Ronald Fairbairn (1889 to 1964) and Margaret Mahler (1897 to 1985). Winnicott, who saw thousands of mothers and babies in his clinic, added several famous ideas. The good-enough mother meets the baby's needs almost completely at first, then fails in small, bearable ways, so the child learns to tolerate frustration. She provides a holding environment of physical and emotional safety. As the child learns that the mother is separate, it adopts a transitional object (1953), a blanket or soft toy that is neither fully 'me' nor 'not me' and soothes it when she is away. Good-enough care lets a spontaneous true self grow; without it the child builds a compliant false self. For example, two-year-old Anaya will not sleep without her old cotton dupatta while her mother is at work: the dupatta is her transitional object. Option d is right because Winnicott alone among the options belongs to the object relations school. Exam trap: Erik Erikson (1950) also wrote famously about childhood, but he was an ego psychologist with psychosocial stages, not an object relations theorist.", "traps": {"a": "Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) founded individual psychology: the inferiority complex, striving for superiority, style of life, social interest and birth order. He is a neo-Freudian, not an object relations theorist.", "b": "Carl Jung (1875 to 1961) founded analytical psychology, with the collective unconscious, archetypes and the introvert and extravert attitudes. His focus was the inherited psyche and adult individuation, not the infant's early relationships.", "c": "Erik Erikson (1902 to 1994) described eight psychosocial stages in Childhood and Society (1950) and belongs to ego psychology. He tempts because he wrote about childhood development, but he is not an object relations theorist."}}, {"q": "The \"Levels of Processing\" theory suggests that the depth at which information is processed during encoding influences its later retrieval. Which level of processing is associated with a shallow, superficial analysis of information?", "concept": "The answer is c: in the levels of processing theory, structural processing is the shallowest level, where only the physical appearance of a stimulus is analysed. Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972) argued against thinking of memory as a set of separate stores. Instead, memory is a by-product of how deeply we process information when we first meet it. There are three broad levels. Structural (visual) processing attends to how a word looks: 'Is the word in capital letters?' Phonemic processing attends to how it sounds: 'Does it rhyme with WEIGHT?' Semantic processing attends to its meaning: 'Does it fit the sentence: He met a ___ in the street?' Craik and Endel Tulving (1975) asked such questions and then gave a surprise memory test. Words processed for meaning were remembered far better than words judged for rhyme, and those better than words judged for type case. So deeper processing leaves more durable traces, and elaborative rehearsal (linking an item to meaning) works better than maintenance rehearsal (mere repetition). For example, if Riya only notices that a new term in her notes is printed in bold, she will soon forget it; if she asks what it means and links it to a patient she has seen, she will remember it. Option c is right because structural processing is the named shallow, superficial level of the model. Exam trap: bottom-up processing sounds shallow, but top-down and bottom-up are directions of processing in perception, not depth levels in Craik and Lockhart's model.", "traps": {"a": "Top-down processing is perception guided by knowledge, expectations and context (conceptually driven). It is a direction of processing in perception theory, not one of the depth levels in the levels of processing model.", "b": "Bottom-up processing is perception built up from raw sensory features (data-driven). It tempts because it starts from physical features, but it is a perception term, not the levels of processing name for shallow encoding.", "d": "Semantic processing, analysing meaning, is the deepest level in Craik and Lockhart's model and gives the best recall. It is the opposite of shallow processing."}}, {"q": "In the context of attention, which term describes the phenomenon where the detection of a target stimulus is impaired when it appears in close temporal proximity to another stimulus?", "concept": "The answer is b: the attentional blink is the failure to detect a second target that appears shortly after a first target in a rapid stream of stimuli. Jane Raymond, Kimron Shapiro and Karen Arnell (1992) named it using rapid serial visual presentation (RSVP): letters flash one after another in the same spot, about ten every second. Participants must identify a first target (T1, a white letter among black ones) and then report whether a second target (T2, a black X) appeared. If T2 comes about 200 to 500 milliseconds after T1, it is often missed, as if attention had blinked. If T2 comes immediately after T1 it is often still seen (lag-1 sparing), and after about half a second detection recovers. The usual explanation is that attention is still busy consolidating T1 into working memory, so T2 cannot be processed in time and fades. The blink shows that attention is limited in time, not only in space. For example, while watching a fast news ticker, Kabir spots his team's score and then misses the breaking headline that flashes a third of a second later. Option b is right because the stem describes impaired detection of a target that follows closely in time after another stimulus, which is the definition of the attentional blink. Exam trap: inattentional blindness and change blindness are also failures of awareness, but they concern unexpected objects and changes in a scene, not a second target close in time.", "traps": {"a": "Inattentional blindness, named by Arien Mack and Irvin Rock (1998), is failing to see an unexpected object in plain view while attention is elsewhere, as in Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris's (1999) gorilla study. It is about unexpectedness, not timing between two targets.", "c": "Change blindness is failing to notice a large change between two views of a scene, as when Daniel Simons and Daniel Levin (1998) swapped the stranger asking for directions behind a passing door. It concerns comparing scenes, not detecting a second target in a rapid stream.", "d": "The cocktail party effect, studied by Colin Cherry (1953), is the ability to follow one voice among many; Neville Moray (1959) later showed that people often catch their own name in the unattended message. It is about selective listening, not a gap in detection over time."}}, {"q": "Visual illusions are powerful tools for studying perceptual processes. The \"Müller-Lyer illusion\" and the \"Ponzo illusion\" are examples of:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Muller-Lyer and Ponzo illusions are classic geometric optical illusions. A geometric (geometrical-optical) illusion is a simple line drawing in which we misjudge length, size, direction or straightness, even when we know the true answer. In the Muller-Lyer illusion (Franz Muller-Lyer, 1889), two equal lines look unequal: the one ending in outward-pointing fins looks longer than the one ending in arrowheads. In the Ponzo illusion (Mario Ponzo, 1911), two equal horizontal bars are drawn between converging lines like railway tracks, and the upper bar, nearer the point where the lines meet, looks longer. Other members of the family include the Ebbinghaus circles and the Zollner, Poggendorff and Hering figures. Most viewers see these illusions, and they persist even after we measure the lines, which is why researchers use them to study how the brain interprets visual cues. Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits (1966) found that people raised among rectangular buildings are more prone to the Muller-Lyer illusion (the carpentered-world hypothesis). For example, Riya measures both Muller-Lyer lines with a ruler, finds them equal, and still sees one as longer. Option d is right because geometric optical illusions is the standard name for this class of figures. Exam trap: the Ponzo figure uses linear perspective, a monocular depth cue, so b tempts, but the figure is an illusion, not a cue. Key note: Richard Gregory (1963) explained both illusions as misapplied size constancy (inappropriate constancy scaling), so c is tempting; but that is an explanation of why they occur, not the name of their class, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Binocular depth cues, such as retinal disparity and convergence, need two eyes and signal how far away things are. These illusions are flat drawings seen just as well with one eye, so they are not binocular cues.", "b": "Monocular depth cues, such as linear perspective, interposition and relative size, signal distance with one eye. The Ponzo figure uses linear perspective, which is why this tempts, but the illusion itself is a misjudgement of size, not a depth cue.", "c": "Size constancy illusions is not the standard class name. Misapplied size constancy scaling is Richard Gregory's (1963) explanation of why these illusions occur, not the category they belong to."}}, {"q": "Working memory is a system responsible for temporarily holding and manipulating information. Which brain area is critically involved in working memory processes?", "concept": "The answer is b: the prefrontal cortex, especially its dorsolateral part, is critically involved in working memory. Working memory is the small, active workspace in which we hold information for a few seconds and work on it, as in mental arithmetic or following directions. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) split it into a central executive (an attentional controller), a phonological loop for sounds and words and a visuospatial sketchpad for images and locations; Baddeley added an episodic buffer in 2000. The brain evidence points to the frontal lobes. Joaquin Fuster and Garrett Alexander (1971) found neurons in the monkey prefrontal cortex that keep firing through the delay while the animal remembers where food was hidden, and stop once it responds. Monkeys with prefrontal lesions fail such delayed-response tasks, and brain imaging in humans shows dorsolateral prefrontal activity rising with working memory load, for example in n-back tasks. Damage here produces a dysexecutive syndrome: poor planning, distractibility and difficulty switching between tasks. For example, when Arjun keeps a new phone number in mind while dialling, and adjusts it when told one digit is wrong, his dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is doing the work. Option b is right because the prefrontal cortex holds and manipulates information online, which is what working memory means. Exam trap: the hippocampus is famous for memory, but it consolidates new long-term memories; after surgery removed about the front half of each hippocampus, patient H.M. could not form new long-term memories yet kept a normal digit span.", "traps": {"a": "The hippocampus consolidates new declarative memories into long-term storage. Patient H.M. (William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957) could not form new long-term memories after surgery removed about the front half of each hippocampus and nearby medial temporal tissue, yet his digit span and working memory were normal.", "c": "The amygdala handles emotional memory and fear conditioning, giving memories their emotional weight. It is not the workspace for holding and manipulating information.", "d": "The temporal lobes store long-term semantic knowledge and support recognition and language comprehension, and the medial temporal lobe houses the hippocampus. They serve long-term memory, not the online workspace of working memory."}}, {"q": "Flashbulb memories are vivid and highly detailed memories of emotionally significant events. According to Brown and Kulik's theory, what contributes to the formation of flashbulb memories?", "concept": "The answer is b: Roger Brown and James Kulik (1977) held that flashbulb memories form when an event is highly surprising and highly consequential, that is, emotionally arousing and distinctive. A flashbulb memory is a vivid, detailed and confident memory of the circumstances in which one heard shocking news: where you were, who told you, what you were doing. Brown and Kulik coined the term after asking Americans how they learned of President John Kennedy's assassination in 1963; most could describe the scene years later as if it had been photographed. They proposed a special 'Now Print!' mechanism: surprise triggers a check of how much the event matters, and if it matters, the whole scene is printed into memory. Emotional arousal is the engine; later retelling then shapes the story. Later research challenged the photograph idea. Ulric Neisser and Nicole Harsch (1992) had students write how they heard of the Challenger shuttle disaster the morning after; two and a half years later many accounts had changed, yet confidence stayed high. Jennifer Talarico and David Rubin (2003) found the same after the 9/11 attacks: flashbulb memories are no more consistent than everyday memories, but they feel more vivid. For example, Meera can 'see' exactly where she was when she heard about the 2008 Mumbai attacks, yet her story today may differ from what she wrote in her diary that week. Option b is right because arousal and distinctiveness (surprise plus consequence) are what Brown and Kulik named. Exam trap: rehearsal does matter in their account, but as a later shaping process, not as the trigger that forms the memory.", "traps": {"a": "Elaborate rehearsal and deep encoding come from the levels of processing tradition (Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart, 1972). Brown and Kulik saw retelling as shaping the memory afterwards, not as what creates the flashbulb.", "c": "The serial position effect and its primacy component describe better recall of the first and last items of a list (Bennet Murdock, 1962). They concern list learning, not memories of shocking events.", "d": "Semantic clustering and spreading activation belong to network models of semantic memory (Allan Collins and Elizabeth Loftus, 1975). They explain how related concepts prime each other, not vivid memories of emotional events."}}, {"q": "The \"framing effect\" in decision making refers to the phenomenon where individuals' choices are influenced by the way a decision is presented. Which prospect theory concept explains why people tend to be risk-averse when options are framed in terms of gains and risk-seeking when options are framed in terms of losses?", "concept": "The answer is c: loss aversion is the prospect theory concept offered here that explains why framing the same choice as gains or as losses changes people's appetite for risk. Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky (1979) proposed prospect theory to describe how people actually decide under risk. Outcomes are judged as gains or losses from a reference point, not as final states of wealth, and losses loom larger than equal gains: losing ₹1,000 hurts roughly twice as much as winning ₹1,000 pleases. Tversky and Kahneman (1981) showed the framing effect with the Asian disease problem, in which 600 people face a deadly outbreak. Framed as gains ('200 people will be saved' versus a one-third chance that all 600 are saved), most people chose the sure option: risk aversion. Framed as losses ('400 people will die' versus a one-third chance that nobody dies), most chose the gamble: risk seeking. The options were identical; only the wording changed. For example, Kabir agrees to a surgery when his doctor says it has a 90 percent survival rate, while his friend refuses the same surgery when told it has a 10 percent death rate. Option c is right because loss aversion, the heavier weight of losses, is the prospect theory principle that pushes people to gamble rather than accept a sure loss. Exam trap: the endowment effect also grows out of loss aversion, but it is about overvaluing what we own, not about risk under framing. Key note: strictly, the switch in risk attitude comes from the S-shaped value function (concave for gains, convex for losses: diminishing sensitivity), and loss aversion is the separate fact that the loss limb is steeper; but loss aversion is the only prospect theory concept among the options, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Anchoring bias, from Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman's (1974) heuristics and biases work, is staying too close to an initial number when making an estimate. It concerns numerical estimates, not risk attitudes under gain and loss frames.", "b": "The endowment effect is valuing an object more once we own it (Richard Thaler, 1980; Daniel Kahneman, Jack Knetsch and Richard Thaler, 1990, with coffee mugs). It is a consequence of loss aversion about ownership, not the account of risk aversion for gains and risk seeking for losses.", "d": "Overconfidence bias is rating our own knowledge or accuracy higher than it really is. It is not a prospect theory concept and says nothing about how framing shifts risk preference."}}, {"q": "Formal reasoning involves deductive reasoning based on logical rules. Which famous logical problem illustrates the challenges of formal reasoning and the tendency for people to focus on irrelevant information?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Wason selection task is the famous logical problem that shows how poorly people handle formal reasoning and how they are drawn to irrelevant information. Peter Wason (1966) showed people four cards: E, K, 4 and 7. Each card has a letter on one side and a number on the other. The rule is: 'If a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other.' Which cards must be turned over to test the rule? The correct answer is E and 7. E must be checked because an odd number behind it would break the rule; 7 must be checked because a vowel behind it would break the rule. K is irrelevant, and so is 4, because the rule says nothing about what must lie behind an even number. Fewer than one person in ten gets it right; most choose E and 4, or E alone. They pick the cards named in the rule (matching bias) and look for confirming cases instead of the one card that could falsify it. Performance improves sharply with familiar social rules: Richard Griggs and James Cox (1982) used 'If a person is drinking beer, they must be over 19', and Leda Cosmides (1989) argued that humans have evolved a skill for detecting cheaters. For example, Riya easily checks the beer drinker and the 16-year-old at a party, yet fails the identical logic with letters and numbers. Option b is right because the selection task is the classic demonstration of these errors. Exam trap: syllogisms also test deduction and show belief bias, but the famous problem for confirmation-seeking and irrelevant cards is Wason's.", "traps": {"a": "The Monty Hall problem is a probability puzzle: after the host opens a losing door, switching wins two times in three. It shows poor intuition about conditional probability, not errors in testing a conditional rule.", "c": "Syllogism tasks (two premises and a conclusion) also test deduction and reveal belief bias and the atmosphere effect. But the famous problem for focusing on irrelevant cards and seeking confirmation is Wason's selection task.", "d": "The Tower of Hanoi is a well-defined puzzle of moving discs between pegs, used to study planning and means-end analysis (Allen Newell and Herbert Simon). It is about problem solving, not formal deductive reasoning."}}, {"q": "According to the \"Whorfian hypothesis\" (linguistic relativity), the language we speak influences our thoughts and perceptions of the world. Which version of the hypothesis suggests that language has a strong determinative influence on thought and perception?", "concept": "The answer is a: linguistic determinism is the strong version of the Whorfian hypothesis, the claim that language determines thought and perception. Edward Sapir (1884 to 1939) and his student Benjamin Lee Whorf (1897 to 1941) argued that the language we speak shapes how we see the world; Whorf's essays were collected in Language, Thought and Reality (1956). The idea comes in two strengths. The strong version, linguistic determinism, says language fixes thought: if your language has no word or grammatical form for something, you cannot think it. The weak version, linguistic relativity, says only that language influences thought, making some distinctions easier to notice, remember or talk about. The strong version has largely failed. Eleanor Rosch (then Heider, 1972) found that the Dani of New Guinea, who have only two basic colour words, still remembered focal colours much as English speakers do. The weak version has some support, such as small speed advantages in telling apart colours that one's language names separately. For example, Hindi separates chacha, mama, mausa and phupha where English has only 'uncle'. On the strong version, Kabir's English-speaking friend could not grasp these relationships; in fact he can, though he needs more words to express them, which fits relativity at most. Option a is right because determinism is the strong, determinative claim the stem describes. Exam trap: linguistic relativity, the label printed in the stem itself, is the weak version, so it cannot be the strong one.", "traps": {"b": "Linguistic relativism (relativity) is the weak version: language influences thought and makes some distinctions easier, but does not fix what we can think. It is the softer claim, not the strong determinative one.", "c": "Linguistic universals are features shared by all languages (Joseph Greenberg, 1963; Noam Chomsky's universal grammar, 1965). The idea stresses what all languages and minds have in common, the opposite emphasis to Whorf's.", "d": "Linguistic determinacy is a made-up near-miss on determinism. It is not a version of the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis."}}, {"q": "In language, pragmatics refers to the study of how context influences the interpretation of meaning. Which of the following is an example of a pragmatic inference?", "concept": "The answer is d: inferring what a speaker really means from their tone of voice is a pragmatic inference. Language is studied at several levels: phonology (sounds), morphology and syntax (word forms and grammar), semantics (the literal meaning of words and sentences) and pragmatics (how people use language in context to mean more than the words say). Pragmatics covers indirect requests ('Can you pass the salt?' is a request, not a question about ability), politeness, turn-taking and sarcasm. Paul Grice (1975) explained much of this with his cooperative principle: listeners assume speakers are being informative, truthful, relevant and clear (the maxims of quantity, quality, relation and manner). When a speaker seems to break a maxim, the listener infers a hidden meaning, called a conversational implicature. Tone of voice (prosody) is one of the strongest cues, since the same words can praise or mock. For example, when Kabir drops his phone in a puddle and his friend says 'Great job' in a flat, drawn-out voice, Kabir knows he means the opposite. The literal meaning is praise; the intended meaning is teasing. Reading it needs context and knowledge of the speaker, not just a dictionary, which is why people with autism spectrum disorder or right-hemisphere damage often struggle with such inferences. Option d is right because it goes beyond the literal words to the speaker's intention, using context and tone. Exam trap: metaphor also goes beyond literal meaning, so c tempts, but c is about knowing the figurative sense of an expression, while d is about reading one speaker's intention in one situation. Key note: many linguists, following Paul Grice, treat metaphor as a pragmatic implicature too, so c is not wholly outside pragmatics; but inferring intended meaning from tone is the clearer, textbook pragmatic inference, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Recognising which homophone is meant (a river bank or a money bank) from the surrounding words is lexical ambiguity resolution. It uses sentence context to select a word's literal meaning, which is semantics and word recognition, not inference about the speaker's intention.", "b": "Using verb tense to mark when an event happened is grammar: morphology (the -ed ending) and syntax. It is part of the literal structure of the sentence, not a pragmatic inference.", "c": "Identifying a metaphor ('time is a thief') by its figurative meaning concerns the figurative sense of the expression itself, although Paul Grice treated metaphor as a kind of implicature. It is a borderline case, while d is the clear case of reading a speaker's intended meaning from context."}}, {"q": "Kurt Lewin's field theory emphasizes the dynamic interplay between an individual and their environment. What term refers to the psychological space around a person, including their thoughts, feelings, and experiences?", "concept": "The answer is d: in Kurt Lewin's (1936) field theory the life space is the whole psychological world of a person at a given moment, the person plus the environment as he or she perceives it. Lewin (1890 to 1947), a Gestalt-trained psychologist who fled Nazi Germany, borrowed the idea of a field from physics: behaviour is caused by all the forces acting on a person at that moment. His formula B = f(P, E) says behaviour is a function of the person and the environment, and the two together make up the life space. The environment here is psychological, not physical: what matters is how things appear to the person, including his or her needs, goals, fears and memories. In Principles of Topological Psychology (1936) Lewin mapped the life space as regions (activities and goals) separated by boundaries; regions carry positive or negative valences that attract or repel, and vectors (forces) push the person along paths. The framework let him describe conflicts such as approach-approach and approach-avoidance, and it led to his later work on group dynamics. For example, Arjun's life space on the evening before an exam includes his textbook (a goal region), a cricket match on TV (an attractive region with positive valence), his fear of failing (a negative valence) and a power cut (a barrier). Option d is right because life space is Lewin's own term for the psychological space around a person. Exam trap: the word field appears in field theory, so emotional field sounds Lewinian, but it is not one of his terms.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive domain belongs to Benjamin Bloom's (1956) taxonomy of educational objectives (knowledge, comprehension, application and so on). It is an education term, not part of Lewin's field theory.", "b": "Social domain is a general phrase for the area of social life or social development. It is not a technical term in Lewin's field theory.", "c": "Emotional field borrows the word field from field theory but is not a Lewin term. His concept for the total psychological situation, feelings and goals included, is the life space."}}, {"q": "H.J. Eysenck's personality theory suggests that personality can be described along three major dimensions: extraversion-introversion, neuroticism-stability, and psychoticism. What is the term used to describe the underlying biological basis for these personality traits?", "concept": "The answer is b: Hans Eysenck (1916 to 1997) treated his three dimensions as temperaments, broad, biologically based styles of behaviour that are largely inherited. Eysenck used factor analysis to find a few superfactors, or types, at the top of a hierarchy that runs from specific responses to habitual responses to traits to types. He then asked why these dimensions exist, and in The Biological Basis of Personality (1967) he answered with biology. Extraversion depends on the ascending reticular activating system, which sets the level of cortical arousal: introverts have a higher baseline arousal and avoid extra stimulation, while extraverts are under-aroused and seek it. Neuroticism depends on the reactivity of the limbic system, the visceral brain, which governs emotional responses. Psychoticism, developed with Sybil Eysenck (1976), was tentatively linked to male sex hormones. Twin studies supported a sizeable genetic share for all three. Eysenck tied his scheme to the four temperaments of Galen (2nd century CE): melancholic (unstable introvert), choleric (unstable extravert), sanguine (stable extravert) and phlegmatic (stable introvert). For example, Riya, a calm, quiet student who prefers the library to a noisy cricket stadium, falls in the phlegmatic quadrant, because on Eysenck's account her cortex is already well aroused. Option b is right because temperament is the term for the inherited, biological core that Eysenck's dimensions describe. Exam trap: traits sounds right because Eysenck is a trait theorist, but traits are a lower, descriptive level of his hierarchy, not the biological basis.", "traps": {"a": "Archetypes are Carl Jung's inherited, universal images in the collective unconscious (the shadow, the anima, the hero). They are symbolic contents of the psyche, not a biological basis for Eysenck's dimensions.", "c": "Traits are the descriptive units at the third level of Eysenck's hierarchy (specific responses, habitual responses, traits, types), such as sociability or impulsiveness. They describe behaviour; they are not the biological basis underlying it.", "d": "Biogenic factors is a general phrase meaning causes of biological origin, not a term in Eysenck's theory. His word for the inherited basis of personality was temperament."}}, {"q": "George Kelly's personal construct theory proposes that individuals create mental frameworks, known as personal constructs, to interpret and understand the world around them. What term describes the process by which individuals adjust their constructs when faced with new experiences that do not fit existing categories?", "concept": "The answer is c: accommodation is the term offered for adjusting one's constructs when new experiences do not fit the old categories. George Kelly (1955) proposed in The Psychology of Personal Constructs that every person acts like a scientist: we build personal constructs, bipolar dimensions such as friendly versus hostile or honest versus dishonest, use them to predict events, and revise them when predictions fail. His experience corollary states that a person's construction system changes as he or she construes new events, and his modulation corollary adds that how far it can change depends on how permeable the constructs are (open to new elements) rather than impermeable (rigid whatever the evidence, as in prejudice). Kelly called his stance constructive alternativism: we can always re-construe our lives. The word accommodation comes from Jean Piaget (1936), who used it for changing a schema to fit new experience, in contrast to assimilation, fitting new experience into an existing schema. For example, Arjun construes all professors as strict versus lenient; when he meets a professor who is strict about deadlines yet warm and flexible about ideas, he splits the construct into two separate ones. That revision is accommodation. Option c is right because it is the only option that names changing the framework itself to fit new experience. Exam trap: assimilation is the opposite process, keeping the construct unchanged and forcing the new experience to fit it. Key note: Kelly's own words for this are construct revision (the experience corollary) and permeability, while accommodation is Piaget's term; it is used here because it names the same adjustment, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Social comparison is Leon Festinger's (1954) theory that we judge our own abilities and opinions by comparing ourselves with other people. It is about self-evaluation, not about revising constructs.", "b": "Cognitive dissonance is Leon Festinger's (1957) tension from holding inconsistent cognitions, reduced by changing attitudes or behaviour. It is a motivational state, not Kelly's process of construct revision.", "d": "Assimilation, in Jean Piaget's terms, is fitting new experience into an existing schema without changing it. It is the opposite of what the stem describes, where the construct itself is adjusted."}}, {"q": "Personal construct theory posits that individuals have unique systems of personal constructs that shape their perceptions of reality. What term describes the tendency to interpret ambiguous information in a way that confirms existing beliefs or expectations?", "concept": "The answer is b: confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, interpret and remember information in ways that confirm what we already believe or expect. Peter Wason (1960) showed it with his 2-4-6 task. Participants were told that the triple 2-4-6 fitted a rule and had to discover the rule by proposing new triples. Most guessed 'numbers rising by two' and tested only triples that fitted their guess (8-10-12, 20-22-24), never ones that could prove it wrong. The real rule was simply 'any increasing numbers'. Raymond Nickerson (1998) reviewed the bias and found it in science, medicine, courts and daily life. It works at every step: we choose sources that agree with us, read ambiguous evidence as support, and remember hits better than misses. In George Kelly's (1955) personal construct theory, constructs channel what we notice, so a person tends to see what his or her constructs predict. For example, Meera believes her new flatmate Kavya is rude; when Kavya does not reply to a message, Meera reads it as rudeness rather than a busy day, and she forgets the times Kavya helped her. Clinicians face the same trap: once a diagnosis is suspected, they may look only for symptoms that fit it. Option b is right because interpreting ambiguous information so that it supports existing beliefs is the definition of confirmation bias. Exam trap: the self-fulfilling prophecy also starts from an expectation, but there the expectation changes behaviour and makes itself come true, while confirmation bias only distorts how we read the evidence.", "traps": {"a": "A self-fulfilling prophecy (Robert Merton, 1948) is an expectation that changes behaviour so that it comes true, as when teachers' raised expectations produced real gains in Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson's (1968) Pygmalion study. It changes reality, not just interpretation.", "c": "Social desirability bias is answering questionnaires in a way that looks good rather than truthfully, measured by Douglas Crowne and David Marlowe's (1960) scale. It is a response bias in self-report, not a way of reading ambiguous information.", "d": "The false consensus effect (Lee Ross, David Greene and Pamela House, 1977) is overestimating how many other people share our opinions and habits. It concerns beliefs about others' views, not reading evidence to confirm our beliefs."}}, {"q": "Raymond B. Cattell's 16 Personality Factors (16PF) questionnaire assesses a wide range of personality traits. Which of the following is NOT one of the primary personality factors measured by the 16PF questionnaire?", "concept": "The answer is d: agreeableness is not one of the 16PF's primary factors; it is a Big Five dimension. Raymond B. Cattell (1949) built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire by factor analysis. He began from the lexical hypothesis, that important traits are encoded in everyday words; Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936) had listed about 18,000 English trait words. Cattell reduced these to surface trait clusters and then, by factor analysis of ratings, questionnaires and objective tests, to 16 source traits. The 16 primary factors, labelled by letter, are Warmth (A), Reasoning (B), Emotional Stability (C), Dominance (E), Liveliness (F), Rule-Consciousness (G), Social Boldness (H), Sensitivity (I), Vigilance (L), Abstractedness (M), Privateness (N), Apprehension (O), Openness to Change (Q1), Self-Reliance (Q2), Perfectionism (Q3) and Tension (Q4). Factoring the primaries gives five global, second-order factors: Extraversion, Anxiety, Tough-Mindedness, Independence and Self-Control. Scores are reported in stens (1 to 10). Agreeableness is one of the Big Five dimensions, measured for example by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae's NEO Personality Inventory (1985). For example, Kabir's 16PF profile might show high Warmth and high Dominance, but it contains no agreeableness score at all. Option d is right because agreeableness belongs to a different model and is not measured by the 16PF. Exam trap: Emotional Stability sounds like a Big Five label (the reverse of neuroticism), but it and Dominance are genuine 16PF primaries (C and E). Key note: extraversion is not one of the 16 primaries either, since it is a global, second-order factor the 16PF also scores; but agreeableness is not measured by the 16PF at all, so d is the best answer and stands.", "traps": {"a": "Extraversion is not one of the 16 primaries, but the 16PF does score it as a global, second-order factor built from primaries such as Warmth, Liveliness and Social Boldness. So it is measured by the 16PF, unlike agreeableness.", "b": "Emotional Stability is primary factor C of the 16PF (calm and mature versus reactive and easily upset). It is a genuine 16PF primary.", "c": "Dominance is primary factor E of the 16PF (assertive and forceful versus deferential). It is a genuine 16PF primary."}}, {"q": "Hans Eysenck proposed that personality traits are influenced by biological factors, particularly variations in brain functioning. What term refers to the stable and enduring characteristics that define an individual's personality?", "concept": "The answer is d: a disposition is a stable, enduring tendency to think, feel and act in a characteristic way, and personality is usually described as a set of such dispositions. Trait theorists use the word for the lasting building blocks of personality. Gordon Allport (1937) treated traits as real structures inside the person and sorted them into cardinal (one ruling passion), central (the five to ten words a friend would use) and secondary (narrow preferences); he later called these individual traits personal dispositions (1961). Hans Eysenck (1967), named in the stem, argued that such dispositions rest on biology: extraversion on the arousal level of the ascending reticular activating system and neuroticism on the reactivity of the limbic system. Example: Kabir is generous with classmates, with strangers on the train and with his younger cousins, year after year; that consistency across situations and time is a disposition. Option d is right because the stem asks for the general term for the stable and enduring characteristics that define a personality, and that is exactly what disposition means. Exam trap: the stem opens with biology, which pulls you towards temperament. But temperament is the narrower, inherited, early-appearing core of emotional style (emotionality, activity and sociability in Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin's 1984 model; the easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up babies of Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, 1977), on which traits are later built. Key note: because Eysenck's dimensions are biological, some would argue that temperament (a) fits. The definition asked for, however, is the broad one covering all stable characteristics of personality, not only the inherited core, so the answer used here, d, stands.", "traps": {"a": "Temperament is the biologically based, early-appearing style of emotional reacting (Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin; Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess). It is only the inherited raw material of personality, not the general term for all its stable characteristics.", "b": "An idiosyncrasy is an odd personal habit or quirk, such as always tapping a pen twice before writing. It is peculiar and often trivial, not an enduring characteristic that defines personality.", "c": "Heritability is a population statistic: the proportion of variance in a trait within a population that is due to genetic differences (often around 40 to 50 percent for personality traits in twin studies). It describes groups, not a characteristic that a person has."}}, {"q": "The distribution of sample means approaches a normal distribution as the sample size increases, as per which theory?", "concept": "The answer is a: the central limit theorem states that as sample size grows, the distribution of sample means approaches a normal curve, whatever the shape of the population. Imagine drawing sample after sample from a population, working out each sample's mean, and plotting those means. That plot is the sampling distribution of the mean. The theorem says three things about it: its mean equals the population mean (μ); its spread, the standard error, equals the population SD divided by the square root of N; and its shape becomes normal once N is large, conventionally 30 or more. Abraham de Moivre (1733) first showed this for coin tosses, and Pierre-Simon Laplace (1810) generalised it. Example: Riya studies monthly pocket money among Pune students, which is badly skewed because a few students get very large sums. If she draws many samples of 40 students, the means of those samples still pile up in a roughly bell-shaped curve around the true mean. This is why z tests, t tests and confidence intervals can rely on the normal curve even when raw psychological scores are skewed. Option a is right because the stem restates the theorem itself: sample means become normally distributed as N increases. Exam trap: do not confuse the central limit theorem with the law of large numbers of Jacob Bernoulli (1713). The law of large numbers says a sample mean gets closer to the population mean as N grows (accuracy); the central limit theorem says the means become normally distributed (shape). Examiners also invent look-alike names from familiar words, such as 'central tendency theorem'.", "traps": {"b": "There is no 'central tendency theorem'. Central tendency is the family of averages (mean, median, mode), a descriptive idea that says nothing about the shape of a sampling distribution.", "c": "'Theory of dispersion' is not a named theorem. Dispersion is the spread of scores (range, variance, SD); the standard error is a kind of dispersion, but the rule that sample means become normal is the central limit theorem.", "d": "'All of these' fails because b and c are not real theorems; only the central limit theorem makes this claim."}}, {"q": "The probability of event A occurring given that event B has occurred is better explained by:", "concept": "The answer is a: the probability of A given that B has occurred, written P(A|B), is a conditional probability, and Bayes' theorem is the rule for working it out. Thomas Bayes (1763), an English Presbyterian minister, set out the idea in an essay that his friend Richard Price published after his death. The theorem reads P(A|B) = P(B|A) × P(A) / P(B). In words: start with the prior probability of A (how common it is before any evidence), multiply by how likely the evidence B is if A is true, and divide by how likely B is overall. The result is the posterior probability, the updated belief once B is known. Example: depression affects 1 percent of students at Arjun's college. A screening questionnaire catches 90 percent of true cases but also flags 10 percent of healthy students. Arjun screens positive. Bayes gives P(depressed | positive) = (0.90 × 0.01) / (0.90 × 0.01 + 0.10 × 0.99), about 8 percent. A positive result is far less telling than it feels, because the base rate is low; Paul Meehl and Albert Rosen (1955) made this base-rate point for psychological tests. Option a is right because Bayes' theorem is the standard tool for finding the probability of one event given another. Exam trap: students often treat P(A|B) as the same as P(B|A). They are not: the chance of a positive test if depressed (90 percent) is very different from the chance of being depressed if the test is positive (about 8 percent). Ignoring the base rate in this way is the base-rate fallacy.", "traps": {"b": "The binomial distribution gives the probability of each number of successes in N independent two-outcome trials, such as 7 heads in 10 tosses (mean Np). It assumes the trials are independent, so it does not update one event's probability in the light of another.", "c": "The central limit theorem describes the shape of the sampling distribution of the mean (normal once N is large). It concerns sample means, not the probability of one event given another.", "d": "NPC is the normal probability curve, the symmetrical bell curve derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733). It gives the probability of scores falling within a range, not a conditional probability."}}, {"q": "What is the non-parametric alternative to the independent t-test?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t test. The t test compares the means of two independent groups and assumes interval data, roughly normal distributions and similar variances. When those assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small samples or badly skewed scores, you switch to a rank test. Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947) built the U test: pool the scores of both groups, rank them from lowest to highest, then ask whether one group's ranks run consistently higher than the other's. U counts how often a score in one group beats a score in the other. It is equivalent to the rank-sum test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). Example: Meera rates the homesickness of 12 hostel students and 10 day scholars on a 1 to 5 scale. The ratings are ordinal and skewed, so she ranks all 22 together and runs Mann-Whitney instead of t. Option b is right because each parametric test has a rank-based partner, and for two independent groups that partner is the Mann-Whitney U test. Exam trap: match the design before the test. Two related samples (paired t) take the Wilcoxon signed-rank test; three or more independent groups (one-way ANOVA) take the Kruskal-Wallis H test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952); three or more related samples (repeated-measures ANOVA) take the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937). Students who remember only 'Wilcoxon' often pick the signed-rank test for independent groups, which is wrong.", "traps": {"a": "ANOVA, from Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, is itself a parametric test comparing the means of three or more groups under normality and equal-variance assumptions. Its non-parametric partner is the Kruskal-Wallis H test, so it cannot be the alternative to a t test.", "c": "MANOVA is a parametric multivariate test comparing groups on two or more dependent variables at once. It adds assumptions (multivariate normality) rather than dropping them.", "d": "The Wald-Wolfowitz runs test (Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz, 1940) can compare two independent samples by counting runs in the pooled, ordered scores, but it is a weak, general test of any difference between distributions and is mostly used to check the randomness of a sequence. It is not the standard alternative to the t test."}}, {"q": "As per Spearman, the path of science is paved with achievements of the:", "concept": "The answer is a: the line credited to Charles Spearman (1863 to 1945) says that the path of science is paved with achievements of the 'allegedly unachievable'. The point is that scientific progress usually comes from doing what experts had declared impossible. The word 'allegedly' carries the meaning: the task was only said to be unachievable, and then someone achieved it. Spearman, a British psychologist who took his doctorate in the Leipzig laboratory founded by Wilhelm Wundt (1879), is himself a good example of that spirit. In 1904, in 'General Intelligence, Objectively Determined and Measured', he showed that schoolchildren's marks in different subjects all correlate positively and explained this by a general factor, g, common to every test plus a specific factor, s, peculiar to each: the two-factor theory. In doing so he founded factor analysis and claimed to measure intelligence objectively at a time when many held that the mind could not be measured. He also gave psychology the rank-order correlation coefficient, rho (1904), and the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula (1910) for estimating reliability when a test is lengthened. Option a is right because it is the only completion that makes the sentence say something: progress is built from feats that were once called impossible. Exam trap: d ('unachievable') looks close but makes the line self-contradictory, since an achievement of the truly unachievable cannot exist; the whole trick lies in the single word 'allegedly'. Key note: the exact source of this line could not be traced, so no book or year is given for the quote itself. Of the four options only 'allegedly unachievable' gives a meaningful sentence, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "'Achievable' makes the line trivial: of course science is built from things that can be achieved. It loses the contrast between what was claimed and what was done, which is the point of the quote.", "c": "'Allegedly achievable' says the reverse of the quote: it suggests progress rests on things only claimed to be possible, which sounds like doubt rather than triumph over doubt.", "d": "'Unachievable' turns the line into a contradiction, since something that has been achieved cannot be unachievable. Without 'allegedly' there is no gap between the claim of impossibility and the achievement."}}, {"q": "MANOVA compares means of multiple dependent variables across groups. _____ does the same while taking into consideration covariates as well.", "concept": "The answer is a: MANCOVA, multivariate analysis of covariance, compares groups on two or more dependent variables at once while statistically adjusting for one or more covariates. The names are built from parts. ANOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, compares group means on one dependent variable (DV). Add 'M' for multivariate and you test several DVs together (MANOVA), which avoids the inflated Type I error of running many separate ANOVAs; its most reported statistic is Wilks' lambda. Add 'C' for covariance and you first remove the influence of a covariate, a variable related to the DV that you could not control by design, such as baseline severity, age or IQ. So: ANOVA = one DV; ANCOVA = one DV plus covariates; MANOVA = several DVs; MANCOVA = several DVs plus covariates. Example: Kabir compares CBT, mindfulness training and a waiting list on two outcomes, depression and anxiety scores after eight weeks. Because the groups started at different severity levels, he enters baseline depression as a covariate. Two DVs plus a covariate means MANCOVA. Option a is right because the stem asks for the MANOVA design that also takes covariates into account, which is exactly what the C adds. Exam trap: in all these names, M means more than one DV and C means a covariate. ANCOVA has the covariate but only one DV, so it is the near-miss. The assumption the covariance designs add is homogeneity of regression slopes: the covariate must relate to the DV in the same way in every group.", "traps": {"b": "ANOVA (Ronald Fisher) compares group means on a single DV with no covariate. It drops both the multivariate part and the covariate, so it does less than MANOVA, not more.", "c": "ANCOVA does adjust for covariates, but on only one DV. The stem wants the multivariate version that handles several DVs at once.", "d": "'None of these' fails because MANCOVA is a standard named technique that fits the description exactly."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a qualitative data analysis software?", "concept": "The answer is d: ATLAS.ti, NVivo and MAXQDA are all qualitative data analysis programs, members of the family called CAQDAS (computer-assisted qualitative data analysis software). Qualitative research produces words and images rather than numbers: interview transcripts, field notes, open-ended answers, videos. These programs let the researcher import that material, attach codes to passages, retrieve every passage with the same code, write memos, link codes into categories and draw maps of how themes relate. ATLAS.ti grew out of a project at the Technical University of Berlin; NVivo comes from QSR International in Australia and succeeded its older program NUD*IST; MAXQDA is made by VERBI Software in Berlin. Example: Riya has 25 interviews with caregivers of people with dementia. In NVivo she tags every mention of guilt, then pulls up all the guilt passages side by side to build a theme. Option d is right because each of the three named programs is a standard qualitative analysis package. Exam trap: the software only organises the data; the coding decisions and the interpretation remain the researcher's work. Do not confuse these programs with statistical packages such as SPSS, R, SAS or Stata, which analyse numbers; in a 'Which is not qualitative software?' question, SPSS is the usual answer.", "traps": {"a": "ATLAS.ti is a qualitative analysis package, but choosing it alone ignores that NVivo and MAXQDA are qualitative packages too.", "b": "NVivo is probably the most widely used qualitative package, but it is not the only one listed; ATLAS.ti and MAXQDA do the same job.", "c": "MAXQDA is a qualitative and mixed-methods package, but picking it alone leaves out ATLAS.ti and NVivo, which qualify equally."}}, {"q": "As per Miles and Huberman, data analysis involves:", "concept": "The answer is d: for Matthew Miles and A. Michael Huberman (1984; 2nd edition 1994), qualitative analysis involves coding, categorising and abstracting, because all three are part of what they call data reduction. In Qualitative Data Analysis they describe analysis as three concurrent flows of activity. Data reduction is 'the process of selecting, focusing, simplifying, abstracting, and transforming' the raw field notes and transcripts: you code passages, write summaries, cluster codes into categories and pull out abstract themes. Data display organises the reduced data into matrices, charts and networks so that patterns can be seen at a glance. Conclusion drawing and verification notes regularities and explanations and then tests them against the data. The three run back and forth throughout a study rather than in a fixed sequence. Example: Arjun interviews 15 first-generation college students. He codes 'fear of letting parents down' in many transcripts (coding), groups it with related codes under 'family obligation' (categorising), and lifts that into the broader idea of 'carrying the family's hopes' (abstracting). He then lays the themes out in a matrix by gender (display) and checks his hunch against cases that do not fit (verification). Option d is right because coding, categorising and abstracting are all parts of data reduction in their model. Exam trap: students remember the three headings (reduction, display, conclusion drawing) and then fail to see that coding, categorising and abstracting are activities inside the first one. A third edition (2014) added Johnny Saldana as co-author.", "traps": {"a": "Coding, tagging chunks of text with labels, is the core of data reduction, but it is only one of the listed activities; categorising and abstracting are also part of Miles and Huberman's analysis.", "b": "Abstracting appears in their own definition of data reduction, but choosing it alone leaves out coding and categorising, which belong to the same stage.", "c": "Categorising (clustering codes into broader groups) is part of data reduction too, so it is one correct element, not the whole answer."}}, {"q": "The most common criticism faced by qualitative research is its:", "concept": "The answer is d: low external validity, limited generalisability and subjectivity are all standard criticisms of qualitative research. Qualitative studies explore meaning and experience in depth, usually through interviews, observation or documents, with small purposive samples chosen because they are information-rich, not because they represent a population. That design brings two linked charges. First, findings from 8 or 10 participants in one setting may not hold elsewhere: this is low external validity, which is the same idea as limited generalisability under another name. Second, the researcher is the instrument who collects, codes and interprets the data, so personal values and expectations can shape the findings, another researcher might read the same transcripts differently, and replication is hard. Qualitative researchers reply that their aim is depth, not statistical generalisation. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) replaced the experimental criteria with trustworthiness: credibility, transferability (the reader judges the fit to another setting from thick description), dependability and confirmability, supported by reflexivity, member checking and triangulation. Example: Meera interviews nine first-time mothers with postnatal depression in one Chennai hospital. Her account is rich, but critics ask whether it applies to mothers elsewhere and whether her own views coloured her coding. Option d is right because each listed criticism is a standard one, and a and b are two names for the same problem. Exam trap: when two options say the same thing in different words, as a and b do here, neither can be the single correct answer, which points to 'All of these'.", "traps": {"a": "Low external validity is a real criticism, but it is only part of the answer; limited generalisability (the same problem in other words) and subjectivity are criticised just as often.", "b": "Limited generalisability is the same concern as low external validity, so it cannot be the only answer; subjectivity is also a standard criticism.", "c": "Subjectivity (researcher bias in collecting and interpreting data) is a central criticism, but the small, non-representative samples that limit generalisation are criticised equally."}}, {"q": "If a researcher collects data along a straight line within a particular habitat, the sampling strategy involved is:", "concept": "The answer is c: collecting data along a straight line laid across a habitat is line transect sampling. A transect is a line or path across an area. The researcher marks the line, walks it, and records the organisms seen on or near it, usually noting each one's distance from the line. In a belt (strip) transect only individuals within a fixed width on each side are counted; in distance sampling the drop-off in sightings with distance is used to estimate population density for the whole area. The method comes from ecology and wildlife surveys (birds, deer, plants along a hillside) because it covers a large area cheaply and shows how species change from one zone to the next, such as from a riverbank to dry scrub. Social researchers borrow the idea in the 'transect walk' of participatory rural appraisal, where a team walks across a village with residents and notes land use, water points and housing along the way. Example: Kabir lays three 1 km lines across a grassland reserve in Gujarat, counts every blackbuck he sees and records its distance from the line, to estimate how many live in the reserve. Option c is right because the stem's two clues, a straight line and a habitat, define a transect. Exam trap: 'line sampling' is a vague near-miss that sounds right but drops the defining word; the standard name of the method is line transect sampling. Area sampling, the other look-alike, divides a region into geographic clusters rather than following a line.", "traps": {"a": "'Line sampling' sounds right but is not the standard name for this habitat survey; it drops the defining term transect, which is what examiners and ecology texts use.", "b": "Area sampling is a form of cluster sampling in which a region is divided into geographic units (blocks, villages, quadrats) and a random set of whole areas is surveyed. It samples areas, not observations along a line.", "d": "Expert sampling is a kind of purposive (non-probability) sampling in which people with special knowledge are chosen, such as senior clinicians for a Delphi panel. It concerns choosing informants, not where in a habitat to collect data."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a style of report writing?", "concept": "The answer is d: APA, ASA and CMS are all style guides used in writing research reports. A style guide sets the rules for how a report is laid out and, above all, how sources are cited in the text and listed at the end. APA style comes from the American Psychological Association's Publication Manual, now in its 7th edition (2020). It uses author-date citation, for example (Milgram, 1963), and is the standard in psychology, so it governs most dissertations and journal articles in the field. ASA style is the American Sociological Association's guide, also author-date, used in sociology. CMS is The Chicago Manual of Style from the University of Chicago Press, first published in 1906, which offers two systems: notes and bibliography (footnotes, common in history) and author-date. Other common styles are MLA (Modern Language Association, humanities), Harvard (a generic author-date style) and Vancouver (numbered references, medicine). Example: Riya writes her M.Phil dissertation in APA style, but when she sends a chapter to a history journal she must reformat the references in Chicago notes and bibliography. Option d is right because each abbreviation names a recognised style of report writing and citation. Exam trap: ASA is easy to misread as the American Statistical Association, and CMS as something technical; here ASA means the American Sociological Association and CMS the Chicago Manual of Style, both genuine style guides.", "traps": {"a": "APA style is the one psychologists use most, but it is not the only style listed; ASA and CMS are recognised styles too.", "b": "ASA style (American Sociological Association) is a genuine author-date style, but choosing it alone ignores APA and CMS.", "c": "CMS (The Chicago Manual of Style) is a genuine style guide with notes-bibliography and author-date systems, but APA and ASA also qualify."}}, {"q": "The concept of keeping participants, researchers or both unaware about what has been assigned to the treatment group is called:", "concept": "The answer is a: keeping participants, researchers or both unaware of who has been assigned to the treatment group is called blinding (or masking). Its purpose is to stop expectations from biasing the results. Participants who know they got the real treatment may improve simply because they expect to (the placebo effect), and researchers who know may treat or score the groups differently without meaning to (experimenter expectancy effects). Robert Rosenthal and Kermit Fode (1963) showed the second: students told their rats were 'maze-bright' recorded better maze learning than students told theirs were 'maze-dull', though the rats were alike. Levels of blinding: in a single-blind study the participants do not know their condition; in a double-blind study neither the participants nor the researchers who deal with them know; in a triple-blind study the data analysts are kept unaware as well. Example: in Kabir's trial of a new anxiety app against a sham app, the students do not know which app they have, and the psychologist who rates their anxiety at eight weeks does not know either. That is double-blind. In psychotherapy trials participants usually know which therapy they receive, so often only the outcome assessor can be blind. Option a is right because the stem gives the exact definition of blinding. Exam trap: do not confuse blinding with allocation concealment. Allocation concealment hides the random sequence before and during enrolment, so the recruiter cannot steer patients into a group; blinding hides the assigned condition after allocation.", "traps": {"b": "Confounding is the presence of an extraneous variable that varies along with the independent variable, so you cannot tell which caused the effect. Blinding helps prevent one kind of confound (expectancy), but confounding is the problem, not the technique.", "c": "A placebo is an inert treatment (a sugar pill, a sham app) given to a control group; Henry Beecher (1955) drew attention to its power. A placebo needs blinding to work, since it fails if people know it is fake, but it is not the act of keeping people unaware.", "d": "'None of these' fails because blinding is the exact, standard term for the concept described."}}, {"q": "In which of the following studies is a group of subjects followed over time?", "concept": "The answer is b: in a cohort study a group of people (a cohort) is identified and then followed over time to see what happens to them. Typically the cohort is free of the outcome at the start; members are classified as exposed or unexposed to some factor and tracked forward to see who develops the outcome. This gives incidence (new cases) and the relative risk, and it places the cause before the effect in time. A prospective cohort runs forward from now; a retrospective (historical) cohort uses old records to follow people from a past starting point. Classic examples are the Framingham Heart Study, which has followed residents of a Massachusetts town since 1948; the British Doctors Study of Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill, begun in 1951 to track smoking and death; and the Dunedin study in New Zealand, which has followed about 1,000 children born in 1972 to 1973. Example: Riya records the screen time of 800 Class 9 students in 2024 and follows them for five years to see who develops depression. Option b is right because following a group of subjects over time is the defining feature of a cohort study. Exam trap: the case-control study looks the other way. It starts with people who already have the outcome (cases) and similar people who do not (controls), then looks back at their past exposures, as in Doll and Hill's 1950 study of lung cancer patients. Case-control is backward-looking and gives odds ratios; cohort is forward-looking and gives relative risk.", "traps": {"a": "A case-control study begins with people who already have the outcome and matched controls who do not, and looks back at past exposure (Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill, 1950, smoking and lung cancer). It reconstructs the past rather than following subjects forward.", "c": "A cross-sectional study measures different people once, at a single point in time, like a snapshot of prevalence or of several age groups. Nobody is followed, and age differences are confounded with cohort (generation) effects.", "d": "'None of these' fails because the cohort study is the standard design defined by following a group over time."}}, {"q": "The overall score that is obtained from various subtests of Wechsler's test is called:", "concept": "The answer is b: on the Wechsler scales the overall score combined from the subtests is the Full Scale IQ (FSIQ). David Wechsler (1939) built the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale, and later the WAIS (1955), WISC and WPPSI, because he wanted a profile of abilities as well as a single total. Each subtest gives a scaled score (mean 10, SD 3). On the WAIS-IV (2008), 10 core subtests form four index scores: Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed. The four indices together yield the Full Scale IQ, the best single estimate of general intellectual ability. Older editions combined a Verbal IQ and a Performance IQ into the FSIQ; the WISC-V (2014) uses five primary indices. Index scores and the FSIQ are reported as deviation IQs, with mean 100 and SD 15. Example: Arjun's index scores range from 85 on Working Memory to 112 on Verbal Comprehension. His FSIQ falls in the average range, but the gap between the two indices tells the clinician more than the total does. Option b is right because Full Scale IQ is Wechsler's own name for the overall composite. Exam trap: deviation IQ is tempting because the FSIQ is expressed as one. But deviation IQ is the scoring method, a standard score comparing a person with others of the same age, not the name of the total; every Wechsler index is a deviation score too.", "traps": {"a": "'Large scale IQ' is not a term in any intelligence test; it is a made-up echo of 'full scale'.", "c": "'Composite IQ' is a generic description: index scores are composites too, and some other batteries use the word. It is not Wechsler's name for the overall score.", "d": "Deviation IQ is the scoring method Wechsler introduced (mean 100, SD 15, comparing a person with their own age group), replacing the old ratio IQ. The FSIQ is reported as a deviation IQ, but the overall score is called the Full Scale IQ."}}, {"q": "Emotional quotient is measured as:", "concept": "The answer is c: on the textbook ratio formula, emotional quotient (EQ) = emotional age (EA) divided by chronological age (A) × 100. The formula copies the old ratio IQ. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age to get an intelligence quotient, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied by 100 in the Stanford-Binet to remove decimals: IQ = MA/CA × 100. Other age-based quotients follow the same pattern: developmental quotient = developmental age/CA × 100, and the social quotient of Edgar Doll's Vineland Social Maturity Scale (1935) = social age/CA × 100. For EQ, emotional age is the age level of emotional maturity a person shows. Example: Meera is 10 years old, and her emotional maturity matches that of a typical 12-year-old. EQ = 12/10 × 100 = 120, above average. Emotional intelligence itself was defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) as the ability to perceive, use, understand and manage emotions; Daniel Goleman's Emotional Intelligence (1995) popularised it, and Reuven Bar-On's Emotional Quotient Inventory (EQ-i, 1997) made the label EQ common. Option c is right because it places emotional age over chronological age, which is how every ratio quotient is built. Exam trap: option a is the ratio IQ (mental age, not emotional age), and option b is the same ratio turned upside down. Key note: modern EI tests such as the EQ-i and the MSCEIT report deviation-style standard scores (mean 100, SD 15) rather than an age ratio, so no current test computes EQ this way. The ratio formula is the textbook definition the question asks for, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "MA/A × 100 is the ratio IQ of William Stern and Lewis Terman: mental age over chronological age. It measures intellectual, not emotional, level.", "b": "A/MA × 100 inverts the ratio IQ, putting chronological age on top, so it would fall as ability rose; it is not any recognised quotient.", "d": "'None of these' fails because c gives the standard textbook EQ formula."}}, {"q": "The book \"Hereditary Genius\" is authored by:", "concept": "The answer is a: Hereditary Genius (1869) was written by Francis Galton (1822 to 1911), the English polymath who founded the study of individual differences. Galton traced eminence through the families of judges, statesmen, commanders, scientists, poets and others, and found that eminent men had far more eminent relatives than chance would predict. He concluded that ability is inherited, largely discounting the advantages of wealth, schooling and connections that such families also shared. The book launched his lifelong work on heredity: he popularised the phrase 'nature and nurture' (1874), coined the word eugenics (1883), ran an anthropometric laboratory in London (1884), developed correlation and regression towards the mean, pioneered twin studies and the questionnaire, and tried word association on himself (1879). Option a is right because Galton is the author of Hereditary Genius. Exam trap: Charles Darwin is the tempting wrong answer, because the book applies his ideas on variation and heredity to human ability and because Galton was his half-cousin. Darwin's own books were On the Origin of Species (1859) and The Descent of Man (1871); Galton wrote Hereditary Genius after reading the Origin.", "traps": {"b": "Charles Darwin (1809 to 1882), Galton's half-cousin, wrote On the Origin of Species (1859) and The Descent of Man (1871). Galton's book was inspired by Darwin's theory but written by Galton.", "c": "Gregor Mendel (1866) worked out the laws of inheritance from pea-plant crosses; his work was ignored until 1900 and he wrote nothing on human genius.", "d": "Ernst Heinrich Weber (1834) studied touch and the just noticeable difference, giving Weber's law in psychophysics; he did not study the inheritance of ability."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a correct statement?", "concept": "The answer is a: of the four statements, the one meant as correct is that Alexander Luria had nothing to do with the Luria-Nebraska Battery. Alexander Romanovich Luria (1902 to 1977) was a Soviet neuropsychologist. In Higher Cortical Functions in Man (1962; English 1966) he described a flexible, qualitative examination: instead of comparing scores with norms, he watched exactly how and where a patient's performance broke down. He also grouped brain activity into three functional units (arousal; receiving and storing information; planning and checking action). Anne-Lise Christensen (1975) gathered his test procedures into a kit, Luria's Neuropsychological Investigation. Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch in Nebraska then turned those items into a standardised, scored battery (1978; manual 1980): Form I has 269 items in 11 clinical scales, from Motor and Rhythm to Memory and Intellectual Processes, and Form II adds a 12th, Intermediate Memory. Luria died in 1977, before the battery appeared; he neither built nor endorsed it, and his own method was the opposite of a fixed, normed battery. Option a is right because Luria lent the battery only his name and his test ideas. Exam trap: the name makes b look obvious, but a battery named after a person need not be built by that person. Option c fails because the battery has 11 or 12 clinical scales, not three dimensions. Key note: 'nothing to do with' overstates the case, because the items come from Luria's examination. But he took no part in developing or standardising the battery, which is the point the statement makes, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Luria was not the battery's proponent: it was standardised by Charles Golden, Thomas Hammeke and Arnold Purisch in Nebraska after Luria's death, from Anne-Lise Christensen's collection of his procedures.", "c": "The Luria-Nebraska Battery has 11 clinical scales in Form I (12 in Form II) plus summary and localisation scales. 'Three' may echo Luria's three functional units of the brain, which are a theory, not the battery's structure.", "d": "'None of these' fails because statement a, read as 'Luria did not develop the battery', is true."}}, {"q": "The original intent of Halstead's test was to describe the function of which part of the brain?", "concept": "The answer is d: Ward Halstead (1947) designed his tests to describe the functions of the frontal lobes. Working at the University of Chicago from the 1930s, Halstead set out to measure what he called 'biological intelligence', a brain-based capacity distinct from the school-type intelligence that IQ tests sample. He believed it depended mainly on the frontal lobes, and his book Brain and Intelligence: A Quantitative Study of the Frontal Lobes (1947) reported tests given to patients with frontal damage, patients with other brain damage and healthy controls. His tests included the Category Test (abstract reasoning and concept formation), the Tactual Performance Test (fitting blocks into a form board while blindfolded) and Finger Tapping. His student Ralph Reitan (1922 to 2014) found that the tests were sensitive to brain damage wherever it lay, not only in the frontal lobes, and turned them into the Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery for detecting and localising brain dysfunction, scored with a Halstead Impairment Index (0.5 or more suggests impairment). Example: Kabir, recovering from a frontal head injury, may still do well on vocabulary yet struggle to find the rule on the Category Test, the kind of loss Halstead wanted to capture. Option d is right because the original purpose was a quantitative study of frontal lobe function, as his book's title says. Exam trap: the finished battery is a general screen for brain damage, so students assume Halstead aimed at the whole brain or at memory structures; his original target was the frontal lobes.", "traps": {"a": "The occipital lobe processes vision (primary visual cortex); Halstead's tests were not designed to study visual function.", "b": "The temporal lobe handles hearing, language comprehension and memory. Some Halstead-Reitan tests (Seashore Rhythm, Speech-Sounds Perception) tap auditory skills, but these were not Halstead's original target.", "c": "The hippocampus consolidates new long-term memories (patient H.M., William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957). It lies inside the temporal lobe, and Halstead's work was not about memory."}}, {"q": "The Halstead-Reitan Battery encompasses:", "concept": "The answer is d: the Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes the Speech-Sounds Perception Test, the Aphasia Screening Test and the Seashore Rhythm Test. The battery is named after Ward Halstead and Ralph Reitan (1922 to 2014): it began with Halstead's tests of 1947 and was expanded and validated by Reitan, his student, as a fixed battery to detect brain damage, judge its severity and suggest where it lies. Its core tests are the Category Test (abstract reasoning, learning from feedback); the Tactual Performance Test (blindfolded form board, giving time, memory and location scores); the Seashore Rhythm Test (are two rhythmic patterns the same or different?), adapted from the musical talent tests of Carl Seashore (1919) and tapping sustained auditory attention; the Speech-Sounds Perception Test (picking the spoken nonsense word from written choices); and the Finger Tapping Test (motor speed of each hand). Allied tests are the Trail Making Test, Parts A and B; the Reitan-Indiana Aphasia Screening Test, adapted from the screening test of Ward Halstead and Joseph Wepman (1949), which checks naming, reading, writing, spelling, arithmetic and copying shapes; a sensory-perceptual examination; and grip strength. Example: if Riya, after a left-hemisphere stroke, fails items on the Aphasia Screening Test and taps more slowly with her right hand, the pattern points to left-sided damage. Option d is right because each of the three named tests belongs to the battery. Exam trap: the Speech-Sounds Perception Test is often printed as 'Speech Perception Test', and the Aphasia Screening Test is an allied test, so students wrongly rule them out; the Rorschach, by contrast, is never part of the battery.", "traps": {"a": "The Speech-Sounds Perception Test (printed here as 'Speech Perception Test') is a core Halstead-Reitan test, but choosing it alone ignores the other two listed tests, which also belong to the battery.", "b": "The Reitan-Indiana Aphasia Screening Test is a standard allied part of the battery, but it is not the only one named; the rhythm and speech-sounds tests belong too.", "c": "The Seashore Rhythm Test is a core Halstead-Reitan test of auditory attention, but picking it alone leaves out the other two listed tests."}}, {"q": "How many cards in Rorschach's test are black and white?", "concept": "The answer is a: five of the ten Rorschach cards are black and white (achromatic): Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII. Hermann Rorschach (1921), a Swiss psychiatrist, published the test, a set of ten symmetrical inkblots, in Psychodiagnostik; he died the next year, aged 37. The cards are always shown in the same order, I to X. Of the other five, two (II and III) are black and grey with patches of bright red, and three (VIII, IX and X) are fully coloured in pastels. Hence the textbook phrase 'five black-and-white and five coloured'. Colour matters in scoring, because responses that use it (such as 'blood, because it is red') are read as signs of emotional responsiveness. Card V is the one most often seen as a bat or butterfly. Today records are usually scored with John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) or its successor, R-PAS (2011). Option a is right because Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII are the achromatic ones. Exam trap: 6 and 4 come from miscounting Cards II and III, which are mostly black but carry red, so they count as chromatic; 3 is the number of fully coloured cards, not the black-and-white ones.", "traps": {"b": "Three is the number of fully coloured cards (VIII, IX and X), not the black-and-white ones.", "c": "Six would mean counting one of the black-and-red cards (II or III) as achromatic; their red patches make them chromatic.", "d": "Four undercounts the achromatic set; Cards I, IV, V, VI and VII are all black and grey, which makes five."}}, {"q": "The Word Association Test is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the word association test is a projective technique. The examiner reads a list of stimulus words, one at a time, and the person replies with the first word that comes to mind. Because the stimulus is open and the reply unplanned, the answer is assumed to reveal inner associations the person would not disclose on a questionnaire. Francis Galton (1879) first tried the method on himself. Carl Gustav Jung (1904 to 1910) made it a clinical tool with 100 stimulus words, timing every reply and treating 'complex indicators' (a long delay, no reply, repeating the stimulus word, an odd or very personal answer) as signs of an emotionally charged complex. Grace Kent and Aaron Rosanoff (1910) published a 100-word list with frequency norms from 1,000 normal adults, so a reply could be scored as common or unusual. Gardner Lindzey (1959) sorted projective tests into five families by the response they demand (association, construction, completion, choice or ordering, expression), and the word association test, with the Rorschach, is the classic association technique. Example: when the examiner says 'mother', Meera pauses for eight seconds and then says 'gone'; the delay and the reply point to a painful complex worth exploring. Option c is right because the test uses an ambiguous stimulus, an open response and a disguised purpose, the marks of a projective method. Exam trap: because its stimuli are plain words rather than inkblots or pictures, some papers treat the word association test as 'not projective'. By Lindzey's standard classification it is projective, and it is usually called the oldest projective technique.", "traps": {"a": "Objective tests, such as the MMPI or the 16PF, ask structured questions with fixed answer choices scored by set rules. The word association test allows any reply and is interpreted clinically, so it is not objective.", "b": "'Subjective' is not a category of psychological test; it describes judgement-based scoring in general, not a type of technique.", "d": "'None of these' fails because the word association test is a recognised projective technique in Lindzey's association family."}}, {"q": "In the case of projective techniques, a therapist is more interested in exploring the _____ mind.", "concept": "The answer is c: projective techniques aim to explore the unconscious mind. Their logic is the projective hypothesis: when a person faces an ambiguous, unstructured stimulus (an inkblot, a vague picture, half a sentence), the stimulus supplies so little structure that whatever structure appears in the response must come from the person. Needs, conflicts, wishes and fears that the person cannot or will not report are 'projected' into the answer, usually without awareness. The idea draws on the topographic model of Sigmund Freud (1900), which divides the mind into the conscious (what we are aware of now), the preconscious (what can be recalled with effort) and the unconscious (wishes and memories kept out of awareness that still drive behaviour). For Freud, projection was a defence that attributes one's own unacceptable impulse to someone else; testing borrowed a broader sense. Lawrence Frank (1939) coined the term 'projective methods' and set out the hypothesis behind them. Example: shown the TAT card of a boy looking at a violin, Arjun tells a story about a father who smashes it in anger, and the clinician asks whether the punishing parent comes from Arjun's own life. Option c is right because projectives were designed to bypass conscious control and reach unconscious material. Exam trap: 'subconscious' looks like a synonym, but it is a lay word that Freud rejected because it blurs the preconscious and the unconscious; the technical term is unconscious.", "traps": {"a": "Conscious material, what a person knows and can report, is what objective self-report inventories such as the MMPI tap; projectives are designed to get past it.", "b": "'Subconscious' is a popular word, not a term of psychoanalytic theory; Sigmund Freud avoided it because it blurs the preconscious and the unconscious.", "d": "'All of these' fails because the whole purpose of a projective test is to bypass conscious control, not to explore every level of the mind equally."}}, {"q": "Which edition of ICD is currently in use by therapists?", "concept": "The answer is d: the edition of the International Classification of Diseases currently in use is the 11th, ICD-11. The ICD is the World Health Organization's (WHO) classification of all diseases and health conditions, used for diagnosis, health statistics and insurance worldwide. ICD-6 (1948) was the first to include a section on mental disorders. ICD-10 was endorsed by the World Health Assembly in 1990 and came into use from 1994, with mental disorders in Chapter V (F codes, such as F20 for schizophrenia) and its 'blue book' of Clinical Descriptions and Diagnostic Guidelines (1992). ICD-11 was adopted in May 2019 and took effect on 1 January 2022. Mental disorders now form Chapter 6, 'Mental, behavioural or neurodevelopmental disorders', with codes such as 6A20 for schizophrenia. Changes include new diagnoses such as complex PTSD, prolonged grief disorder and gaming disorder, a dimensional, severity-based model of personality disorder, and the removal of the schizophrenia subtypes. Example: Kabir, a clinical psychologist in Delhi, still sees F32 written in some hospital files, but the WHO's current code for a single depressive episode lies in the 6A70 block. Option d is right because ICD-11 has been the WHO's official edition since 2022. Exam trap: the 10th edition tempts because it was in use for nearly three decades and still fills Indian case records and textbooks. Key note: many hospitals, clinics and insurers, including in India, still code with ICD-10 during the change-over, which is why 10th is tempting. But 'currently in use' means the WHO's current edition, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "ICD-7 (the 1955 revision) made only small changes to ICD-6 and was replaced decades ago; it is of historical interest only.", "b": "ICD-10 (endorsed 1990, in use from 1994) was the standard for nearly three decades and is still common in Indian records, but the WHO replaced it with ICD-11 on 1 January 2022.", "c": "ICD-6 (1948) matters as the first edition to include mental disorders, which is why it appears in exam questions, but it is long obsolete."}}, {"q": "How many personality disorders are mentioned in DSM-5?", "concept": "The answer is a: DSM-5 (2013) lists ten specific personality disorders. A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, stays stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-5 sorts the ten into three clusters by descriptive similarity. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid and schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A memory aid is 'weird, wild and worried', with counts of 3, 4 and 3. DSM-5 also has personality change due to another medical condition and other specified and unspecified personality disorder, but these are not specific types. DSM-5-TR (2022) keeps the same ten, and DSM-IV also had ten. Example: Kabir is so preoccupied with orderliness, perfectionism and control that he misses every deadline; he fits obsessive-compulsive personality disorder in Cluster C. Option a is right because 3 + 4 + 3 gives ten specific disorders in the main (Section II) DSM-5 classification. Exam trap: the number 6 comes from the Alternative DSM-5 Model for Personality Disorders in Section III, which keeps only six specific types (antisocial, avoidant, borderline, narcissistic, obsessive-compulsive and schizotypal) and rates everyone else by level of personality functioning and pathological traits. ICD-11 goes further and drops the types altogether, using a single personality disorder graded mild, moderate or severe, with trait domain qualifiers.", "traps": {"b": "Eight undercounts the list: it is what you get if you forget two of the ten, for example schizotypal (Cluster A) and narcissistic (Cluster B); DSM-5 names ten.", "c": "Nine is the usual slip of dropping one disorder, often schizotypal, which DSM-5 also lists in the schizophrenia spectrum chapter; it is still counted among the ten personality disorders.", "d": "Six is the number of specific types kept in the Section III Alternative Model for Personality Disorders (antisocial, avoidant, borderline, narcissistic, obsessive-compulsive, schizotypal), not the official Section II count."}}, {"q": "Which of Mendel's laws is generally considered universally acceptable and without any exception?", "concept": "The answer is a: the law of segregation is the one Mendelian law treated as universal, with no standard exception. Gregor Mendel (1866), a monk who crossed pea plants at the abbey in Brno, published 'Experiments on Plant Hybridization'; it was ignored until Hugo de Vries, Carl Correns and Erich von Tschermak rediscovered it in 1900. Three rules come from his crosses. Law of dominance: in a heterozygote one allele (dominant) masks the other (recessive). Law of segregation: the two alleles of a gene separate when gametes form, so each gamete carries only one; the alleles never blend or contaminate each other, which is why it is also called the law of purity of gametes. Law of independent assortment: alleles of different genes sort into gametes independently of each other. Segregation follows directly from meiosis, where the two members of each chromosome pair are pulled apart, so it applies to every sexually reproducing diploid organism. Example: Riya's parents are both Tt for a trait; each egg or sperm carries either T or t, never both, which is how a tt child can appear from two Tt parents. Option a is right because segregation is the only law with no textbook exception, while the other two break in well-known cases. Exam trap: dominance is the law students meet first, so it feels the most basic, but incomplete dominance (red x white snapdragons give pink) and codominance (blood group AB) break it. Key note: strictly, rare events can upset it (nondisjunction can put both alleles into one gamete, and meiotic drive lets one allele reach more than half of the gametes), but textbooks treat segregation as the universal law, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "The law of dominance has clear exceptions: incomplete dominance (a red x white cross in snapdragons or four o'clock flowers gives pink) and codominance (both A and B antigens expressed in blood group AB).", "c": "The law of independent assortment holds only for genes on different chromosomes or far apart on one; linked genes travel together, as William Bateson and Reginald Punnett found in sweet peas (1905) and Thomas Hunt Morgan showed in fruit flies (1910 to 1911).", "d": "None of these fails because one law does hold universally: segregation is built into meiosis itself."}}, {"q": "Watson and Crick proposed:", "concept": "The answer is d: Watson and Crick proposed all three: specific complementary base pairing, mutation through tautomeric shifts and semi-conservative replication. James Watson and Francis Crick (1953) published the double-helix model of DNA in Nature on 25 April 1953. They built on X-ray data from Rosalind Franklin (her Photo 51, 1952) and Maurice Wilkins (1953), and on Erwin Chargaff's (1950) finding that the amount of adenine equals thymine and guanine equals cytosine. Their model has two antiparallel sugar-phosphate strands with the bases inside, held by hydrogen bonds in fixed pairs: adenine with thymine, guanine with cytosine. That is the point of option a, loosely worded: only complementary bases may pair. The paper ended: 'It has not escaped our notice that the specific pairing we have postulated immediately suggests a possible copying mechanism for the genetic material.' Their second Nature paper (30 May 1953) spelled this out: the strands unwind and each serves as a template for a new partner, so every daughter molecule keeps one old strand and one new strand (option c). The same paper suggested that spontaneous mutations arise when a base briefly takes a rare tautomeric form (a shifted hydrogen atom) and mispairs, for example adenine pairing with cytosine (option b). Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl (1958) later proved semi-conservative replication by growing bacteria on heavy nitrogen (15N) and then light nitrogen. Example: when Arjun draws a replication fork with each old strand paired to a new one, he is drawing the Watson and Crick proposal. Option d is right because each of the three statements comes from their two 1953 papers. Exam trap: students credit semi-conservative replication to Meselson and Stahl; they proved it, but Watson and Crick proposed it.", "traps": {"a": "Complementary base pairing (A with T, G with C) is part of their proposal, but choosing it alone leaves out the tautomeric mutation idea and semi-conservative replication.", "b": "Mutation by tautomeric shift was proposed in their second 1953 paper, but it is only one of the three ideas listed, all of which are theirs.", "c": "Semi-conservative replication was their proposal (proved by Matthew Meselson and Franklin Stahl in 1958), but picking it alone ignores base pairing and tautomeric mutation."}}, {"q": "In the human genome, chromosome _____ has the most genes and _____ the least.", "concept": "The answer is a: chromosome 1 has the most genes and the Y chromosome the fewest. A human body cell has 23 pairs of chromosomes: 22 pairs of autosomes, numbered roughly from largest (1) to smallest, plus the sex chromosomes, XX in females and XY in males. The whole genome holds about 20,000 protein-coding genes, a figure firmed up after the Human Genome Project was completed in 2003. Chromosome 1 is the largest, about 8 percent of all human DNA, and carries roughly 2,000 protein-coding genes, more than any other chromosome. The Y chromosome is small, much of it repetitive DNA, and carries only a few dozen protein-coding genes. The best known is SRY (the sex-determining region of the Y), which switches on testis development in the embryo. Over evolution the Y lost most of the genes it once shared with the X, because along most of its length it does not swap segments with a partner chromosome, so damaged genes are not repaired by recombination. Option a is right because it names the largest, gene-richest chromosome (1) first and the gene-poorest (Y) second, matching the order of the blanks. Exam trap: X and Y form a pair, so students assume they are alike, but the X is a large chromosome with about 800 protein-coding genes, while the Y has only a few dozen.", "traps": {"b": "Chromosome 2 is the second largest, not the most gene-rich, and the X carries about 800 protein-coding genes, far more than the Y, so it cannot be the gene-poorest.", "c": "This reverses the pair: the Y has the fewest genes, not the most, and chromosome 1 has the most.", "d": "Y is right for the fewest genes, but chromosome 2, though second largest, carries fewer genes than chromosome 1."}}, {"q": "The actual structure of t-RNA looks like:", "concept": "The answer is c: in its real three-dimensional form, transfer RNA (tRNA) is folded into an inverted L shape. tRNA is the adaptor molecule of protein synthesis: one end carries a specific amino acid, the other end reads a codon on messenger RNA at the ribosome. Each tRNA is a single strand, usually about 76 to 90 nucleotides long, that base-pairs with itself. Drawn flat, this pairing gives the cloverleaf model, worked out by Robert Holley (1965) for yeast alanine tRNA (Nobel Prize 1968): an acceptor stem ending in CCA, where the amino acid attaches, a D arm, an anticodon arm and a T arm. The cloverleaf, however, is only the secondary structure. X-ray crystallography of yeast phenylalanine tRNA in 1973 to 1974, by the groups of Alexander Rich and Sung-Hou Kim and of Aaron Klug, revealed the tertiary structure: the arms stack on one another to form two limbs at right angles, an L. The acceptor end sits at the tip of one limb and the anticodon loop at the tip of the other, so the amino acid and the codon-reading part are held far apart, which suits their separate jobs inside the ribosome. Example: when Meera revises translation, she should draw the cloverleaf to show which bases pair, but remember that the molecule in the cell is the compact inverted L. Option c is right because inverted L is the standard name for tRNA's actual, folded shape. Exam trap: the cloverleaf is the most familiar diagram, and the word 'actual' in the stem is there to push you past it to the three-dimensional form.", "traps": {"a": "Inverted U describes nothing in tRNA biology; a single hairpin (stem-loop) looks a little like a U, but tRNA has four arms folded into an L.", "b": "No standard text describes tRNA as S shaped; the two pictures you need are the flat cloverleaf (secondary structure) and the folded L (tertiary structure).", "d": "T shaped tempts because one arm is called the T arm (the TΨC loop, named after its bases), but that name refers to nucleotides, not to the shape of the molecule."}}, {"q": "In a typical monohybrid cross, the phenotypic ratio is _____ and the genotypic ratio is _____.", "concept": "The answer is a: in a typical monohybrid cross the F2 phenotypic ratio is 3:1 and the genotypic ratio is 1:2:1. A monohybrid cross follows one gene with two alleles. Gregor Mendel (1866) crossed pure-breeding tall pea plants (TT) with pure-breeding dwarf plants (tt). Every F1 plant was Tt and tall, because T is dominant. When the F1 plants self-pollinated (Tt x Tt), each parent made T and t gametes in equal numbers (segregation), and a Punnett square gives four equally likely combinations: TT, Tt, tT and tt. Genotypes: 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt. Phenotypes: TT and Tt plants are both tall, so 3 tall : 1 dwarf. Mendel's own counts came very close, for example 787 tall to 277 dwarf plants, about 2.84 to 1. Option a is right because it gives the phenotype ratio (3:1) in the first blank and the genotype ratio (1:2:1) in the second, matching the order in the stem. Exam trap: the two ratios are often printed in swapped order, so check which blank asks for appearance (phenotype) and which for genetic make-up (genotype). Related ratios worth knowing: a test cross (Tt x tt) gives 1:1, a dihybrid F2 gives 9:3:3:1, and with incomplete dominance the phenotype ratio equals the genotype ratio, 1:2:1.", "traps": {"b": "This swaps the two: 1:2:1 is the genotype ratio and 3:1 the phenotype ratio, so the order does not fit the blanks.", "c": "1:3 turns the phenotype ratio upside down (the dominant phenotype is the majority), and 1:1:2 is not a Mendelian genotype ratio.", "d": "3:1 is right for phenotype, but 1:3 is not a genotype ratio; three genotypes (TT, Tt, tt) appear, in the ratio 1:2:1."}}, {"q": "The concept of the synapse was given by:", "concept": "The answer is c: the concept, and the name, of the synapse came from the British physiologist Charles Scott Sherrington (1897). A synapse is the junction where one neuron passes its signal to another cell across a tiny gap. Santiago Ramon y Cajal's neuron doctrine (from 1888) had argued that neurons are separate cells rather than a continuous net, which raised the question of how they communicate. Sherrington, writing the nervous system volume of the seventh edition of Michael Foster's Textbook of Physiology (1897), introduced the word synapse (from the Greek for 'clasping together'); he had first proposed 'syndesm', and the Cambridge classicist Arthur Verrall suggested the better word. Sherrington never saw a synapse. He inferred its properties from spinal reflexes in cats and dogs: a delay at the junction, temporal and spatial summation, one-way conduction and central inhibition, including reciprocal inhibition of opposing muscles. The Integrative Action of the Nervous System (1906) set this out, and he shared the 1932 Nobel Prize with Edgar Adrian. Example: when Kabir's knee jerks to a tap, the tiny delay inside his spinal cord is the kind of evidence Sherrington used. Option c is right because Sherrington is the scientist credited with the synapse concept. Exam trap: Donald Hebb (1949) is famous for the 'Hebbian synapse', so his name sounds right, but he described how synapses strengthen with use, not the synapse itself. Key note: the paper prints 'William Sherrington'; the man is Charles Scott Sherrington, and the option clearly means him, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Donald Hebb, in The Organization of Behavior (1949), proposed that a synapse strengthens when the cells on both sides fire together (the Hebbian synapse, cell assemblies); he built on the synapse concept but did not originate it.", "b": "Milner is either Brenda Milner, known for patient H.M. and memory (with William Scoville, 1957), or Peter Milner, who found brain reward sites with James Olds (1954); neither introduced the synapse.", "d": "All of these fails because only Sherrington introduced the synapse; Hebb and the Milners worked half a century later and built on his idea."}}, {"q": "Which of the following holds true about cardiac pacemaker cells?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three statements are true of cardiac pacemaker cells. These are specialised heart muscle cells, mainly in the sinoatrial (SA) node in the wall of the right atrium, that set the heartbeat by firing on their own (automaticity). A neuron or a ventricular muscle cell sits at a stable resting potential (about -70 mV in a neuron, nearer -90 mV in ventricular muscle) until something stimulates it. Pacemaker cells have no stable rest. After each beat the membrane starts at about -60 mV and drifts slowly upward, the pacemaker potential (phase 4): first through the 'funny current' (If), a mainly sodium inward current through HCN channels that open when the cell is most negative, then through T-type calcium channels. When the drift reaches threshold, about -40 mV, L-type calcium channels open and carry the upstroke. This upstroke is slower than the fast sodium spike of neurons, and there is no plateau as in ventricular muscle; potassium channels then repolarise the cell and the cycle repeats. The autonomic nervous system tunes the slope of the drift: sympathetic noradrenaline steepens it and speeds the heart, vagal acetylcholine flattens it and slows the heart. Example: when Riya runs up the stairs, sympathetic input steepens her pacemaker drift and her heart rate rises. Option d is right because the slow drift is a graded depolarisation (a), the calcium-driven, plateau-free spike departs from the usual action potential (b), and the lack of a fixed resting level is an unstable resting membrane potential (c). Exam trap: students learn one textbook action potential (a fast sodium upstroke from a fixed -70 mV) and assume every excitable cell follows it; pacemaker cells are the standard exception.", "traps": {"a": "Graded depolarisation is true (the slow pacemaker drift towards threshold), but it is only one of three true features, so choosing it alone is incomplete.", "b": "True: the pacemaker action potential has a slow calcium upstroke and no plateau, so it departs from the usual pattern, but a and c are also true.", "c": "True: there is no stable resting potential, which is why the cells fire on their own, but a and b are equally true, so a single option is incomplete."}}, {"q": "The neurons that hyperpolarize initially and then rapidly depolarize are called:", "concept": "The answer is a: neurons that are first hyperpolarised and then fire a rapid burst of depolarisation are called rebound neurons, after the behaviour known as post-inhibitory rebound. Normally an inhibitory input (an IPSP) pushes the membrane below rest and makes firing less likely. In some neurons, notably thalamic relay neurons, the hyperpolarisation itself primes a later burst. Two kinds of channel explain it. T-type (low-threshold) calcium channels are inactivated at the normal resting level; a spell of hyperpolarisation removes that inactivation, so when the inhibition ends they open and produce a low-threshold calcium spike crowned by a burst of sodium action potentials. The h-current, carried by HCN channels, is switched on by hyperpolarisation and also pulls the membrane back up. Henrik Jahnsen and Rodolfo Llinas (1984) described these properties in guinea-pig thalamic neurons. Post-inhibitory rebound lets inhibition create rhythm: it contributes to thalamic oscillations such as sleep spindles and to the alternating bursts of the central pattern generators for walking and breathing. Example: think of Arjun pressing a spring down and then letting go; the spring shoots back past its starting point, as the membrane does when the inhibition is lifted. Option a is right because it is the only option that names the firing behaviour in the stem; the other three are kinds of cell defined by shape or by not being neurons. Exam trap: bipolar and unipolar can sound like a two-phase or one-phase response, but they count the processes leaving the cell body and say nothing about firing pattern. Key note: 'rebound neurons' is not a formal cell class like bipolar or unipolar neurons; the behaviour is post-inhibitory rebound, and the other three options are structural classes, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "Glial cells (astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, Schwann cells, microglia) are non-neuronal support cells; they do not fire the action potential burst described in the stem.", "c": "Bipolar neurons are a shape class: one axon and one dendrite leave opposite ends of the cell body, as in the retina and olfactory epithelium; the term says nothing about firing pattern.", "d": "Unipolar (pseudo-unipolar) neurons have one process that splits into two branches, as in dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons; again a shape class, not a firing behaviour."}}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is affected by opiates?", "concept": "The answer is b: opiates act on the endorphin system, the body's own opioid chemicals. Opiates are drugs from the opium poppy and their relatives: morphine, codeine and heroin (wholly synthetic drugs such as fentanyl are usually called opioids). They relieve pain, produce euphoria and slow breathing. In 1973 Candace Pert and Solomon Snyder showed that the brain has specific opiate receptors, which raised the question of why a brain would carry receptors for a plant drug. In 1975 John Hughes and Hans Kosterlitz isolated the first natural ligands, the enkephalins; the endorphins (the name means 'endogenous morphine') and the dynorphins followed. These endogenous opioid peptides act at mu, delta and kappa receptors, and morphine and heroin act mainly at mu receptors by mimicking them. The system damps pain, especially in the periaqueductal grey and the spinal cord, and is released in stress, injury and intense exercise. Example: Kabir, a long-distance runner, feels less pain late in a race partly because his endorphins are acting on the same receptors that morphine would occupy. Option b is right because opiates bind directly to the receptors of the endorphin (opioid) system. Exam trap: dopamine is the famous 'reward' transmitter and opiates do raise dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens, so c tempts, but that effect is indirect. Key note: endorphins are neuropeptides that act as neuromodulators rather than classic small-molecule transmitters, and opiates reach dopamine and GABA only indirectly (they inhibit GABA interneurons in the ventral tegmental area, which frees the dopamine neurons); the system opiates act on directly is the endorphin system, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Serotonin is the target of SSRIs such as fluoxetine and of LSD and other hallucinogens (at 5-HT2A receptors); opiates do not bind serotonin receptors.", "c": "Dopamine is the reward transmitter of the mesolimbic pathway; opiates raise its release only indirectly, by removing GABA inhibition in the ventral tegmental area, so it is a downstream effect, not the system opiates bind.", "d": "GABA is the brain's main inhibitory transmitter, targeted by alcohol, benzodiazepines and barbiturates at the GABA-A receptor; opiates only suppress certain GABA interneurons through opioid receptors."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following is an example of a generalized reinforcer?", "concept": "The answer is c: money is the classic generalised reinforcer. B. F. Skinner (1953), in Science and Human Behavior, separated primary reinforcers, which work without learning because they meet biological needs (food, water, warmth), from conditioned (secondary) reinforcers, which were once neutral but became reinforcing by being paired with primary ones. A conditioned reinforcer paired with many different primary reinforcers becomes a generalised reinforcer: it keeps its power whatever the person happens to lack at the moment, because it can be exchanged for almost anything. Money buys food when you are hungry, a drink when you are thirsty, shelter, entertainment and status, so it rarely loses its effect through satiation. Tokens in a token economy work the same way; Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968) described long-stay psychiatric patients who earned tokens and exchanged them for privileges and goods. Example: Meera's parents give her a small sum for each revision chapter she finishes; because the money can buy books, snacks or a film ticket, it keeps working whether or not she wants any one of them right now. Option c is right because money is the textbook case: one conditioned reinforcer linked to many primary ones. Exam trap: water is the opposite type, a primary reinforcer, and it loses its power once thirst is satisfied. Key note: Skinner also listed attention, approval and affection as generalised reinforcers, so a social smile (approval) and grades (which lead to praise, admission and jobs) can work this way too; money is the textbook example because it exchanges for almost any primary reinforcer, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Grades are conditioned reinforcers tied mainly to approval and achievement; they can act broadly, but they cannot be traded for almost any primary reinforcer as money can, so they are not the standard example.", "b": "A social smile is a social reinforcer (approval); Skinner counted approval among generalised reinforcers, but it is narrower than money, which is the textbook case.", "d": "Water is a primary (unconditioned) reinforcer: it works without learning but only while the person is thirsty, the opposite of a generalised reinforcer."}}, {"q": "The idea that personality can be created by the interaction between a person, his environment and his behaviour is known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: the idea that personality grows out of the mutual influence of the person, the environment and behaviour is Albert Bandura's reciprocal determinism. Albert Bandura (1978), in 'The self system in reciprocal determinism', argued against two one-way views: the radical behaviourism of B. F. Skinner (1953), in which the environment shapes behaviour, and trait views, in which inner dispositions drive behaviour. In his model, later called triadic reciprocal causation in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986), three sets of factors are joined by two-way arrows: person factors (thoughts, expectations, self-efficacy, feelings), behaviour, and the environment (other people, rewards, settings). Each is both cause and effect. People are not simply pushed by their surroundings; their behaviour also selects and changes the environment they then meet. Example: Meera expects to do badly in statistics (person), so she skips practice (behaviour); her teacher, seeing little effort, gives her less attention (environment), which lowers her expectation further. If she joins a study group, the same loop can run the other way. Option c is right because reciprocal determinism is the exact name for this three-way, mutual influence. Exam trap: interactionism also says that person and situation work together, but it is a two-factor model in which behaviour is only the outcome; Bandura's addition is that behaviour is itself a third, active cause.", "traps": {"a": "Circular creation is not a term in personality theory; it borrows the circular feel of the model but names no theory.", "b": "Interactionism comes from the person-situation debate that followed Walter Mischel's (1968) critique of traits, as in Norman Endler and David Magnusson's (1976) interactional psychology: in its usual form, behaviour is the product of person x situation rather than a third cause that changes the other two.", "d": "Combination theory is not an established personality theory; it is a made-up label that echoes the idea of combining factors."}}, {"q": "Which of the following traits is considered to be highly heritable?", "concept": "The answer is c: introversion (the low end of extraversion) is the trait with strong, well-replicated heritability. Heritability is the proportion of variation in a trait, within a given population, that is due to genetic differences between people; it says nothing about how genetic one person's trait is. Behaviour geneticists estimate it by comparing identical (monozygotic) twins, who share all their genes, with fraternal (dizygotic) twins, who share about half, and by adoption studies. In the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart, run by Thomas Bouchard from 1979, identical twins raised in different homes were about as alike in personality as those raised together; Auke Tellegen and colleagues (1988) found that roughly half of the variation in most personality traits was genetic. Across twin studies, extraversion-introversion is one of the most consistently heritable dimensions. Hans Eysenck (1967) tied introversion to a chronically higher level of cortical arousal, and Jerome Kagan's studies of behaviourally inhibited infants in the 1980s showed that shy, cautious temperament appears very early. Example: Riya and her identical twin, adopted into different families in Pune and Chennai, both turn out quiet, prefer small gatherings and need time alone, while their adoptive siblings differ. Option c is right because introversion-extraversion is a broad temperament dimension with a strong biological basis. Exam trap: honesty sounds like a fixed part of character, but Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928) found that children's honesty changed from one situation to the next.", "traps": {"a": "Honesty is a moral character trait shaped mainly by upbringing, values and the situation; Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May's (1928) Character Education Inquiry found children honest in one setting and dishonest in another.", "b": "Cleanliness is a learned habit and cultural value, built through training and social norms rather than a heritable temperament dimension.", "d": "Steadfastness is an everyday virtue word, not a standard personality dimension studied in twin research; the textbook example of a highly heritable trait is introversion-extraversion."}}, {"q": "All schizophrenics exhibit:", "concept": "The answer is c: distorted (disordered) thinking is the feature treated as present in all people with schizophrenia. Emil Kraepelin (1893, 1899) called the illness dementia praecox, stressing early onset and decline. Eugen Bleuler (1911) renamed it schizophrenia ('split mind', meaning a splitting of mental functions, not multiple personalities) and divided its symptoms into fundamental symptoms, present in every case, and accessory symptoms, such as delusions and hallucinations, which come and go. His fundamental symptoms are the four As: loosened associations, flat or incongruous affect, ambivalence and autism (withdrawal into an inner world). For Bleuler the loosening of associations, a disturbance of thinking, was the central one. Modern criteria differ: DSM-5 needs two or more of five symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, negative symptoms) for a significant part of one month, at least one being delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech, with signs lasting six months. Example: Arjun hears voices and has grown withdrawn and flat but holds no delusions; he still meets DSM-5 criteria, and a careful interview often shows loose, tangential thinking, Bleuler's core sign. Option c is right because, in the classical view the stem reflects, disturbed thinking is the core of the illness and the only listed option close to universal. Exam trap: delusions are the best-known symptom, but they are accessory in Bleuler's scheme and not required by DSM-5. Key note: modern criteria make no symptom universal, but in Bleuler's sense distorted thinking is the core feature of the illness, and it is the only option that comes close to 'all', so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Sad mood is the core of depressive disorders; depression often co-occurs with schizophrenia, but many patients do not have it, and prominent mood episodes point instead to schizoaffective disorder or a mood disorder with psychotic features.", "b": "Anhedonia is a negative symptom (loss of pleasure) found in many but not all patients, and it also occurs in depression, so it is neither universal nor specific.", "d": "Delusions are common but not required: DSM-5 can be met by hallucinations plus negative symptoms alone, and Eugen Bleuler counted delusions among the accessory symptoms."}}, {"q": "The relationship between Parkinsonism and schizophrenia is:", "concept": "The answer is d: schizophrenia is linked with too much dopamine activity and Parkinsonism with too little, so the two sit at opposite ends of the same transmitter. Dopamine neurons form several pathways. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra to the striatum and helps control movement; the mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and serves reward and salience. In Parkinson's disease the dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra die; Oleh Hornykiewicz (1960) found the striatum of patients almost empty of dopamine, which led to treatment with L-dopa, the precursor that crosses the blood-brain barrier. The result of the loss is resting tremor, rigidity, slowness of movement (bradykinesia) and a shuffling gait. The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia grew from drug evidence. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that chlorpromazine and haloperidol work by blocking monoamine receptors, dopamine among them; Philip Seeman and colleagues (1976) showed that clinical antipsychotic doses track how strongly the drugs bind dopamine receptors (the type now called D2); and amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can produce a paranoid psychosis. Excess mesolimbic activity is thought to drive the positive symptoms. The clinical see-saw shows the link: Kabir, given a D2-blocking antipsychotic for his voices, develops a tremor and a stiff, slow walk (drug-induced parkinsonism), while a Parkinson's patient on too much L-dopa may begin to hallucinate. Option d is right because it states this opposite-direction relationship. Exam trap: c sounds plausible because amphetamine does cause psychosis, but it does not cause Parkinsonism, which comes from the death of nigral dopamine neurons.", "traps": {"a": "Too much dopamine fits the positive symptoms of schizophrenia but not Parkinsonism, which is a nigrostriatal dopamine deficiency treated by adding dopamine (L-dopa).", "b": "Too little dopamine fits Parkinsonism, but the positive symptoms of schizophrenia are tied to excess dopamine activity and are treated by blocking D2 receptors.", "c": "Amphetamine releases dopamine and can cause a paranoid psychosis that mimics schizophrenia, but Parkinson's disease is caused by the loss of dopamine neurons in the substantia nigra, not by stimulant use."}}, {"q": "Tardive dyskinesia is:", "concept": "The answer is d: tardive dyskinesia (TD) is a disorder of involuntary, repetitive movements, mainly of the face and mouth and sometimes of the limbs and trunk. 'Tardive' means late and 'dyskinesia' abnormal movement: it appears after months or years of treatment with dopamine-blocking drugs, especially first-generation antipsychotics such as haloperidol and chlorpromazine, and sometimes the anti-sickness drug metoclopramide. Typical signs are lip smacking, chewing, puckering, tongue thrusting, grimacing and rapid blinking, along with writhing of the fingers or rocking of the trunk. The leading explanation is that long blockade of D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway makes them supersensitive, so the movement circuits overreact. TD may be irreversible, so prevention matters: the lowest effective dose and regular screening with the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS). In 2017 two VMAT2 inhibitors, valbenazine and deutetrabenazine, were approved in the United States to treat it. TD is the last of the extrapyramidal side effects to appear: acute dystonia within hours to days, akathisia and drug-induced parkinsonism within days to weeks, tardive dyskinesia after months to years. Example: Meera, on haloperidol for ten years, keeps making chewing movements and pushing her tongue into her cheek even when she is not eating. Option d is right because TD is defined by repetitive, involuntary movements of the face and body. Exam trap: psychomotor agitation sounds like 'too much movement', but restless pacing soon after starting an antipsychotic is akathisia, an early side effect, not TD.", "traps": {"a": "Memory loss is a cognitive symptom (amnesia, dementia, or an effect of ECT or anticholinergic drugs), not a movement disorder.", "b": "Loss of sensation (numbness or anaesthesia) is a sensory problem from nerve or spinal damage, or a conversion symptom; TD affects movement, not feeling.", "c": "Psychomotor agitation is restless overactivity seen in mania, agitated depression or akathisia (an early antipsychotic side effect of inner restlessness); TD is involuntary, stereotyped movement that appears late."}}, {"q": "Which one of the following is called the hunger centre?", "concept": "The answer is a: the lateral hypothalamus (LH) was called the hunger, or feeding, centre. The hypothalamus, a small structure below the thalamus, regulates eating, drinking, temperature and hormone release. Classic lesion studies in rats produced a dual-centre model of hunger. In 1940 Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson found that lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) made rats overeat (hyperphagia) and grow obese, so the VMH looked like a satiety, or stop, centre. In 1951 the Indian physiologist Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck found that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus made rats stop eating (aphagia) and drinking (adipsia), even to the point of starving, while electrical stimulation of the LH could make a well-fed animal eat. Eliot Stellar (1954) combined these findings into the dual-centre theory: the LH switches eating on, the VMH switches it off. Example: in the dual-centre picture, when Kabir has skipped lunch the LH 'start' signal dominates and he feels hungry; after a large thali the VMH 'stop' signal takes over and he feels full. Option a is right because the LH is the site the dual-centre model names as the hunger centre. Exam trap: VMH is the best-known hypothalamic eating abbreviation, so students pick it, but it is the satiety centre, the opposite role. The modern view is more complex: the arcuate and paraventricular nuclei, hormones such as leptin and ghrelin, and peptides such as neuropeptide Y and orexin all take part, and some VMH lesion effects came from damage to nearby fibre tracts and raised insulin.", "traps": {"b": "The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) is the satiety centre: lesions there (Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson, 1940) caused overeating and obesity, the opposite of a hunger centre.", "c": "VSM is not a recognised hypothalamic nucleus or eating centre; it is a jumble of letters made to look like VMH.", "d": "MH (medial hypothalamus) is not a standard label for a hunger centre; the medial region containing the VMH is linked with satiety, not hunger."}}, {"q": "In DID, the host identity refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: in dissociative identity disorder (DID) the host is the dominant identity, the one in charge of the body most of the time. DID, called multiple personality disorder until DSM-IV (1994), requires two or more distinct personality states, or an experience of possession, that disrupt the sense of self, with recurrent gaps in memory for everyday events, personal information or trauma. The identities are of two kinds. The host is the identity that holds executive control most often, handles daily life, usually carries the person's legal name and is the one who comes for treatment; it is often unaware of the others. DSM-IV-TR described this primary identity as often passive, dependent, guilty and depressed. The alters are the other identities: child alters, protectors, persecutors and helpers, which may differ in age, gender, voice and handwriting. Changing from one to another is a switch. Most patients report severe childhood abuse or neglect. Example: Meera comes to the clinic as Meera, a quiet schoolteacher puzzled by clothes she does not remember buying; later an angry alter calling herself Rani appears in a session. Meera, who runs her life most days, is the host. Option b is right because 'most dominant' captures the host's defining feature, executive control most of the time. Exam trap: the host is often assumed to be the original person, but it is frequently not the identity that existed before the disorder began. Key note: c describes the original (birth) identity, which may be dormant and is not necessarily the host; the host is the one in charge most of the time, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "The first identity to emerge as a new identity is an alter that has split off, not the host; alters appear later, often around trauma.", "c": "The identity that existed before the onset is the original or birth personality; it may be dormant or rarely out, so it is not necessarily the host.", "d": "Being the most erratic defines no role; persecutor or child alters may be the erratic ones, while the host is often the steady, if depressed, everyday self."}}, {"q": "Chronic Fatigue Syndrome includes:", "concept": "The answer is d: chronic fatigue syndrome (CFS) can include extreme fatigue, muscle pain and chest pain. CFS, also called myalgic encephalomyelitis (ME/CFS), is a long-lasting illness of profound, unexplained exhaustion. The research definition most often taught is that of Keiji Fukuda and colleagues for the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (1994): at least six months of persistent or relapsing fatigue that is new, not the result of ongoing exertion, not substantially relieved by rest and that greatly reduces activity, plus four or more of eight symptoms: impaired memory or concentration, sore throat, tender lymph nodes, muscle pain, pain in several joints without swelling or redness, new headaches, unrefreshing sleep and post-exertional malaise lasting more than 24 hours. Other medical causes, such as an underactive thyroid or anaemia, must be ruled out. In 2015 the Institute of Medicine proposed the name systemic exertion intolerance disease, stressing that effort makes the symptoms worse. Its historical relative is neurasthenia, named by George Miller Beard (1869) for unexplained nervous exhaustion. Example: Riya, a software engineer, has been exhausted for eight months since a viral fever; a short walk leaves her in bed for two days, her muscles ache and her sleep never refreshes her. Option d is right because fatigue is the defining symptom, muscle pain is one of the listed criteria, and chest pain is among the other symptoms patients report. Exam trap: the name points only to fatigue, so a looks sufficient, but the syndrome is a cluster of symptoms. Key note: chest pain is not one of the core criteria; it is among the other symptoms some patients report, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Extreme fatigue is the defining feature, but picking it alone ignores the other symptoms the syndrome includes, such as muscle pain.", "b": "Muscle pain (myalgia) is one of the eight Fukuda criterion symptoms, but it is only one part of the picture, not the whole syndrome.", "c": "Chest pain is reported by some patients but is not a core criterion, so on its own it is the weakest description; it belongs here only as part of the full cluster."}}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies utilizes body language the most?", "concept": "The answer is d: Gestalt therapy makes the most use of body language. Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970), with his wife Laura Perls, built a therapy around awareness in the here and now; its founding book, Gestalt Therapy: Excitement and Growth in the Human Personality (1951), was written by Fritz Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman. Its idea is that people avoid feelings and carry 'unfinished business', and that what they avoid shows in the body before it is put into words. So the therapist watches posture, gestures, breathing, tone of voice and facial expression, and draws the client's attention to them: 'What is your hand doing right now?' 'Be your hand; give it a voice.' Exaggeration exercises ask the client to repeat and enlarge a small movement, such as tapping a foot, until its meaning becomes clear. Other techniques include the empty chair (speaking to an absent person or a part of oneself), the top dog versus underdog dialogue, and staying with a feeling instead of explaining it. 'Why' questions are discouraged; 'what' and 'how' questions keep the client in present experience. Example: Kabir says calmly that he is not angry with his father, but his jaw is tight and his fist clenched; the Gestalt therapist asks him to notice the fist and speak as it. Option d is right because attending to and working with nonverbal, bodily expression is a central Gestalt method. Exam trap: Rogerian therapy is also present-centred and warm, but it works mainly through reflecting the client's words and feelings, not through the body.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) relies on free association with the patient lying on the couch facing away, so the analyst works with words, dreams and transference rather than watching the body.", "b": "Rogerian (person-centred) therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) works through empathy, unconditional positive regard and reflection of what the client says; it notices feelings but does not make bodily expression its main tool.", "c": "Group therapy is a format, not a school; it can be psychodynamic, cognitive-behavioural or interpersonal (Irvin Yalom, 1970), so it has no fixed emphasis on body language, though psychodrama (Jacob Moreno) does use action."}}, {"q": "Hilde Bruch proposed a theory regarding:", "concept": "The answer is c: Hilde Bruch proposed a theory of eating disorders, above all anorexia nervosa. Hilde Bruch (1962), a German-born American psychiatrist (1904 to 1984) who worked for decades with obese and anorexic young people in New York and Houston, described three core disturbances in anorexia nervosa: a distorted body image (the emaciated girl does not see herself as thin), a failure to read inner bodily signals such as hunger, fullness and emotion (interoceptive confusion), and an all-pervasive, paralysing sense of ineffectiveness. Her books Eating Disorders: Obesity, Anorexia Nervosa, and the Person Within (1973) and The Golden Cage (1978) traced these to early family life: a compliant, high-achieving child whose needs were met according to what her parents decided she needed rather than her own cues, so she never learned to trust her own feelings. In adolescence, rigid control of food and weight becomes the one area where she feels truly in charge. Bruch's treatment helped patients discover their own feelings and abilities rather than interpreting hidden conflicts for them. Example: Meera, a top student who has always done what her parents wanted, starts counting every calorie at fifteen; for Bruch the dieting is her bid for autonomy and control. Option c is right because Bruch's theory concerns anorexia nervosa and related eating disorders. Exam trap: anorexia was once called anorexie hystérique, so hysteria tempts, but Bruch's account is a developmental theory of control and selfhood, not a theory of hysteria.", "traps": {"a": "Convulsive disorders (epilepsy) belong to neurology; Bruch did not theorise about seizures.", "b": "OCD theories come from Sigmund Freud's anal-stage account and, later, cognitive models by Stanley Rachman and Paul Salkovskis, not from Bruch.", "d": "Hysteria theory is the work of Jean-Martin Charcot, Pierre Janet, and Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895); Bruch wrote on eating disorders."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Symptoms are caused and maintained by interpersonal problems. Reason (R): Interpersonal therapy is a psychodynamic therapy that focuses on current relationships.", "concept": "The answer is b: both A and R are true, but R does not explain A. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) was set out by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984) as a brief treatment, usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions. Assertion A is true as IPT's working premise: symptoms arise and are kept going in an interpersonal context, so the therapist links the depression to one of four problem areas (grief, role disputes, role transitions, interpersonal deficits) and works on that problem. Reason R is also true as a description: IPT grew from the interpersonal school of Adolf Meyer and Harry Stack Sullivan (1953) and from John Bowlby's attachment theory (1969), so it is often grouped with the brief psychodynamic therapies, and it focuses on current relationships rather than childhood conflict. But R does not explain A. R says what kind of therapy IPT is and where it looks; it gives no reason why symptoms come from interpersonal problems. If anything, A is the rationale for the therapy's focus, not the other way round. Example: Arjun became depressed after moving from Lucknow to Bengaluru for a new job and losing his friends; an IPT therapist treats this as a role transition and helps him build new ties. Option b is right because both statements hold but R is not the reason for A. Exam trap: when R repeats the topic of A, students assume it explains A; ask whether R answers 'why?' for A. Key note: IPT itself is agnostic about the ultimate cause of depression, and some would mark R false because IPT does not interpret the transference or dig into the past; as the statements are commonly read, both stand and R does not explain A, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "R is true but only describes IPT's lineage and focus; it gives no reason why symptoms arise from interpersonal problems, so it cannot be the explanation of A.", "c": "R is not false: IPT does focus on current relationships and is often placed among the brief, psychodynamically derived therapies through Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal school.", "d": "A is not false: it is the central premise of IPT, which treats depression in its interpersonal context."}}, {"q": "Multimodal therapy assesses a person's:", "concept": "The answer is b: multimodal therapy assesses a person's BASIC I.D. Arnold Lazarus (1973), a South African-born clinical psychologist trained in behaviour therapy, argued that lasting change needs work across all the modalities of human functioning, and developed multimodal therapy, later set out in Multimodal Behavior Therapy (1976) and The Practice of Multimodal Therapy (1981). BASIC I.D. is an acronym for seven interacting modalities: Behaviour, Affect, Sensation, Imagery, Cognition, Interpersonal relationships, and Drugs/biology (medication, health, diet, exercise). The therapist builds a modality profile, listing problems and treatments under each heading, and may use 'bridging' (starting in the client's preferred modality before moving to an avoided one) and 'tracking' (noting the firing order of modalities, for example sensation, then cognition, then affect). Lazarus called his stance technical eclecticism: borrow effective techniques from any school while explaining them with one theory, social learning. Example: for Riya's panic attacks the profile might list avoiding the metro (behaviour), terror (affect), palpitations (sensation), images of fainting (imagery), the thought 'I am dying' (cognition), dependence on her brother (interpersonal) and too much coffee (drugs/biology), each with its own intervention. Option b is right because BASIC I.D. is the multimodal assessment framework itself. Exam trap: Arnold Lazarus (multimodal therapy) is often confused with Richard Lazarus, the stress and coping theorist (Stress, Appraisal, and Coping, 1984, with Susan Folkman).", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive functioning is only the C of BASIC I.D., one of seven modalities, so it is too narrow to describe the assessment.", "c": "A biological profile is only the D (drugs and biology) modality; multimodal assessment covers six other areas as well.", "d": "Overall intelligence is measured by IQ tests such as the Wechsler scales; it is not part of the multimodal assessment."}}]});
